r-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186418972} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b170edf-da65-5c4e-b55d-7609f6f82353" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{k}^{3}" :db/id 17592186418820} {:answer/id #uuid "640c02c3-24c8-56fb-9481-8ba2fdbbd711" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3k+1" :db/id 17592186418832} {:answer/id #uuid "7315045e-7a31-5705-b63a-01d7337f2587" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{k}^{2}" :db/id 17592186418843} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3f7b4f-675d-55d7-bb75-72e0c95f5fcb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(k+1)^{3}" :db/id 17592186418904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b170edf-da65-5c4e-b55d-7609f6f82353" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{k}^{3}" :db/id 17592186418820} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0adae27-4bc6-5d43-887d-ab7ae94287b2" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186418982} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bb0c974-1191-5c44-9152-c22c2f9a3f6d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=4" :db/id 17592186418836} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a3c9b3-26b1-5916-9555-6b5bc8d4d6af" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k\\ge 3" :db/id 17592186418906} {:answer/id #uuid "dfdc54ad-cfc6-523c-b5a6-6a3abe8212c6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>4" :db/id 17592186418917} {:answer/id #uuid "f107928f-8d5f-5ec7-81fa-6e98c583ef91" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=3" :db/id 17592186418931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2a3c9b3-26b1-5916-9555-6b5bc8d4d6af" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k\\ge 3" :db/id 17592186418906} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb18aca5-2599-5953-850a-6c238aa579a5" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186418986} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aabb323-bbd9-5f8f-a974-350a80be3297" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{k}^{3}+3" :db/id 17592186418781} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd4e36f-947f-5209-8e77-539944777958" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3k+1)^{3}" :db/id 17592186418821} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2e9a0a-f81c-57f1-91f7-1dd03416a55c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{k}^{3}" :db/id 17592186418847} {:answer/id #uuid "d83d1135-c44b-5df2-a150-79e8ab3c47f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(k-1)^{3}" :db/id 17592186418910} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ef61b9-43b4-528f-8121-a4e5ad07341f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(k+1)^{3}" :db/id 17592186418920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6ef61b9-43b4-528f-8121-a4e5ad07341f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(k+1)^{3}" :db/id 17592186418920} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8f64acc-2d69-52fd-8d6b-98d6c837fb6b" :answer-field/key "field-13" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186418987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e55b1bb6-4fdf-5aaa-8b3c-9ba7f8dbcdd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-183786" :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that $6(n+1)<{n}^{3}$ for $n\\ge 3,n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=3.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}<{{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(k+2) & ={{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(k+1)+6 & <{{field-10}} \\\\  & ={{field-11}} \\\\  & <{{field-12}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ={{field-13}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $6(n+1)<{n}^{3}$ is true for all\n{{field-14}}." :question/worked-solution "A variant of the principle of induction states the following: Let ${n}_{0}$ be an integer. Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P({n}_{0}),P({n}_{0}+1),P({n}_{0}+2),…,$ where\n\n- $P({n}_{0})$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every integer $k\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every integer $n\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n6(n+1)<{n}^{3}\\,\\text{for}\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(3).$ To do this, we substitute $n=3$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is less than the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=3.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(3+1)=24,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3}=27,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=3.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 3.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 3,$ we have\n\n$$\n6(k+1)<{k}^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n6((k+1)+1)=6k+2<(k+1)^{3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is less than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k+2 & =6(k+1)+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{6(k+1)}_{⏟}}_{<{k}^{3}}+6 & <{k}^{3}+6 \\\\  & ={k}^{3}+{{5}_{⏟}}_{<3{k}^{2}+3k}+1 \\\\  & <{k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}+3k+1 \\\\  & =(k+1)^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ is less than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $6(n+1)<{n}^{3}$ for all $n\\ge 3.$"} {:db/id 17592186096577 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186096772 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "467ab5ee-b3b4-514e-9928-b7a9e7444d27" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Number Times an Exponent In Scientific Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "81b782cb-9c2a-5f53-bcce-c88d9537770c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7712" :question/problem "Write $7\\,800 \\times 10^{3}$ in scientific notation." :question/worked-solution "To write a number in scientific notation, we need to express it in the form\n\n$$\na\\times 10^n\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n1 \\leq a < 10\n$$\n\nand $n$ is an integer.\n\nLet's start by writing $7\\,800$ in scientific notation.\n\nTo find $a$, we place the decimal point directly after the first non-zero digit. In this case, we have the following:\n$a = 7.8$\n\nTo find $n$, we take the number $a$ that we just found and count the number of spaces we need to move the decimal point to retrieve the original number.\n\n$$\n7.8 \\rightarrow 7\\,800\n$$\n\nWe can see that the decimal point must move $3$ spaces to the *right*, so $n=3$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7\\,800 = 7.8 \\times 10^{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can express our original number in scientific notation using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7800 \\cdot 10^{3} &= 7.8 \\cdot 10^{3}_(⏟)_(7800) \\cdot 10^{3} \\\\\n&= 7.8 \\cdot 10^{3} \\cdot 10^{3} \\\\\n&= 7.8 \\cdot 10^{3 + 3} \\\\\n&= 7.8 \\cdot 10^{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186100589 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186100815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a673b6-4025-5075-9a8a-120b90bc4f45" :knowledge-point/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Perpendicular Bisector"}] :question/id #uuid "a6999a8e-aa6a-50f1-98db-8715924cd7a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12595" :question/problem "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(3,-2)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(-1,1)$ and $B(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](images/a2/a22e7e19b360c91bcdae8eba8a3230943bd69270eac9dd158652d1648e24de51.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overline{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(3,-2)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+{y}^{2}+4y+4 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6x+4y+13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-1,1)$ and $B(2,0).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^2 = (QB)^2,$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-(-1))^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+{y}^{2}-2y+1 & ={x}^{2}-4x+4+{y}^{2} \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+2x+1+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-2y+1 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-4x+4+\\cancel{{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2x-2y+2 & =-4x+4 \\\\ 6x-2 & =2y \\\\ y & =3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(3x-1)^{2}-6x+4(3x-1)+13 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+9{x}^{2}-6x+1-6x+12x-4+13 \\\\  & =10{x}^{2}+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =20x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve $S'(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 20x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\nS''(0) =20>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=0$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny = 3(0)-1 = -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $\\left(0,-1\\right).$"} {:db/id 17592186100784 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186101020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d2b1036-0ea4-5fd1-bfed-885dcbae618b" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function by Constructing a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/id #uuid "a63d3bdb-ca20-51f5-b876-7ec8950a46dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-7574" :question/problem "A hotel has $21$ rooms. The manager has observed that when the room rate is $\\[math]12$ per night,  all rooms are occupied and, for every $\\[/math]1$ increase in the rate, a single room is vacated. If the maintenance of each rented room is $\\[math]3$ per night, what rate per room should the manager set to obtain the maximum profit, and what is the maximum profit?" :question/worked-solution "First, note that if there are $x$ *vacant* rooms, the hotel has $(21-x)$ occupied rooms, and the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(21-x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{occupied rooms}}\\times \\,\\,\\,{{(12+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{rate per room}} \\\\  & =252+21x-12x-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =252+9x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for having $(21-x)$ rooms rented out, the hotel has a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =3(21-x) \\\\  & =63-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =(252+9x-{x}^{2})-(63-3x) \\\\  & =189+12x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are $21$ rooms, the permitted domain is $0 \\leq x\\leq 21.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 \\lt x\\lt 21.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nP'(x) = 12 - 2x \\,.\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 12-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =12 \\\\ x & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=6$ lies in the interval $0 <  x < 21.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=6$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\nP''(x) = -2 <             0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=6$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=6$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the hotel will have a maximum profit when there are $x=6$ vacant rooms.  \n\nWe know that the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. If there are $6$ vacant rooms, then the rate per room is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12+6 & =\\text{\\[}math]18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the manager should charge a room rate of $\\[/math]18$ per night."} {:db/id 17592186101113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186101020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d2b1036-0ea4-5fd1-bfed-885dcbae618b" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function by Constructing a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7d727ea-08a3-55dd-a017-4d107ec1aa2f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id 17592186098014} {:answer/id #uuid "e882f15d-cbb3-58de-8db6-593afd851087" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id 17592186098015} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9cdd31-c47f-5335-b161-947bd2eae8c6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id 17592186098016} {:answer/id #uuid "51adfce4-2819-5700-8925-9a7b4a6d258d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id 17592186098017} {:answer/id #uuid "5755991d-f45e-58ee-b0da-3896a698d7fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id 17592186098018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a9cdd31-c47f-5335-b161-947bd2eae8c6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id 17592186098016} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c185337-13a1-5e13-a931-af896063703c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186098013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "284fac66-d855-554d-969c-bb28dda1de3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-119091" :question/problem "A summer camp has capacity for $200$ kids. The price for each kid attending the camp is $$300.$ The cost of setting up the camp is made up of a fixed cost of $$2\\,000$ plus a cost of $$100$ per kid. How many kids should attend the summer camp to maximize its profit?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for $x$ kids attending, the summer camp has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=300\\cdot x=300x\n$$\n\nand a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nC(x)={{2\\,000}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}\\,\\,+{{100x.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =300x-(2\\,000+100x) \\\\  & =200x-2\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 200.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=200$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<200.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=200.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ has no solutions.\n\nThus, our candidates are $x=0$ and $x=200.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =200(0)-2\\,000=-2\\,000 \\\\ P(200) & =200(200)-2\\,000=38\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the summer camp will have a maximum profit level when $200$ kids attend."} {:db/id 17592186101196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186101020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d2b1036-0ea4-5fd1-bfed-885dcbae618b" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function by Constructing a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8a7cfdf-560a-5dda-bb00-88b7b7db33a5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "110" :db/id 17592186098512} {:answer/id #uuid "19ba7c3f-bf30-5918-99d4-41a94c11d7c2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id 17592186098513} {:answer/id #uuid "21b956ae-a328-5df2-9861-617d0c217f66" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id 17592186098514} {:answer/id #uuid "29843d37-166e-57c1-a30b-fcf65e24db6e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id 17592186098515} {:answer/id #uuid "7107455b-60e8-57ee-ae33-c39125e56685" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id 17592186098516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21b956ae-a328-5df2-9861-617d0c217f66" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id 17592186098514} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ac3cf8d-6c84-5024-abb1-2a750c1f027c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186098511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be45f13d-84b8-5eb7-9480-670af18ba1fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-15331" :question/problem "A cinema hall can hold $120$ people. The price of each ticket is $$10.$ The cost of maintaining the cinema is made up of a fixed cost of $$400$ plus a cost of $$0.25$ per person. How many tickets should the cinema sell to maximize its profit?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ tickets, the cinema has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=10\\cdot x=10x\n$$\n\nand a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nC(x)={{400}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}\\,\\,+{{0.25x.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =10x-(400+0.25x) \\\\  & =9.75x-400.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 120.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=120$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<120.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=9.75.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ has no solutions.\n\nOur candidates are $x=0$ and $x=120.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =9.75(0)-400=-400 \\\\ P(120) & =9.75(120)-400=770\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the cinema will have a maximum profit level when it sells $120$ tickets."} {:db/id 17592186101537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186100850 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f41b2bd-0c1d-5267-b91d-7aba35ea7efd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0576dca-11ba-5b81-bf79-e77472f267a5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id 17592186100384} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb4a866-f956-5ce2-ad86-8287d8515746" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{2}" :db/id 17592186100385} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa5fda2-4378-571b-b50b-efb7de57160e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id 17592186100386} {:answer/id #uuid "65bcb312-0899-57e5-a9c8-c811b3232950" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id 17592186100387} {:answer/id #uuid "6b551bf2-63ed-5177-96f0-1ce36cc4f2f9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186100388}] :answer-field/id #uuid "73c85ce3-46e4-50e5-bdee-6d8ed6fbddb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186100383} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58bd0173-3936-50ed-8e91-8c7d13b175f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id 17592186377723} {:answer/id #uuid "f154ec8c-3a1a-5eea-8c15-c1e5aa29a8eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186377724} {:answer/id #uuid "4c1151ce-976b-5a90-931b-21dcc3c16111" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{2}" :db/id 17592186377725} {:answer/id #uuid "165d0f78-8482-5b12-b5f3-721f4eb6dd3c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id 17592186377726} {:answer/id #uuid "a538c059-9365-52d6-b129-5e6c9890bfb6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id 17592186377727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c1151ce-976b-5a90-931b-21dcc3c16111" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{2}" :db/id 17592186377725} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd2c180a-696c-5404-bd71-821e2ffd7726" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186377722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc5ca1dd-ed37-5566-960b-b2d315c18f6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-75314" :question/problem "![](images/a5/a55c30803fa9e8351613c39c20133f8fc31045ff46fb4e11b63eeca10f9234c2.png)\n\nConsider the graph of a function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,0]$  and the line segment for $x\\in [0,4].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,0].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4].$ The corresponding region is rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =lw \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186106485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0efa50-9154-5cf3-bf01-e1b6eead489d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e0e2534-557c-5d1d-a4c1-a63a3979c0e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.75" :db/id 17592186105059} {:answer/id #uuid "a5dd965c-513e-5c45-be86-386b4fde7bdc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id 17592186105060} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f8f7a3-d0f4-5000-b70c-55ece98dc00f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id 17592186105061} {:answer/id #uuid "e569548f-e5e4-5a19-a049-83ace359cba6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186105062} {:answer/id #uuid "59352711-4b84-54f5-8f21-16affdcef33e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.25" :db/id 17592186105063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e0e2534-557c-5d1d-a4c1-a63a3979c0e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.75" :db/id 17592186105059} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9bbd5d1-52fc-5532-9c11-a0ca2391cb50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186105058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d9f3e81-c696-5e30-a1a6-32de40a197d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-74324" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=4-2x$ over the interval $[0,1]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.25.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/c4/c478dd4988aaf23348c2453e1f9fca137ddd33284cde7cb7eae1775982bd4b2e.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0.25)+f(0.5)+f(0.75)+f(1)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3.5$ | $3$ | $2.5$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3.5+3+2.5+2)\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =11\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.75\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186106541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbeb43f0-d94d-5ee0-b7fb-fbbe0a804cb7" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Hyperbolic Functions Using the Quotient Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15289765-de82-59df-97ba-0d29417be3c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-2{sech}^{2}⁡2x" :db/id 17592186513586} {:answer/id #uuid "2867b1dd-6971-5434-8f40-5cf09d8f7298" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-2\\operatorname{sech}⁡2x" :db/id 17592186513643} {:answer/id #uuid "396bbd32-45a7-503a-a769-5eda633edbb8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x+2{sech}^{2}⁡2x" :db/id 17592186513682} {:answer/id #uuid "4bcf399a-5aed-50cf-b2a4-349b4f1efa84" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sech}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x" :db/id 17592186513739} {:answer/id #uuid "fd90929f-74bf-5dd1-86c7-150fc2bee7dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sech}^{2}⁡2x-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x" :db/id 17592186514199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15289765-de82-59df-97ba-0d29417be3c4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-2{sech}^{2}⁡2x" :db/id 17592186513586} :answer-field/id #uuid "131bc404-3c34-51d8-a0e1-1f3044bf61e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186105394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0908157-429d-5cf0-a396-e72a48ee6365" :question/math-academy-id "q-95320" :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $w(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x},$ then ${w}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot g(x)}{{tanh}^{2}⁡2x}.$ What is $g(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $w(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{w}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-{e}^{x}\\cdot 2{sech}^{2}⁡2x}{{tanh}^{2}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\overset{g(x)}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-2{sech}^{2}⁡2x)}}}{{tanh}^{2}⁡2x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡2x-2{sech}^{2}⁡2x.$"} {:db/id 17592186110400 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186110771 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a2d7c38-0e96-5f81-81e3-d6acd0215463" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Variable Given an Exterior Angle and Two Internal Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "1642f15d-32c8-5a19-b20b-95f501dc0a97" :question/math-academy-id "e-2711" :question/problem "Find the value of $x$ in the following figure.\n\n![](images/e7/e74d124c971c55844381cf9cedaa0e392e634e5ceda31d6af7a136426678f4db.png)" :question/worked-solution "*Method 1*\n\nLet's start by finding the value of the missing interior angle at $B.$ We use the fact that the interior and exterior angles are supplementary:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{73}^{∘}+m∠B & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={180}^{∘}-{73}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={107}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate $x$ using the fact that the interior angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {41}^{∘}+{107}^{∘}+2x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {148}^{∘}+2x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={32}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={16}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n*Method 2*\n\nWe could rewrite the equation $m\\angle{A} +m\\angle{B} +m\\angle{C} = 180^\\circ$ as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠B}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]}}\\,\\,\\,=m∠A+m∠C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting, we can solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{180}^{∘}-m∠B}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Exterior angle at [math]B[/math]}}\\,\\,\\, & =m∠A+m∠C \\\\ {73}^{∘} & ={41}^{∘}+2x \\\\ {32}^{∘} & =2x \\\\ {16}^{∘} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186114975 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186115395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c68664c1-bc59-51c1-932c-d6f7c8476470" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Vector Function Using Properties of Continuous Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "e08c55e9-d21c-5a33-890c-509847fbbbe8" :question/math-academy-id "e-2484" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf g(1)$ given that $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $\\mathbf f(1) = \\left\\langle 1, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle$ and \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{t\\rightarrow 1} \\big[ 2\\mathbf f(t)-\\mathbf g(t) \\big] = \\left\\langle 2, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)-g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}2f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 1}[-g(t)] \\\\  & =2{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)-{lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =2f(1)-g(1) \\\\  & =2{{⟨1,1,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{f(1)}-g(1) \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-2⟩-g(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{t\\rightarrow 1} \\big[ 2\\mathbf f(t)-\\mathbf g(t) \\big] = \\left\\langle 2, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $\\mathbf{g} (1)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)-g(t)] & =⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,2,-2⟩-g(1) & =⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =⟨2,2,-2⟩-⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186115804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c68664c1-bc59-51c1-932c-d6f7c8476470" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Vector Function Using Properties of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54aee2c5-cc51-57ec-9a8a-f399690213ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2},1⟩" :db/id 17592186563138} {:answer/id #uuid "63cd7361-80a7-5610-b411-db6d96b03f98" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,6,-2⟩" :db/id 17592186563175} {:answer/id #uuid "8b1fb0b6-ee0d-51a1-80d9-cbcb788fb076" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,4,2⟩" :db/id 17592186563257} {:answer/id #uuid "b02a670b-dc6a-56a6-bfc7-88e12bb41e1c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,3,-1⟩" :db/id 17592186563338} {:answer/id #uuid "db46a840-c91e-50d2-8618-cbba1038d021" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,8,4⟩" :db/id 17592186563427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b02a670b-dc6a-56a6-bfc7-88e12bb41e1c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,3,-1⟩" :db/id 17592186563338} :answer-field/id #uuid "42daf895-ed79-526d-aef4-19952bd37e0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186113761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a9c34c8-3d9e-57aa-9737-7f2bbf9076b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-35104" :question/problem "Find $g(2)$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $f(2)=⟨1,1,2⟩$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)]=⟨-1,7,0⟩\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple, and the addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 2}2g(t) \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)+2{lim}_{t\\to 2}g(t) \\\\  & =f(2)+2g(2) \\\\  & ={{⟨1,1,2⟩}_{⏟}}_{f(2)}+2g(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)]=⟨-1,7,0⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $g(2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)+2g(t)] & =⟨-1,7,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,2⟩+2g(2) & =⟨-1,7,0⟩ \\\\ 2g(2) & =⟨-1,7,0⟩-⟨1,1,2⟩ \\\\ 2g(2) & =⟨-2,6,-2⟩ \\\\ g(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ⟨-2,6,-2⟩ \\\\ g(2) & =⟨-1,3,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186119594 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/id #uuid "d5d020cb-50ee-53f6-9d8b-d384bc9b7999" :question/math-academy-id "e-4433" :question/problem "Given that \n![](images/58/589324d1b345360020e9c2b1096b64a08008a52b05a13fdc7fd39bcb063a8d58.svg)\nfind \n![](images/f4/f4925a93ca952863cac520a853c1bc5d7c815dc8324043d6c0963d20d2b2b1ff.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+0 & b+0 & c+1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{-10}+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10+1(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-10-1 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186119603 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "0e45ac96-0eee-51a3-83e0-192582dcf57d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4765" :question/problem "Given ![](images/e3/e34daf05ba492435f014fedd8336dd4bb4c65ae9c1688947d7873b8fba3b3e22.svg) find its LU decomposition." :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186119604 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186119793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "e1d1f80c-70d1-566d-9cc0-94899b0fa05d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4767" :question/problem "Given \n![](images/d5/d5f838110aeb104064744379d4879fb237a2ad9ad7b4728f4216d9f1bd39cd8a.svg), find its LU decomposition." :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186119605 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186119794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4d10f94-98d4-53d8-a35c-e65a06dd2c55" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using a Single Elementary Row Operation"}] :question/id #uuid "f7324b66-d8f7-5bdb-9786-4160808f2a7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4768" :question/problem "Given that\n![](images/ae/aea6bc2396725e1f24bbd196f15c590fbd9628dd15f220bc57653bae25840773.svg) \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186119606 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186119795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c91fb2b-5607-555d-b897-f0e76765dfa6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lower Triangular Matrix Given the Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "58955d86-8a94-5174-83fa-072314037298" :question/math-academy-id "e-4769" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $F$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+R_1, \\qquad\n\tR_3:=R_3+2R_1\n$$\n\nGiven that $F$ has the LU factorization $F=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying $R_2:=R_2 + {\\color{red}1} \\cdot R_1$ and then $R_3:=R_3+ {\\color{red}2} \\cdot R_1$ is equivalent to multiplying $F$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n![](images/a9/a99ebe6a4021d9124320439383f1e5351f134a321a353c2dafaa7bbf8555aae7.svg)\n\nNow, since $E_2E_1F=U,$ we can invert $E_2$ and $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}F & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}F & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ F & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ F & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ F & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ F & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186119607 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186119796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "50685ac4-9980-5841-a669-783f6086a9dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-4770" :question/problem "Given that\n![](images/c2/c21c2b99cdd8480ab891a23f92687d29230da7da9de9bd75a903919e54c48a1f.svg)\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{4}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_2E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_2$ and $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186119610 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186119799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7634b3ac-f9e6-514e-87ba-e78590c9fa53" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 Linear System Using LU Factorization"}] :question/id #uuid "9552ad30-9d46-5884-9877-18ca8842c252" :question/math-academy-id "e-4775" :question/problem "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b},$ given by\n![](images/f1/f1124e83e3b7614557a1a6de54523cc99f62ba2a52af1da8e4d22e14466ce020.svg)\nIf $\\langle x_1,x_2 \\rangle$ and $\\langle y_1,y_2 \\rangle$ are the solutions to the systems $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ and $L\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{b}$ respectively, what is the value of $x_1 \\cdot y_2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/a1/a185a6fd91c78ed3e291b3ac8b99f9430290e5d8c1060a7d97317efc230aeb40.svg)\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n![](images/1e/1e780d384ca1fd957d742bbd047399c558ab016d187ce4cf71682546673aba35.svg)\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=6 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-8.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $y_1=6$ and $y_2=4.$ Therefore, \n![](images/58/58da62903247cf6dfdf33be86293c181ef5184048ca7cd6c921360e5d202f353.svg)\n\n- Next, we solve $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n![](images/e0/e0563dde4944ecbe2ccb98bc91246fa18c7a808df96ab77f9f8efb5510084677.svg)\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=6 \\\\ -2{x}_{2} &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $x_2=-2$ and $x_1=2.$ Therefore, \n![](images/a8/a84088cff2a3589652ef7cabf09e3d8429fad7797ea20d44a416f85dcc7f9b65.svg)\n\nFinally, $x_1 \\cdot y_2 = 2 \\cdot 4 = 8.$"} {:db/id 17592186119611 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186119800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa9fbb8-b2bc-5c93-bb07-bc8c7ef7c115" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution of an Intermediate System Using the LU Factorization of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "24bc4954-462b-5066-83d4-8a4a87efc7b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4777" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $\\mathbf b,$ given by \n![](images/a1/a13a7752a224bdabc27558a6a41e2b24d3c7918af1ca4d9cff802dcb54259238.svg)\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $\\mathbf{y}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/60/605f569e0b4a54d2ca68d7b3330a2a0ebb06dcaba2b7efa911f58dbffd796fb0.svg)\n\nWe now solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n![](images/d2/d259a6740aa32e0e577756375fd85589b815232314511c74dc434a88d0146a3b.svg)\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=2 \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=4 \\\\ {y}_{1}-2{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =2, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =4-{y}_{1} \\\\  & =4-(2) \\\\  & =2, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =1-{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2} \\\\  & =1-(2)+2(2) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](images/c5/c5198aa83d3976d992b200f63941261f952282fedb241db780c20c12c97e3ba9.svg)"} {:db/id 17592186119631 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186119820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4f1675-9146-5279-9b64-07a63a9bed99" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Given Its Coordinates Relative To a Given Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "0a0ea798-5125-5d0e-a755-96bf9af5f543" :question/math-academy-id "e-4823" :question/problem "Find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ if $\\,\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\},$ and\n![](images/79/798c2f477907d0d3414eeed0a2f37871c69466d9b4a098699a364f6a034c0679.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We know that any vector in  $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,\\mathcal{B}.$ Since we are told that ![](images/de/de91d9b4cd4eabed0a27696d7bf0512c19f6c66eb3c81ab6dc6a73fc0a1c15a5.svg) we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={{(-1)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{{(\\,3\\,)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186119706 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186119886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5250e0e1-4909-5075-8a8e-aa2f1be0bd9a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution of an Intermediate System Using the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "cbefba9a-eda2-5a8e-8bb5-59ea4175b835" :question/math-academy-id "e-8926" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $\\mathbf b,$ given by \n\n![](images/9d/9d0b186213211813b290f502888f68e0603bc9f77bcd7ea9d6262cb7449c00e7.svg)\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $\\mathbf{y}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/42/4298fe244fd6c66c7623ad508c97b96464321387506aa1e4be5e2399a5c9a862.svg)\n\nWe now solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 66\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=5 \\\\ 15{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=66.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $y_1=5$ and $y_2=-9.$ \n\nTherefore, ![](images/a8/a86dfd0e07b8c415e526764cf9f7ba8f020609bf7b13cc90536deba868ec4aa6.svg)"} {:db/id 17592186120080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7634b3ac-f9e6-514e-87ba-e78590c9fa53" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 Linear System Using LU Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b502cec-1775-59f9-809f-3acfaa819cb3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186238705} {:answer/id #uuid "56dfee9c-b30c-5648-9828-3201f728cb12" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186238805} {:answer/id #uuid "8a110af8-df14-5052-b118-eca598e4d15c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186239259} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d9476f-d8c5-5847-bd4a-30cf5e082d35" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id 17592186239672} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c5a8f7-548e-56d7-a1c2-fc6e1e17cc92" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186239955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a110af8-df14-5052-b118-eca598e4d15c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186239259} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4fe466f-bde9-5495-bd5f-97f097a5d1a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc6bf17a-cfc4-5f52-984e-78af80897af9" :question/math-academy-id "q-105949" :question/problem "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=-1 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-1$ and ${y}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=y=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=2$ and ${x}_{1}=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{1}=(-1)\\cdot (-1)=1.$"} {:db/id 17592186120182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0c76f10-16e5-5123-ada9-c46ffc7d80fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id 17592186117254} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc9837c-7702-5746-bde7-5a45cd35ac22" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186117255} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1b278a-4688-5c9f-be88-8efc4ef7dcca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id 17592186117256} {:answer/id #uuid "368bae87-266a-53a8-9012-a8bbe2a28dd7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id 17592186117257} {:answer/id #uuid "67555b5d-1335-5b3d-b929-cd5b7d5dc573" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id 17592186117258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0c76f10-16e5-5123-ada9-c46ffc7d80fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id 17592186117254} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0c8d38b-40e5-574f-8375-b3ab7592221a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186117253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b6bfb23-75e1-5b07-8a9e-de9285790a99" :question/math-academy-id "q-32529" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=3,{u}_{22}=-1,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=-3.$"} {:db/id 17592186120183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd1da0c-9706-54e7-a5f2-f3d28e865dbb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186300194} {:answer/id #uuid "412e17c7-0020-53f1-a0ef-539e1365d3d9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186300281} {:answer/id #uuid "4b04aaca-0605-5891-9aea-47020aa5f0dd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186300348} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7cfc9b-c118-57b5-980b-fd9d456c0e75" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186300566} {:answer/id #uuid "a9cb8b03-0892-5abc-894c-fd8f3ea0915c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186300812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a7cfc9b-c118-57b5-980b-fd9d456c0e75" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186300566} :answer-field/id #uuid "91857d77-adc5-5d07-b88e-f2dabe47806e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186301690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36e66381-e67c-58c9-901d-001e6d2f3331" :question/math-academy-id "q-32532" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186120185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7634b3ac-f9e6-514e-87ba-e78590c9fa53" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 Linear System Using LU Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3f3ee05-d3f7-5c86-895e-6f43ced8c9d8" :answer/type {:db/id 1759