eld/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186462864} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b92dd1c2-7470-51b7-84bd-43a97469e0ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186462223} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8c081b-8a77-5970-9a81-d525e92f384c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186462224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d8c081b-8a77-5970-9a81-d525e92f384c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186462224} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa0501cc-b8f8-5bbe-b3f3-b801954fc967" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186462865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "587a7a92-f47b-5043-840d-bba7363fe179" :question/math-academy-id "q-183495" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $6\\,|\\,x$ $\\,$ $Q(x):$ $3\\,|\\,x$\n\nConsider the predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ above. Which of the following expresses the conditional predicate $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$\n- **I.** $6\\,|\\,x$ is sufficient for $3\\,|\\,x$\n- **II.** For $3\\,|\\,x,$ it is sufficient that $6\\,|\\,x$\n- **III.** $6\\,|\\,x$ is necessary for $3\\,|\\,x$\n- **IV.** For $6\\,|\\,x,$ it is necessary that $3\\,|\\,x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe have the following grammatical constructions for $S⇒N:$\n\n- $S$ is sufficient for $N$\n\n- For $N,$ it is sufficient that $S$\n\n- $N$ is necessary for $S$\n\n- For $S,$ it is necessary that $N$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I reads\n\n$$\n{{6\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is sufficient for}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option II reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x,}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,\\text{it is sufficient that}\\,{{6\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Option III reads\n\n$$\n{{6\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{is necessary for}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $Q(x)⇒P(x).$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- Option IV reads\n\n$$\n\\text{for}\\,{{6\\,|\\,x,}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,\\text{it is necessary that}\\,{{3\\,|\\,x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to $P(x)⇒Q(x).$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and IV only.\""} {:db/id 17592186203675 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186203938 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "606373a5-e088-5cb1-9f57-cf6982e5aebe" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Irrationality of Sums and Differences"}] :question/id #uuid "bc4078d4-571e-535d-bad0-044af3876063" :question/math-academy-id "e-13358" :question/problem "Prove by contradiction that $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational. You may assume without proof that $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational." :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\nP{:} \\quad 3-\\sqrt{2} \\: \\textrm{is irrational}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P{:} \\quad 3-\\sqrt{2} \\: \\textrm{is rational}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is rational.\n\nSince we're assuming $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is rational, we can express it as the ratio of two non-zero integers:\n\n> Then $3-\\sqrt{2} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to rearrange the equation, leaving a root on one side and a fraction on the other.\n\n> So, we have\n> $3 - \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{a}{b}; - \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{a}{b} - 3; \\sqrt{2}|= 3 - \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{3b}{b} - \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{3b - a}{b}^(⏞)^(c)$\n\nNow, if we define $c=3b-a$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}=\\dfrac{c}{b}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\sqrt{2}$ is rational.\n\n> Let $c=3b-a$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\sqrt{2} = \\frac{c}{b}$,\n> which is rational.\n> But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational."} {:db/id 17592186203724 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id 17592186203937 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68096338-e6d5-5802-9d8b-c2db96b5df3e" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Irrationality of Products and Quotients"}] :question/id #uuid "c152089a-243c-5469-9a87-5beab58b5fb0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14968" :question/problem "Prove by contradiction that $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is irrational. You may assume without proof that $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational." :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\nP{:} \\quad \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\: \\textrm{is irrational}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P{:} \\quad \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\: \\textrm{is rational}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is rational.\n\nSince we're assuming\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\nis rational, we can express it as the ratio of two non-zero integers:\n\n> Then $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the equation to give a radical on one side and a fraction on the other.\n\n> So, we have\n> $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}|= \\frac{a}{b}; \\sqrt{3}|= (2a^(⏞)^(c))/(b)$.\n\nNow, if we define the integer $c=2a$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}=\\dfrac{c}{b}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\sqrt{3}$ is rational.\n\n> Let $c=2a$. Since $2$ and $a$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{c}{b}$,\n> which is rational.\n> But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is irrational."} {:db/id 17592186204214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cfa972be-3517-5432-8d4c-c787e2a63633" :knowledge-point/title "Disproving Congruences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d816f5e5-49d7-509e-a1e2-863729fce042" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5a≡5b" :db/id 17592186199889} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d6f0b7-dbce-5c24-be13-b9ff9bd93d65" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5a≢5b" :db/id 17592186199890} {:answer/id #uuid "310cca3b-46aa-537c-bf95-56ba60fb3a4e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a≡b" :db/id 17592186199891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d816f5e5-49d7-509e-a1e2-863729fce042" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5a≡5b" :db/id 17592186199889} :answer-field/id #uuid "972823c9-d265-5e56-9e0b-e21ce4fa0e1a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186199888} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0309ea7-1bc0-5d10-a6c5-217f9f93a8c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an example" :db/id 17592186199893} {:answer/id #uuid "2a08a8c7-4152-5fb3-a304-434b32b18221" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a co