nd ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-2.$"} {:db/id 17592186243952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09c91d6d-d971-5458-b5b6-a791fcbf9443" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186241266} {:answer/id #uuid "42963ae3-0b38-512b-8bfd-1ad2c0d6d6bd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186241779} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd12c13-09d7-5e3b-908d-be282fb53d80" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186242558} {:answer/id #uuid "c9987249-694e-5f70-95b6-77fd36db1778" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186242956} {:answer/id #uuid "fc018d5e-5612-54cf-81be-1c70e01d83d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id 17592186243400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc018d5e-5612-54cf-81be-1c70e01d83d0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id 17592186243400} :answer-field/id #uuid "24fa7c93-1123-52bb-88b1-8c115f98680f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9966c61d-ab71-591b-90f6-3c48ad1407ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-106769" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 12 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 12 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=3,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=3.$"} {:db/id 17592186243953 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27a35bec-f769-5040-8918-554a7697cbed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id 17592186241523} {:answer/id #uuid "36cc640e-8f40-5e5c-98c3-85377385c418" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id 17592186241661} {:answer/id #uuid "5f1a9178-bb17-5d00-8b31-ca199e678655" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id 17592186242035} {:answer/id #uuid "71edcb95-1b5d-5373-9c02-94ff15d39ff8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id 17592186242191} {:answer/id #uuid "ea1fd436-d401-5a2d-823f-4992d3c757fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186243253}]