ector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+2I:$\n\n$$\nA+2I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+2I:$\n\n$$\n(A+2I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+2I:$\n\n$$\n(A+2I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} {:db/id 17592186247012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b08290cf-97b2-57cf-844f-51f3f8679205" :answer/type {:db/id 