n Curve Contains Zero Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0208d352-0b58-5dbc-b996-93e62e89d0a6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186269282} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4fd33c-332b-5fe8-8131-65e8649faf39" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186270106} {:answer/id #uuid "742634b0-d5c4-5ec3-b003-ad335e0436b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 5 & {5}^{2} \\\\ 6 & {6}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186270295} {:answer/id #uuid "8f65adec-2de0-518e-8924-7d66ee61522f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 2 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ -3 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 5 & 5 & {5}^{2} \\\\ -2 & 6 & {6}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186270520} {:answer/id #uuid "a226b25c-8824-556a-9476-e820fd1ca677" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {5}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {6}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186270711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a226b25c-8824-556a-9476-e820fd1ca677" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {5}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {6}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186270711} :answer-field/id #uuid "99537d23-98ec-56dd-ac18-ff2e9cd3fcec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df74ead8-2b07-5286-a37b-9876ede4abcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-139676" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-5$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $-2$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-5$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $-2$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {5}^{2} \\\\ 1 & {6}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186271966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2259a17f-5cc0-5255-86c8-c5648372ce89" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2\\,800$" :db/id 17592186269564} {:answer/id #uuid "352e371a-5116-55e8-823a-4d8ea16c5f5d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$4\\,400$" :db/id 17592186269756} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec6dbc9-9c7e-5f08-b5b7-2523fa7d0328" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3\\,700$" :db/id 17592186270377} {:answer/id #uuid "db0484e6-fc73-549b-aacc-acee23620cda" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$4\\,200$" :db/id 17592186271187} {:answer/id #uuid "f31f859d-103d-5d18-8576-c162b1f1e5ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3\\,200$" :db/id 17592186271403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f31f859d-103d-5d18-8576-c162b1f1e5ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3\\,200$" :db/id 17592186271403} :answer-field/id #uuid "26e892f1-6a97-51e5-8f0d-5824fa06ea60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a70d052-0349-524f-90c0-17395372863f" :question/math-academy-id "q-139735" :question/problem "A company collected data on the sale price (in thousands of dollars) of a particular used car model and the car's age (in years). This data is shown below.\n\n| Age $(x)$ | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$6$** | **$7$** | **$8$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Price $(y)$ | $12$ | $10$ | $8.5$ | $7$ | $6.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the sale price of a model that is $10$ years old, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}38 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}44 \\\\ 250\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 8.5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6.5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}38 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}44 \\\\ 250\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}17.2 \\\\ -1.4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=17.2-1.4x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the selling price of a car of the same model that is $10$ years old, we substitute $x=10$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =17.2-1.4(10) \\\\  & =3.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the sale price will be $$3\\,200.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=10$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."} {:db/id 17592186271967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c0dffe5-8c18-5c35-922b-ab2349cf0224" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$826$ meters" :db/id 17592186270230} {:answer/id #uuid "775e536f-38c2-5ab0-af53-9c42718f6442" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$868$ meters" :db/id 17592186270326} {:answer/id #uuid "93d8e9e4-fad7-52e1-8058-4325cb48029f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$906$ meters" :db/id 17592186270562} {:answer/id #uuid "bc89e73c-4770-571b-b13d-e6698523a25d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$902$ meters" :db/id 17592186270927} {:answer/id #uuid "fc3d4783-4f4a-52b1-a174-c83258ea7302" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$872$ meters" :db/id 17592186271476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93d8e9e4-fad7-52e1-8058-4325cb48029f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$906$ meters" :db/id 17592186270562} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e4a8479-bce9-519b-bc40-a906989c59fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186271789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "51f93e5f-4b65-5909-b2fb-547fe7d03d90" :question/math-academy-id "q-139736" :question/problem "A statistician collected data on the height above the ground (in hundreds of meters) of a hot air balloon during the first five minutes of its flight. This data is shown below. \n\n| Time $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Height $(y)$ | $1.6$ | $3.0$ | $4.6$ | $6$ | $7.6$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the balloon's height after $6$ minutes, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}22.8 \\\\ 83.4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1.6 \\\\ 3.0 \\\\ 4.6 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7.6\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}22.8 \\\\ 83.4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.06 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.06+1.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the height of the balloon after $6$ minutes, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.06+1.5(6) \\\\  & =9.06\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the balloon's height after $6$ minutes will be $906$ meters.  \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."}