t :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186342778} {:answer/id #uuid "5590b136-2148-5765-ba42-4872df4f3a5f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186342949} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b63991-e097-507e-8d58-bb1825341d01" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186343849} {:answer/id #uuid "bddcd176-e192-5da2-98c9-8a41a86d4f96" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186343909} {:answer/id #uuid "c13c6f25-efb1-5263-8ada-3dc3f47c23f0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186343935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41d289b1-173b-5941-a82a-cd3eb177ca1a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186342778} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4ad0e08-50c7-5ef0-a697-128f90e1eea2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee75bb73-f205-5858-a7b6-5d0fd3c5399e" :question/math-academy-id "q-76207" :question/problem "Find the imaginary part of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}8+\\text{i} & 3-4\\text{i} \\\\ 9 & \\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "For the given matrix $A,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}8+\\text{i} & 3-4\\text{i} \\\\ 9 & \\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8+\\text{i} & 3-4\\text{i} \\\\ 9+0\\text{i} & 0+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(A)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(A)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Im}(A)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"} {:db/id 17592186345206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b72ed196-a73b-59ab-a7c3-5173249cf7e5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of a Span"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "197bd366-c81c-5437-b024-3f9d9bfd5aef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186342460} {:answer/id #uuid "59e05b25-5ac5-5d45-ae93-9b733bcc76c3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186342992} {:answer/id #uuid "5d257bf3-941e-554d-8180-8794daa7ac3b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186343020} {:answer/id #uuid "d66cf36f-294b-5f58-921a-852e39d8f27f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id 17592186344117} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ca80bb-1258-550a-aa42-b3394f1d777b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$" :db/id 17592186344232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d257bf3-941e-554d-8180-8794daa7ac3b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186343020} :answer-field/id #uuid "326d46d2-1179-5751-b87a-d1f30eaecfc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22366352-9de1-515d-ad32-c51e7aed6aa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-76322" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $B={{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${a}_{3}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=8 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 12 & 36\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{12}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the RREF matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=3,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+3\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 3=6.$"} {:db/id 17592186345207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b72ed196-a73b-59ab-a7c3-5173249cf7e5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of a Span"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95f36be6-cd64-5615-8d16-39562702c1d6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id 17592186343548} {:answer/id #uuid "a08e0041-2623-5213-93f8-af7c8a4a59c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id 17592186343654} {:answer/id #uuid "cab5fefa-ef95-5879-810f-a84973f144fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186344022} {:answer/id #uuid "f42281b7-bdfc-55bb-ad9e-2dd4c13364eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id 17592186344387} {:answer/id #uuid "8d2d9553-3fcc-5436-9ae5-e9313490ab61" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$" :db/id 17592186429760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cab5fefa-ef95-5879-810f-a84973f144fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186344022} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d12dcd5-966c-540d-9792-0af9d3cf5c37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186344690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40af98fe-8e4f-539d-b55b-52163dd7e4ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-76327" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $B=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${a}_{3}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=-6 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the RREF matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=-2$ and ${x}_{2}=-4,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n(-2)\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+(-4)\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-2)\\cdot (-4)=8.$"} {:db/id 17592186348260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087866 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0646fdce-fd3c-56a7-8137-632d215be116" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=2x" :db/id 17592186345404} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9afd84-fe8c-56a0-89e1-00f6ac764479" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(1+x)y=0" :db/id 17592186346123} {:answer/id #uuid "71040d28-62bb-5d78-b4e6-2a190574112e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy={x}^{2}" :db/id 17592186346349} {:answer/id #uuid "d204e6a5-02d7-5404-bddf-a96974b0383d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y=-{x}^{2}" :db/id 17592186347190} {:answer/id #uuid "d895b1af-8150-5cb7-9f48-017f2ef90f34" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id 17592186347259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71040d28-62bb-5d78-b4e6-2a190574112e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy={x}^{2}" :db/id 17592186346349} :answer-field/id #uuid "74ddf3aa-733e-5972-8973-46d76ff540e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f284d9d4-47c8-54c3-aa2b-11e7ba22d40f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79697" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+y}{x}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ do not depend on $y.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{x+y}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x(x+y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}+xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+{{(-x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-x$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}.$"} {:db/id 17592186348261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087866 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "049ae29b-8ef2-515e-817d-95125832b9ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,-5{e}^{x}y=-{e}^{x}" :db/id 17592186345388} {:answer/id #uuid "139be62e-1605-5f69-b8d1-6b7a2554416d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+5y=-{e}^{x}" :db/id 17592186345521} {:answer/id #uuid "3e94f02d-431a-5153-8655-00b4a87c74f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,-5{e}^{x}y=x" :db/id 17592186345883} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef9fe74-cdac-5d6d-8da5-effef6765981" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+5{e}^{-x}y={e}^{-x}" :db/id 17592186346026} {:answer/id #uuid "50906fb5-5aea-54da-a635-e0e0c3771435" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5xy=-{e}^{-x}" :db/id 17592186346040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "049ae29b-8ef2-515e-817d-95125832b9ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,-5{e}^{x}y=-{e}^{x}" :db/id 17592186345388} :answer-field/id #uuid "92ad19c6-417f-54a2-8eac-d9c3b03caa0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186347862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a5eea4d-3d1c-5658-8171-517491bf7c77" :question/math-academy-id "q-79699" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n5{y}^{2}-y{e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y,\\,y\\ne 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ do not depend on $y.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-y{e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -y{e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5{y}^{2}-y & =0 \\\\ -y({e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y{e}^{x}+{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+{{(-5{e}^{x})}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-{e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5{e}^{x}y & =-{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-5{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x)=-{e}^{x}.$"} {:db/id 17592186351196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157424 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "037ff08b-3246-5eed-870b-b4951f319c96" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Hessian Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a863fee-48cb-518a-94c7-332f0d5dfb90" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id 17592186348671} {:answer/id #uuid "236352e1-e284-5137-93e8-01d0c0b82dd3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id 17592186348749} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6da9cd-0b80-5391-a066-ff87ff441cbe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id 17592186350254} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b5d5ad-403a-5e9b-b47e-9495ed54eecd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id 17592186350322} {:answer/id #uuid "da828216-6cb2-53d2-9a0c-74a4b4c5a870" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id 17592186350365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da828216-6cb2-53d2-9a0c-74a4b4c5a870" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id 17592186350365} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f56bbbf-1a13-5c6d-a2d7-69c5469daa32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0dab700a-fa4f-5675-9473-37a2b428ee20" :question/math-academy-id "q-81023" :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2(x-1){y}^{2} & 2(x-1)^{2}y\\end{bmatrix},$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2(x-1){y}^{2} & 2(x-1)^{2}y\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2(x-1){y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2(x-1)^{2}y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(x-1){y}^{2})=2{y}^{2}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(x-1)^{2}y)=4(x-1)y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(x-1){y}^{2})=4(x-1)y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(x-1)^{2}y)=2(x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{2} & 4(x-1)y \\\\ 4(x-1)y & 2(x-1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=2\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=-12.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186351224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157423 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c4234a-3aa1-5b6b-b68b-48e6aa2b0dfb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Function Given Its First Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "425c4600-816d-5d3d-a9f5-610a8c6f6b97" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186349049} {:answer/id #uuid "5b410b83-b065-5e13-9b2d-6ef4baf10829" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2-x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186349287} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7db402-a450-5d26-b53a-3c303da293b9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{2-x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186350237} {:answer/id #uuid "fb980d82-64ab-5a6d-bc31-3b09443b34e7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186350666} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa20bb0-a35b-5276-af26-5bf35fc36a88" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186350703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b410b83-b065-5e13-9b2d-6ef4baf10829" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2-x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186349287} :answer-field/id #uuid "92d68f61-fe7f-5864-9dfe-8847dee23855" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5fc8ae5c-d352-5fd3-ae18-630a629fa87e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81110" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}e-{e}^{2-x} & 2y\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}e-{e}^{2-x} & 2y\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=e-{e}^{2-x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(e-{e}^{2-x})={e}^{2-x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(e-{e}^{2-x})=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2-x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186351225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157425 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf99849-1f2b-5c34-828c-164ad4058c92" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f668f1-6f0e-5f03-b24f-252b61525c29" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 2{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & {e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186348618} {:answer/id #uuid "1add0944-c518-57ff-ac8e-03b370a5e1d6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(2+4{x}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (2+4{y}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186348676} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a2227e-48bd-56c9-81ed-74c049e159a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(2+4{x}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ 4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & (2+4{y}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186349885} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a8889f-460d-579b-bd13-17b7e4dee99e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(2+4{x}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 2{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & (2+4{y}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186350287} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ff04a1-b040-539e-b8a9-e7c0a5de4c45" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(2+4x){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 2{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & (2+4y){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186350455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1a2227e-48bd-56c9-81ed-74c049e159a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(2+4{x}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ 4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & (2+4{y}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186349885} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5510882-92e2-5e9a-b981-503b92ddedf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186351139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e5fb489-dfa6-50c2-a270-dc2d65f25d4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81111" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=(2+4{x}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=(2+4{y}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}(2+4{x}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ 4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & (2+4{y}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186351229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157425 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf99849-1f2b-5c34-828c-164ad4058c92" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0379a8f3-5c08-5a0e-9aa9-ee7247f5bb7d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2x \\\\ 2x & 2({x}^{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186348479} {:answer/id #uuid "0f2c18f5-f6c6-5cb7-9bd3-ef891a719882" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2({x}^{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186348580} {:answer/id #uuid "235323a0-a480-5c75-9639-2308eb39664a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2xy \\\\ 2xy & 2({x}^{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186348748} {:answer/id #uuid "3438c329-ab2c-5701-840d-707e4cfa738f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2({x}^{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186348915} {:answer/id #uuid "703d071f-0017-59ad-ade4-9601ec2c639e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{2} & 4xy \\\\ 4xy & 2({x}^{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186349481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "703d071f-0017-59ad-ade4-9601ec2c639e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{2} & 4xy \\\\ 4xy & 2({x}^{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186349481} :answer-field/id #uuid "1892be87-58b9-5f45-a8a1-451663b051c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "235e87ab-40f0-5bca-9e1d-4479ce5e2de8" :question/math-academy-id "q-81119" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)={y}^{2}({x}^{2}-1).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}({x}^{2}-1)) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}({x}^{2}-1))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{2} & 2y({x}^{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2})=2{y}^{2}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y({x}^{2}-1))=4xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2})=4xy\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y({x}^{2}-1))=2({x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{2} & 4xy \\\\ 4xy & 2({x}^{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186351230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157425 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf99849-1f2b-5c34-828c-164ad4058c92" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c42b794-72d6-514f-85b6-40ef2b7dcd98" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186348690} {:answer/id #uuid "3454730a-5aab-57e6-8c0f-17209a604055" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186348916} {:answer/id #uuid "7b479b77-d903-5f0c-8f7d-530e7863b915" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186349579} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f41cb7-2e5a-5303-bc84-7130dab8c440" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186350611} {:answer/id #uuid "fb35d3bb-cccc-50cf-84a5-4d39d7149a97" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186350661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb35d3bb-cccc-50cf-84a5-4d39d7149a97" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186350661} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb30829b-8d9a-5a87-b3fc-44d8886af1ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186351029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0469958-83f9-5c79-aae7-bbff4a96354f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81123" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(-1,2)$ for the function $f(x,y)=(y-1)({x}^{2}-1).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y-1)({x}^{2}-1)) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}((y-1)({x}^{2}-1))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x(y-1) & {x}^{2}-1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(y-1))=2(y-1)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-1)=2x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(y-1))=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2(y-1) & 2x \\\\ 2x & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(-1,2).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,2)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186351262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "768e3afb-bb09-5e8d-afce-24265c595101" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186349537} {:answer/id #uuid "8b60cf77-97aa-5202-8d4e-811565f0e808" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186349708} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea6050c-f1c9-5dd1-b2f0-9331f4c3eb90" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186349731} {:answer/id #uuid "dd99276d-af91-5354-a572-a9a70ed58f02" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186350384} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e335fc-583e-509e-ace9-504cc6b7c779" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186350581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd99276d-af91-5354-a572-a9a70ed58f02" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186350384} :answer-field/id #uuid "91c9727d-1213-5c42-9fe0-34caddae9641" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c93e7214-d507-511f-96e2-a29d77beb762" :question/math-academy-id "q-81210" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $E,$ then $E$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $E=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $E,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nE={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}(\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 12 \\\\ 16 & 24\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -8 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186351264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7579ab44-bd79-5efe-b0c2-f284657c4cbf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 & 4 \\\\ 15 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186349527} {:answer/id #uuid "8e42582e-5cb4-5323-a399-3b20a65879bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}13 & -12 \\\\ 10 & -9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186349729} {:answer/id #uuid "bb84f3e0-0aaf-50c7-8d43-3326b2bff35b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186350116} {:answer/id #uuid "e07ef508-ba37-54d5-9f11-5ede72a99e7f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186350419} {:answer/id #uuid "e861d1eb-9875-5c71-862e-3da22156c169" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -13 \\\\ 9 & 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186350480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e42582e-5cb4-5323-a399-3b20a65879bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}13 & -12 \\\\ 10 & -9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186349729} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b0b11ff-0df6-52af-aa76-996c6a221114" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186350919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "725672f2-a45d-5f52-bf51-6290d583f846" :question/math-academy-id "q-81215" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $S$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $S,$ then $S$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $S=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $S,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nS={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}18 & 1 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}13 & -12 \\\\ 10 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186351511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c9a982e-789e-57ee-9313-d961168981dd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(x+2)^{n+1}}{(n-1)!}" :db/id 17592186348694} {:answer/id #uuid "2591d7dd-e5ac-526d-b029-e19dd18733ea" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=(n+1)(x+2)^{n}" :db/id 17592186348770} {:answer/id #uuid "370122c6-0175-5269-be0f-7443f6c20c3d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n}}{n}" :db/id 17592186348942} {:answer/id #uuid "38611bce-0eda-5c55-9458-cd66822817fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n+1}}{n}" :db/id 17592186348957} {:answer/id #uuid "f96a6180-de48-52ee-bbac-e0f6f2b37a33" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n}}{n!}" :db/id 17592186350638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f96a6180-de48-52ee-bbac-e0f6f2b37a33" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n}}{n!}" :db/id 17592186350638} :answer-field/id #uuid "fed5e4b2-7bc2-5e3c-934d-74d159ac03ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186351153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3cb83323-0582-536b-82c9-36fd3324b5dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-83461" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n}\\frac{(x+2)^{n+1}}{n!}.\n$$\n\nFind ${a}_{n},$ given that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n}{a}_{n}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2!}(x+2)^{3}-⋯+{{(-1)^{n}\\frac{(x+2)^{n+1}}{n!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-2(x+2)+\\frac{3}{2!}(x+2)^{2}-⋯+{{(-1)^{n}\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n}}{n!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n}\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n}}{n!}\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(n+1)(x+2)^{n}}{n!}.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186351512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14efd8da-92d9-56ca-87f0-7b9c109fd645" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}" :db/id 17592186348624} {:answer/id #uuid "2d12e31b-4e0d-5cbf-b3f5-530c47674393" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{n(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}" :db/id 17592186348843} {:answer/id #uuid "74c8d362-fff4-5ad6-a29f-ddb61e16e9c3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n}}{n+3}" :db/id 17592186349520} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f48129-9fe7-5698-ad6a-8a09d7e036b7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}" :db/id 17592186349919} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e5bc7a-a560-5d6d-b4ad-5f19f818004a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{n(x-3)^{n}}{n+3}" :db/id 17592186350435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14efd8da-92d9-56ca-87f0-7b9c109fd645" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}" :db/id 17592186348624} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd8be60f-8890-5517-8355-b858e2ff7c0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186351093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3072a41e-55dc-5057-8605-bd6994ecfa75" :question/math-academy-id "q-83463" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{n(x-3)^{n}}{n+3}.\n$$\n\nFind ${a}_{n},$ given that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n+1}{a}_{n}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-3)-\\frac{2}{5}(x-3)^{2}+⋯+{{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{n(x-3)^{n}}{n+3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{4}{5}(x-3)+⋯+{{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}(x-3)^{n-1}}{n+3}.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38a65464-7728-54f8-82e5-5a2139643a60" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{141}" :db/id 17592186352091} {:answer/id #uuid "82393b05-c41a-5547-a882-b804e6c62041" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/id 17592186352700} {:answer/id #uuid "aacaaba4-ab74-5368-89bd-bd264c67aed5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/id 17592186353013} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2b6dc7-09dd-5774-8bb3-21a78e74780b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{111}" :db/id 17592186353324} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c12ebd-3d57-549f-806e-0c3911d9efbc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{114}" :db/id 17592186353565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4c12ebd-3d57-549f-806e-0c3911d9efbc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{114}" :db/id 17592186353565} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c5b6c7d-97a2-5520-a2b8-341edc162192" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186353949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "476850d6-1065-514f-9c2e-38a6ce482b21" :question/math-academy-id "q-85278" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+(-7)^{2}}=\\sqrt{114}.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eb3bac9-cce7-5665-8498-a91231b362d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{7}" :db/id 17592186351693} {:answer/id #uuid "2b1a3d3a-a796-5667-954c-1ac273943f89" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{17}" :db/id 17592186351958} {:answer/id #uuid "37274342-d16b-5dbb-b2ca-57ddd587c88b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{71}" :db/id 17592186352080} {:answer/id #uuid "8bca07bf-2a3f-5acc-bea4-d63eb68cd5f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{71}" :db/id 17592186352786} {:answer/id #uuid "b7338b51-8139-5d2f-8cea-d81f922627ea" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}" :db/id 17592186353134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37274342-d16b-5dbb-b2ca-57ddd587c88b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{71}" :db/id 17592186352080} :answer-field/id #uuid "a221e58d-1676-5cff-be01-420fdcdb8b0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186354097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c34a906-4ce9-5f9d-848e-084be407b879" :question/math-academy-id "q-85280" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 44\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 44\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{(-9)^{2}+(-11)^{2}+(-9)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=2\\sqrt{71}.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59881df0-2ac6-532e-8fb9-91b698aa746f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/id 17592186352356} {:answer/id #uuid "6136a078-6ec6-579a-809a-d2077533b6a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id 17592186352433} {:answer/id #uuid "76500562-20f2-5c7e-bdb3-4992adaf7d17" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{111}" :db/id 17592186352607} {:answer/id #uuid "7917c258-2cdc-5cb9-a21d-ebdf986ccad6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{101}" :db/id 17592186352623} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb29578-9f41-5fba-9274-bad7f34afe91" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{110}" :db/id 17592186352661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cb29578-9f41-5fba-9274-bad7f34afe91" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{110}" :db/id 17592186352661} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e227d03-ecbf-552f-86b6-2f39084c0aba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186354049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "22b88813-fc85-5da7-b050-31934e04cdb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-85284" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are shown below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{110}.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092180 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bec73486-156f-5124-97b4-58f65ea8430d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58b5467d-b3c5-5fa1-8dd8-e7968657a579" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{2}" :db/id 17592186352347} {:answer/id #uuid "8710d42d-7935-5a34-a159-c3d52b9c79f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id 17592186352747} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f6acc7-b014-57e3-a7f0-774f0d026721" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75}{4}" :db/id 17592186353144} {:answer/id #uuid "ee21e249-28f2-5619-971e-832911f96ce1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id 17592186353641} {:answer/id #uuid "f92703f1-8b0a-55f6-8313-c3424a334bd8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{39}{2}" :db/id 17592186353743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f92703f1-8b0a-55f6-8313-c3424a334bd8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{39}{2}" :db/id 17592186353743} :answer-field/id #uuid "de862061-c08e-53ba-9c58-6b2f598b2524" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186354196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c8ccca6-4e29-5faa-987e-8624611baf62" :question/math-academy-id "q-85683" :question/problem "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[-1,2]=[-1,0]\\cup [0,2],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the function $y=\\frac{10}{1+{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{10}{1+{x}^{2}}.$ Let's plot this function:\n\n![](images/c4/c4c9aa8e615747199462e0597f4cb7be82031efdb5b2f6585e3fb9c0b652ee78.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-1)+f(0)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(2)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{5+10}{2}\\cdot (0-(-1))+\\frac{10+2}{2}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{12}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}+12 \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092180 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bec73486-156f-5124-97b4-58f65ea8430d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20c33d56-02a5-55a1-987b-f18618f02ab0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{51}{4}" :db/id 17592186351859} {:answer/id #uuid "56c3feda-01c0-54f6-9ead-50588c1e134c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{59}{4}" :db/id 17592186352332} {:answer/id #uuid "6fee69a4-9d15-57e5-9494-bb3b8ffd602f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{2}" :db/id 17592186352550} {:answer/id #uuid "b8126dbd-926a-548c-ae76-4a7fc73ef299" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id 17592186353145} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ed98eb-c41a-50d8-a21b-e1b53145334b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id 17592186353283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fee69a4-9d15-57e5-9494-bb3b8ffd602f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{2}" :db/id 17592186352550} :answer-field/id #uuid "07c262c8-b003-5f0c-b3d2-74d61bf1d3cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186353830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ca2808a-5bb5-5c46-a46a-d74fa247deba" :question/math-academy-id "q-85725" :question/problem "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[-1,4]=[-1,0]\\cup [0,3]\\cup [3,4],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](images/48/48212ed7c5cf3d8b6e523a98b91634320b8684c889e55d62ce4043f082f83cde.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-1)+f(0)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(3)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{3\\text{rd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(3)+f(4)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{0+2}{2}\\cdot (0-(-1))+\\frac{2+4}{2}\\cdot (3-0)+\\frac{4+5}{2}\\cdot (4-3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 3+\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+9+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092180 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bec73486-156f-5124-97b4-58f65ea8430d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66866db0-87c2-53a2-889d-76a9fbcb702d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id 17592186352471} {:answer/id #uuid "73e18a41-8ed8-533b-8dbe-522231f48baf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{2}" :db/id 17592186352582} {:answer/id #uuid "966717c0-09db-5b27-8cab-82ac8885b55d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id 17592186352869} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6cc7d2-ebd5-5064-84cf-a03a3771dfb3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{39}{2}" :db/id 17592186353153} {:answer/id #uuid "de01fdae-30a5-586f-af19-2e23b6aa2c2d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id 17592186353495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de01fdae-30a5-586f-af19-2e23b6aa2c2d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id 17592186353495} :answer-field/id #uuid "17f73283-19af-5ff4-92be-7a3948c24901" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186353855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "014601a5-7d03-5a59-9fc6-b409b517a1e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-85726" :question/problem "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[-3,3]=[-3,-2]\\cup [-2,0]\\cup [0,3],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $6$ | $4$ | $3$ | $1$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](images/3a/3a9a6a7a340299c0b1f3fa349ad705c34079b74b0d3d1d1c3205d12ed9ef485d.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-3)+f(-2)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-2)+f(0)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{3\\text{rd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(3)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{6+4}{2}\\cdot (-2-(-3))+\\frac{4+3}{2}\\cdot (0-(-2))+\\frac{3+1}{2}\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1+\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =5+7+6 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d23c4292-6fdd-5cba-a2e7-0f4d770b2309" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34c4d3c3-0803-5e7d-9345-96fb67a5c902" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186352058} {:answer/id #uuid "80911abe-21e9-5140-8653-0d040661f186" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186352689} {:answer/id #uuid "bbef92f5-bc23-5fdc-a91b-960a4232941f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186353164} {:answer/id #uuid "e1942471-0602-51ba-8547-44bd01d58aee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186353523} {:answer/id #uuid "ec065dfd-7d91-51b7-91a4-a6cd26c12915" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186353623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1942471-0602-51ba-8547-44bd01d58aee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186353523} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8d7592d-5151-5ba8-a5b7-bd3172011f1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186354110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5a3f449-8731-5956-af27-4aa8fe25fa80" :question/math-academy-id "q-85892" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d23c4292-6fdd-5cba-a2e7-0f4d770b2309" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47f733f4-12f3-5c07-8f4b-304b44459b9f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186352230} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2c331c-8cc2-5e5e-b4b6-de1a54f34fe1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186352806} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ad2f12-4695-596d-8cec-b7f38fb28a29" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186353297} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a56cfe-ee7f-5cca-9b65-eb74bde205f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186353390} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c8975d-a61b-537d-897c-5fdec1b16da4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186353601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2a56cfe-ee7f-5cca-9b65-eb74bde205f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186353390} :answer-field/id #uuid "cda82a11-1d36-50fa-8ecc-f1dd1a7dc6ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186354171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48fc2839-660f-57b5-b91c-b2fcd5bae103" :question/math-academy-id "q-85899" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 32 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 24\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}48 \\\\ 48 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{96}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}48 \\\\ 48 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15daec31-7862-5263-80db-2090dc8fbbd7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id 17592186351758} {:answer/id #uuid "40225e20-962a-5ae3-8315-535b48344c65" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id 17592186352161} {:answer/id #uuid "46ff8bf7-6336-5516-8b73-cfe228faf760" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id 17592186352223} {:answer/id #uuid "c842ab27-883d-524b-b73e-ecdeb8f933ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id 17592186353309} {:answer/id #uuid "e98a1f81-e483-53e1-8bae-170bf4fa1ee5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id 17592186353600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c842ab27-883d-524b-b73e-ecdeb8f933ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id 17592186353309} :answer-field/id #uuid "478d5745-16e4-5393-ada0-6f0f2aa4b32a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186353942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fe94dab-ed8a-5caf-8874-c5b5c72bebb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-85909" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186078598 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "518a1e9d-4fc4-54c2-88f9-b4bece17e9a6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186352301} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb1865f-2880-5084-a8c5-8740d51e1638" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id 17592186352549} {:answer/id #uuid "86b563d9-c591-5329-8785-c1a4b5184185" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186352743} {:answer/id #uuid "cffd7977-8275-5edc-b765-fdc3aaa55f58" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id 17592186353366} {:answer/id #uuid "f979b1f2-b550-5ba4-9afa-1d30662d03af" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id 17592186353746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f979b1f2-b550-5ba4-9afa-1d30662d03af" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id 17592186353746} :answer-field/id #uuid "92fe539f-0edb-5b5c-8b90-21ac128322d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186354076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eafbc70a-7fe6-568d-a054-e25ba5f557b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-86167" :question/problem "![](images/ba/bac953cd2389ef36b415dcdce87730f244958ef0fe2f67911878c5cb3bfcafc6.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in (0,4],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [4,6].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)(4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [4,6].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =8+4-1 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186078598 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dc0a57f-68f8-5052-acd6-9fe8f1132818" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186351677} {:answer/id #uuid "4193aeeb-8b5a-55ca-9758-44520f74d67d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186352172} {:answer/id #uuid "943cdf43-5e78-575c-a6d6-6d9075798804" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id 17592186352851} {:answer/id #uuid "af9b379e-acc2-5073-afed-61fd29ad6b66" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186353063} {:answer/id #uuid "b4645403-897e-5ff6-9e8e-c816d30d009c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id 17592186353110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af9b379e-acc2-5073-afed-61fd29ad6b66" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186353063} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb4bfbbe-d621-5831-bcf0-492fb3da13f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186354254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d1e9774-81f1-5594-9769-363e35f6c2db" :question/math-academy-id "q-86168" :question/problem "![](images/e9/e9e1ba80436256409381b9d1c1762696156abe1cbfb516f03cfb4837ae2330b2.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,0)$ and the line segment for $x\\in (0,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,0).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =4+4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186078598 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "896ffdd3-8589-5928-a8bf-d20ba46df469" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id 17592186352766} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf46d83-25b4-5937-9aae-c7c27751b925" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186352788} {:answer/id #uuid "c001bb20-055e-50b5-be29-744ebf6c4a72" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186353217} {:answer/id #uuid "c803bd64-f6a2-5364-b05c-5a37ac1de747" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id 17592186353306} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4d0092-8f74-5633-8113-3ce745643fbf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186353608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c001bb20-055e-50b5-be29-744ebf6c4a72" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186353217} :answer-field/id #uuid "8adabeca-b826-598f-899a-01480745fc79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186354067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2cefee34-6c96-52a2-810c-1fc692ea436d" :question/math-academy-id "q-86170" :question/problem "![](images/3e/3ec66e7d27a46ad991e1aa10281a9850c500ba649b9699834fd867247b665376.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,1)$ and the line segment for $x\\in (1,5].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,1).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =ab \\\\  & =5(1) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-5.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,5].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+4)(4) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =-5+10 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186078599 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "136111b7-0834-547c-80d0-1d009758dc3c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+\\pi " :db/id 17592186351734} {:answer/id #uuid "2a15147b-b82d-5d08-b257-0ff20f5f4993" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-\\pi " :db/id 17592186351954} {:answer/id #uuid "6d99cb59-adab-577b-b0f7-e416a14ae926" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9-\\pi " :db/id 17592186352531} {:answer/id #uuid "cde67277-a7d8-51e5-baab-0c3c2114f90f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-\\pi " :db/id 17592186353355} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7329cd-d17e-5495-bc47-87ef9fdd13e2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9+\\pi " :db/id 17592186353776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d99cb59-adab-577b-b0f7-e416a14ae926" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9-\\pi " :db/id 17592186352531} :answer-field/id #uuid "66c4b64d-ca76-5776-939f-630ddca986ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186354009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eba3d729-fa27-507f-86e4-8cdb46533946" :question/math-academy-id "q-86174" :question/problem "![](images/3e/3e0eca1a04396c0c86bbf5c3e5a9b3ff7071eacfe3d8c4281549e70cda809e8c.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,3)$ and the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in (3,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,3).$ The corresponding region is a right triange, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (3,5].$ The corresponding region is a combination of an inverted quarter-circle of radius $2$ and a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =5+(4-\\pi ) \\\\  & =9-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186078599 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2574980-7b3e-56b9-9bb5-816d7922784b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14+2\\pi " :db/id 17592186352957} {:answer/id #uuid "c4cf3c4c-27ba-5ebd-92c3-a4887a144a40" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21-2\\pi " :db/id 17592186353271} {:answer/id #uuid "e45864b2-aba3-5647-b30b-b33f87e22338" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19-2\\pi " :db/id 17592186353559} {:answer/id #uuid "ea063350-e7f6-5fd1-bd2f-d066a7fbdb8a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12+2\\pi " :db/id 17592186353604} {:answer/id #uuid "fe9cef2c-3376-5342-98e4-920a5e325266" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18-4\\pi " :db/id 17592186353801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4cf3c4c-27ba-5ebd-92c3-a4887a144a40" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21-2\\pi " :db/id 17592186353271} :answer-field/id #uuid "56c54c8a-827e-5695-9838-346de9675d3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186353969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "805b2992-f685-5d7b-bccd-5900affa2a81" :question/math-academy-id "q-86178" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f9b82ba9e1ed640aedd6f67118beef2d88c419bbd046171cca5b527c2b407a5.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-2]$, the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-2,2),$ and the line segment for $x\\in (2,4].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,2).$ The corresponding region is a combination of an inverted semi-circle of radius $2$ and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh-\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =4(3)-\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =12-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (2,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =6+12-2\\pi +3 \\\\  & =21-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15c0277c-fb4b-5026-9fd8-6c7810e057b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id 17592186351756} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6dd320-c836-51a4-ad23-8614799db41b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id 17592186351838} {:answer/id #uuid "5f17f867-8ede-528f-b531-5e234c5f7333" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id 17592186352410} {:answer/id #uuid "67375961-39e8-54d5-9054-515f282d6534" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id 17592186352482} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a357ac-56ed-5863-b3dc-01fa6b42eb7c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id 17592186353259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3a357ac-56ed-5863-b3dc-01fa6b42eb7c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id 17592186353259} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d77c46d-1aa3-501f-aae2-671960790f6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186353950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "682dd404-2e33-5194-9b75-83eececc2df8" :question/math-academy-id "q-86350" :question/problem "The following table shows the velocity of a plane during a $20$-minute trip.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{minutes})$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)\\,(\\text{kilometers/minute})$ | $17$ | $25$ | $27$ | $k$ | $18$ |\n\nIf the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance traveled during this period has a value of  $450\\,\\text{km}$, what is the value of $k?$\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the velocity-time graph.*" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve $y=v(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{v(5)+v(10)+v(15)+v(20)}})\\cdot {{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $v(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)$ | $25$ | $27$ | $k$ | $18$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(25+27+k+18)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5(k+70) \\\\  & =5k+350\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total distance traveled during the $20$ minute period. Since we are given that the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance is $450\\,\\text{km},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5k+350 & =450 \\\\ 5k & =450-350 \\\\ k & =\\frac{100}{5} \\\\ k & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0065af71-29a6-5ebd-9bb2-e8f6fd53309c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-17 & 30 \\\\ -9 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186351566} {:answer/id #uuid "0e38c9a8-52aa-57b5-8cd2-d15086786ae4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186351684} {:answer/id #uuid "20bda32c-1ae4-5791-b78f-9ded45871b93" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 9 \\\\ -3 & 17\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186351857} {:answer/id #uuid "43179f12-d2ec-5fea-8ae8-1b1e8c829d6e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186352184} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7a0b89-b520-516a-984b-548f08bea2b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 19 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186352367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e38c9a8-52aa-57b5-8cd2-d15086786ae4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186351684} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ec5791-f08c-5efc-bb1c-b825849222ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186353880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa2ab21a-e8b5-56c0-b8a9-838a56ba099f" :question/math-academy-id "q-86676" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $G$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $G,$ then $G$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $G=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $G,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nG={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124572 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce48fd3-6d90-5867-bc8c-48f781a9a134" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186351673} {:answer/id #uuid "6dea34ce-51cc-54ce-bd9e-2e2c5ec68b40" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186352535} {:answer/id #uuid "71ac80d5-5537-5ad5-953b-dc791c6b3dbd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186352561} {:answer/id #uuid "7239e333-3699-5e0e-a679-3638ec2f4a51" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -12 \\\\ 21 & -32\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186352567} {:answer/id #uuid "7b23a21b-03eb-5d9a-8c5d-1e2dbf0ce327" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -19 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186352637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ce48fd3-6d90-5867-bc8c-48f781a9a134" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186351673} :answer-field/id #uuid "60ed20fe-9723-5a36-b174-23a11d61985b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186353992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1fcc8b2-84cd-574b-a993-68fbd5d3c967" :question/math-academy-id "q-86680" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $E,$ then $E$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $E=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $E,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nE={{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}(\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -15 \\\\ 12 & -30\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}19 & -12 \\\\ 32 & -21\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124530 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0838f7e1-1e2a-5de4-899d-0534f10f1b2b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186351641} {:answer/id #uuid "4bdae80f-03b9-55c3-aadd-11684fd45d72" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186352262} {:answer/id #uuid "836126d0-86c5-54f1-8f69-77f8c1c3afc5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186352716} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a16fcf-857f-5b60-adf6-cb3cf7586045" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186353550} {:answer/id #uuid "f648b42e-29eb-56ba-be95-7cf583a892a5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186353717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0838f7e1-1e2a-5de4-899d-0534f10f1b2b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186351641} :answer-field/id #uuid "29f200b4-abd8-556b-96a6-d61eebe5f500" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186353886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18c9e3a0-322a-5b85-8d72-26e41db87e30" :question/math-academy-id "q-86682" :question/problem "Find ${A}^{71},$ where\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A:$\n\n$$\nA={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSince $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{71} & =P{D}^{71}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{71}(\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)^{71} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{71}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124530 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" :knowledge-point/title "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27359bfd-be3c-5cf1-bd01-de39941cd5ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 60 \\\\ -8 & 28\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186351924} {:answer/id #uuid "69b41b65-8c9f-57bf-a764-c4be901cae38" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-60 & 16 \\\\ -8 & 28\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186352504} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca878c6-3ac9-5a69-85fc-d735d7d1e6a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 60 \\\\ -28 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186352524} {:answer/id #uuid "c65ad6da-b0e6-5d01-9b41-a20ca0d5bf88" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 60 \\\\ -18 & 28\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186353289} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c25710-3be0-58ab-8eca-84880e70270f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 6 \\\\ -8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186353299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27359bfd-be3c-5cf1-bd01-de39941cd5ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 60 \\\\ -8 & 28\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186351924} :answer-field/id #uuid "11865484-c0f9-5b42-84f8-6f6395f0b142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186353844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c81d246-a69b-5179-85f7-88df79048f1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-86689" :question/problem "Find ${M}^{24},$ where\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We have been given the following diagonalization of $M.$\n\n$$\nM={{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $M=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${M}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{M}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{24} & =P{D}^{24}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt[12]{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt[8]{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{24}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}(\\sqrt[12]{2})^{24} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (\\sqrt[8]{2})^{24}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 40 \\\\ 4 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 60 \\\\ -8 & 28\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b940dc-40c4-5587-99cb-a46b5669f00c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186351581} {:answer/id #uuid "36451ec8-9550-599f-9524-6efb083c9e84" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186352073} {:answer/id #uuid "5c0011e3-3c09-55fb-a899-6fc1c02d871e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186352377} {:answer/id #uuid "6678d030-b08a-54a4-8dbd-e35a3db5a4f0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186352470} {:answer/id #uuid "92887453-53d2-5699-9d42-9b75a6b33c30" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186352835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92887453-53d2-5699-9d42-9b75a6b33c30" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186352835} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e3561b1-f239-5584-a442-e885bcbde6bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186353984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3d81f51f-fe75-524a-aa0f-42e04cbcf9fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-86691" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}E=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E:$\n\n$$\nE={{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$"} {:db/id 17592186354495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121c30d3-381d-5eec-812d-224e51ec2ed5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186351718} {:answer/id #uuid "28b0ed5c-9277-577c-b0f4-46d45ce08e7f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186351944} {:answer/id #uuid "82f7010b-3ded-54da-922f-5d2a3139d0b8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186352711} {:answer/id #uuid "b584defc-90ec-51d9-af5c-7473d8c554a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186353119} {:answer/id #uuid "c68fd66d-ff23-5aca-a7e5-22e416ac2158" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186353290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "121c30d3-381d-5eec-812d-224e51ec2ed5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186351718} :answer-field/id #uuid "41d0c1c4-db7a-5402-823c-10bcfe18c23c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186353929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a0ebddfd-78f1-5dda-8990-10394fcd7459" :question/math-academy-id "q-86693" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}B=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB={{{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=7$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$"} {:db/id 17592186354496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123147cc-29a9-5a9b-8197-5141287824db" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186351722} {:answer/id #uuid "13590b66-311d-5d93-824a-b8885d95a619" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186351733} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce46df1-f81c-53bf-9562-397b90e58c93" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186352387} {:answer/id #uuid "d8e2a4f2-1115-53ef-a5a2-b66e4951afd5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186353452} {:answer/id #uuid "e2dc21a8-005f-55af-bc53-974ee66b05b1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186353540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8e2a4f2-1115-53ef-a5a2-b66e4951afd5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186353452} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece55d7d-c7d4-5233-ba8f-7f279a6be42b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186354222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5dc95dbe-f9a5-583f-bd3e-71e914c88035" :question/math-academy-id "q-86694" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}T=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $T:$\n\n$$\nT={{{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $T$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$"} {:db/id 17592186354572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186100815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a673b6-4025-5075-9a8a-120b90bc4f45" :knowledge-point/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Perpendicular Bisector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0244a00d-4710-50e9-8997-212737b64b38" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/id 17592186351584} {:answer/id #uuid "27c69d55-1729-5939-a1f1-8d6f552d0aa2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id 17592186351931} {:answer/id #uuid "5ebb7ae7-468c-57ec-9b4d-324812e14481" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id 17592186352406} {:answer/id #uuid "d99c9ed5-0a76-5cae-b60c-722ece8b8e99" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,2)" :db/id 17592186353457} {:answer/id #uuid "dae524f4-19b6-5fa4-8c85-43cf6c01b47a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id 17592186353468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ebb7ae7-468c-57ec-9b4d-324812e14481" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id 17592186352406} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ab93659-2867-5b81-aaea-c8668289e6de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186354014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c3f9e3b-ce11-530a-9baa-05d3b323a845" :question/math-academy-id "q-87502" :question/problem "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(1,2)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n \n\n![](images/05/052b405c104f0d6bc353782c6e07bfb1e0245726d86346a545ea38aa31941711.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(1,2)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-4y+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-2,0)$ and $B(1,3).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-(-2))^{2}+(y-0)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =(x-1)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x+4 & ={x}^{2}-2x+1+{y}^{2}-6y+9 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}+4x+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-2x+1+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-6y+9 \\\\ 6y & =-6x+10-4 \\\\ y & =-x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(-x+1)^{2}-2x-4(-x+1)+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-2x+1-2x+4x-4+5 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =4x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(0)=4>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=0$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=-0+1=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(0,1).$"} {:db/id 17592186354573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186100815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a673b6-4025-5075-9a8a-120b90bc4f45" :knowledge-point/title "The Shortest Distance Between a Point and a Perpendicular Bisector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b12f7b4-7350-5a21-b008-1a533954dcda" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,3)" :db/id 17592186352109} {:answer/id #uuid "abba52c0-814c-5b2f-9e2d-4874044e701a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id 17592186353023} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c9b68d-8b43-58fe-895a-665afc0d4def" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,1)" :db/id 17592186353300} {:answer/id #uuid "f5161e75-5690-5b58-8b4a-c973a4e037ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,1)" :db/id 17592186353707} {:answer/id #uuid "fe92d2b8-ef0e-5966-99eb-7bda1593f02a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/id 17592186353800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5161e75-5690-5b58-8b4a-c973a4e037ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,1)" :db/id 17592186353707} :answer-field/id #uuid "67ed4368-1a6a-5ad3-8782-0a36a45be756" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186354011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b28c6e16-047f-5299-b8dc-57e7d48491a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-87515" :question/problem "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(2,2)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](images/8b/8be39e6dde084154ecfd43718cb76352c69d26bd76d520177a6c41ee69a7f18e.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(2,2)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-4y+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,-2).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-0)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-4)^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =(x-4)^{2}+(y+2)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y+4 & ={x}^{2}-8x+16+{y}^{2}+4y+4 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-4y+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-8x+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}+4y+20 \\\\ 8y & =8x-16 \\\\ y & =x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+(x-2)^{2}-4x-4\\cdot (x-2)+8 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}-4x+4-4x-4x+8+8 \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-12x+20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =4x-12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(3)=4>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=3$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(3,1).$"} {:db/id 17592186354636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47596227-58dd-5c85-b31b-dd2085e9d721" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186352226} {:answer/id #uuid "555a8298-192e-50be-8d44-14723e16f11d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id 17592186352323} {:answer/id #uuid "58619be6-c805-5344-9021-173a95976dd3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id 17592186352344} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9db913-3c22-5c37-be9e-ca617e5d0dee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186353213} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c2b5b7-b8a6-51db-98b0-9f4ba4bb301f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186353585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47596227-58dd-5c85-b31b-dd2085e9d721" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186352226} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bd53aab-78d5-531e-a248-9e6ce0d0938b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186353889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31688145-09ed-5774-a522-d76e627c7dee" :question/math-academy-id "q-88296" :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=4,$\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{4}+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4+1(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186354637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4dd07e-475b-5c89-883b-0e4adcf6a723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Determinant Using the Linearity of the Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59a4042a-f605-574f-a228-ff4ae6d308e5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id 17592186352358} {:answer/id #uuid "73d447eb-1e81-51dc-b291-5725cee52d82" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186352581} {:answer/id #uuid "74558c61-b294-5a8c-be71-44c0d3822c23" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id 17592186352586} {:answer/id #uuid "b27eb1ec-b060-5c68-b2a6-225457f0de0e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id 17592186353093} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c62a94-26b3-5bea-ae7b-f2fbef1384ec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186353125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74558c61-b294-5a8c-be71-44c0d3822c23" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id 17592186352586} :answer-field/id #uuid "607dd21d-aeb7-5920-91bc-f439f202ce2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186353991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27889ca3-5f85-5eb8-aee5-93c6c3d6c2ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-88298" :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b+3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=5,$\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| & ={{|\\begin{aligned}(a+2) & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3) & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|}_{⏟}}_{5}+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5+(-10) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186357690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35013c7e-161d-57b9-8b70-9180bf4e5f9d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d05ac71-25b6-5914-b927-f738d0486d86" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id 17592186355052} {:answer/id #uuid "3001b000-bbc1-5019-bdb2-91a649297e13" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id 17592186355081} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8d6739-43a1-5e1a-b5bb-e3e9b2ecf2f5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id 17592186355224} {:answer/id #uuid "a44c5bce-6f34-5a01-a302-4ac4c7c3863a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id 17592186356122} {:answer/id #uuid "a87f58bb-1dd0-55ac-8681-4dd4396992db" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id 17592186356156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a44c5bce-6f34-5a01-a302-4ac4c7c3863a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)" :db/id 17592186356122} :answer-field/id #uuid "0518f0a3-0881-5d1c-ac25-c50f2ca7e9b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186356920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "909b959c-c1db-57e2-bec5-8dd900c2df28" :question/math-academy-id "q-94210" :question/problem "Find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ of the equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-7y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-7y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and sinusoidal forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=\\frac{A}{{e}^{7t}}+B{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-7(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ (-\\alpha +6\\beta -7\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(-\\beta -6\\alpha -7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ (-8\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(-6\\alpha -8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8\\alpha +6\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ -6\\alpha -8\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{25}$ and $\\beta =-\\frac{4}{25},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-\\frac{4}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186357691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35013c7e-161d-57b9-8b70-9180bf4e5f9d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20626954-1dd4-5be2-abfb-91b616517828" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id 17592186354939} {:answer/id #uuid "27790ecd-b58b-5f7a-a86f-73e12ba0059d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id 17592186354998} {:answer/id #uuid "2b1a703d-5443-5822-8c7f-bdf5f9b97570" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id 17592186355027} {:answer/id #uuid "2c59d3b5-9d2c-5b4e-a801-29a147845b38" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id 17592186355042} {:answer/id #uuid "6af718ea-28df-5262-a2c1-7c0d86852384" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id 17592186355611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b1a703d-5443-5822-8c7f-bdf5f9b97570" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id 17592186355027} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1cb8b1a-c554-5363-8ea8-f95b6c4191c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186357300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f527eab-80ea-5448-9f88-66d2121ee8d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-94212" :question/problem "Find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ of the equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Consider using the substitution $x={e}^{t}.$*" :question/worked-solution "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+2y=3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{t}+B{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-3\\alpha +2(\\alpha t+\\beta )=3t\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha t+2\\beta -3\\alpha =3t.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha &=3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ 2\\beta -3\\alpha &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the constants}).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{9}{4},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3t}{2}+\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186357692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35013c7e-161d-57b9-8b70-9180bf4e5f9d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dcb9a18-82de-5ee8-84d6-8e9aa7a186e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1" :db/id 17592186354919} {:answer/id #uuid "a3acc217-4cb8-52b5-8ab6-ee3f82f020df" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2" :db/id 17592186356116} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0357b8-8630-543e-9b50-d8a82d31a117" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2" :db/id 17592186356478} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f39e99-4e67-5b5f-9891-01a7b54383eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1" :db/id 17592186356578} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7a0d5c-694b-5a72-8a6e-267ccdf81003" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+4" :db/id 17592186356902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff7a0d5c-694b-5a72-8a6e-267ccdf81003" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+4" :db/id 17592186356902} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fad42f1-2574-5030-800a-b322e863848e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186357048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13373132-2d6b-54b5-b2be-c3279162518a" :question/math-academy-id "q-94215" :question/problem "Find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ of the equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Consider using the substitution $x={e}^{t}.$*" :question/worked-solution "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & ={{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{product rule}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chain rule}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y=t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing. \n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{t}+Bt{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-2\\alpha +(\\alpha t+\\beta )=t+2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha t+\\beta -2\\alpha =t+2.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha &=1\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ \\beta -2\\alpha &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the constants}).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =1$ and $\\beta =4,$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t+4.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+4.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186362267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186073812 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f88bd4db-8477-5822-9cda-a608e78039e5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Length of a Line Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28d7e1bc-5d38-5dbf-bad2-264d5125d334" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186361016} {:answer/id #uuid "468566b5-2f03-59a0-8fba-5eabe906d457" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id 17592186361141} {:answer/id #uuid "78fd0fdf-3851-5bd0-8b30-f39005a80cb3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186361370} {:answer/id #uuid "98191b6c-4b30-5677-ad67-7b43d32fa55c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id 17592186361513} {:answer/id #uuid "cc39e97d-bf49-51a0-9117-3e75ec5b3d4e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id 17592186361756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "468566b5-2f03-59a0-8fba-5eabe906d457" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id 17592186361141} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a96fbcf-71c2-5c3f-be82-f1e508bcdc0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c79b8781-46c0-50e5-a099-68a40298184b" :question/math-academy-id "q-98106" :question/problem "![](images/11/1108dd3f3d7114f40a483edbbb353fdc51062892d75097fbf22cb7879e4a52cb.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "The length of $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is equal to the distance between $P$ and $Q.$\n\nThe distance $d$ between two points $P({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $Q({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $P$ and $Q$ have coordinates ${{(3,\\,-2,\\,0)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and ${{(2,0,-2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}},$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =\\sqrt{(2-3)^{2}+(0-(-2))^{2}+(-2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186362268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186073813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3ee7d83-2897-53ea-aa8f-05d52cb1f9e7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047e3625-67ed-5d88-a08b-5a04e64a3e58" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id 17592186360848} {:answer/id #uuid "05a4efbf-a625-5202-aca8-f7ebb98410c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id 17592186360853} {:answer/id #uuid "2201c2a1-350f-5aee-bd3d-14e254f13a74" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id 17592186360986} {:answer/id #uuid "3852b93d-4859-51af-905f-296d60e22b92" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186361093} {:answer/id #uuid "748ceedb-fa5f-522e-abca-04887a6b1390" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186361350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "047e3625-67ed-5d88-a08b-5a04e64a3e58" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id 17592186360848} :answer-field/id #uuid "e231751a-b938-582d-87a2-bcb5599c04aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "648fae78-89f1-556f-9670-0284631ad288" :question/math-academy-id "q-98132" :question/problem "What is the distance between the points $A(-5,2,9)$ and $B(-1,-2,9)?$" :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(-5,2,9)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(-1,-2,9)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(-1-(-5))^{2}+(-2-2)^{2}+(9-9)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{32} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186362361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a864a5-90fe-5a19-9ac4-88e0769c548c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186360831} {:answer/id #uuid "65d64f3a-edaf-58da-9deb-f28ef214723d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186361298} {:answer/id #uuid "a12dda97-cff1-5b8e-8a28-789feebf4648" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186361557} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ad5876-370c-5c06-a2d7-3697c868e7f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186361568} {:answer/id #uuid "eaeb134a-5890-53fb-8184-d8779a754096" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186361883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65d64f3a-edaf-58da-9deb-f28ef214723d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186361298} :answer-field/id #uuid "28ad26b4-9c39-5e27-8b07-ab5502f0379b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33cf532b-66bb-5817-8de3-f7bd5092526d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99342" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186362364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a50332-95be-5e07-9d81-8b32183161c2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186360904} {:answer/id #uuid "64fcd74a-108a-502a-aa23-7b7d13b37949" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186361292} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c04a46-697c-5de1-9e78-234a46b8e932" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186361574} {:answer/id #uuid "d85bbaff-0ad6-5bb3-8ba8-be01b5088a6e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186361799} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd2aa3f-818d-5ba1-b96e-fca5d1384108" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186361971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4c04a46-697c-5de1-9e78-234a46b8e932" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186361574} :answer-field/id #uuid "25d1e6ce-4dee-5f38-be32-5ea8a3c4c6c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6127ecb6-de4f-5077-a618-4d1e1bbd51c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-99411" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-3+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=4\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186362366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e8a11c-8df2-522c-9d68-37338e523245" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186360838} {:answer/id #uuid "0b7dadcb-324d-5ae6-83d1-1909ec3c2702" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186360881} {:answer/id #uuid "70ae5295-6ce2-56c7-99ed-046b974429a6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186361335} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b952f4-df0e-54e9-8f81-8a4186f416b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186361595} {:answer/id #uuid "f4dc671e-5c0b-5d36-9b74-34a8b03a45ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186361933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9b952f4-df0e-54e9-8f81-8a4186f416b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186361595} :answer-field/id #uuid "3094debd-288b-541d-8eb3-a03112bc5fc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8dbe8acf-27c1-5738-b187-818bf1052532" :question/math-academy-id "q-99418" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}3+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2-3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=-2+3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3-2\\text{i} \\\\ -2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186362369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0232ac2d-f477-56d2-bc66-4e4e7ff088f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186360839} {:answer/id #uuid "099c80b1-ec77-53c5-9fec-f0d48316573a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186360872} {:answer/id #uuid "554e46a6-951d-57f0-9e37-f12e320d01df" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -1 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186361221} {:answer/id #uuid "63d5d6f1-3790-5286-95bf-cee8cdd5038a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186361290} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5cabc7-c549-5a80-a1d9-086dbf2714f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186361598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa5cabc7-c549-5a80-a1d9-086dbf2714f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186361598} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e410a0f-46a7-599e-a5b7-d7ec1ab97b1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "975ead56-2d77-565d-8ac3-aceac4612c80" :question/math-academy-id "q-99428" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1+6\\text{i} \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1-5\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=1+5\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1-6\\text{i} \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186362539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133882 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf385144-2f48-5874-8c0d-dec31d8fc6e3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5190752e-a1c8-5dd8-b834-158e58825739" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6xy" :db/id 17592186362541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5190752e-a1c8-5dd8-b834-158e58825739" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6xy" :db/id 17592186362541} :answer-field/id #uuid "02bbd186-1d4e-5461-bc79-ce35c2890910" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186362540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "231df40f-8e86-536b-af50-43d6f14cbda6" :question/math-academy-id "q-169203" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation of $F$ along the border $C$ of the plot $D,$ shown in the diagram above.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Circulation $\\,={∮}_{C}^{}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={∬}_{D}\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{d}A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}{{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Scalar curl of }F}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}{{(3x{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,dx+{{(-{y}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{Q}\\,dy\n$$\n\nIn part 2, we found that the scalar curl of $F$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}-6xy\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186362675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e150edfa-4d44-5ab4-b043-ba48e414155f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186468876} {:answer/id #uuid "4e202e38-3b7c-56bb-b632-db0087893be5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186468877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e150edfa-4d44-5ab4-b043-ba48e414155f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186468876} :answer-field/id #uuid "16b1f541-6829-5760-b5cd-2bf997f751ec" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186470050} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daff9470-1403-525c-a778-8cd8a3abbaa3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186468878} {:answer/id #uuid "411c8d9b-b9e8-5e53-9add-722476432317" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186468879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "411c8d9b-b9e8-5e53-9add-722476432317" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186468879} :answer-field/id #uuid "d99a7be7-c36c-521b-a83f-222874be7a98" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186470051} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ae47d9b-d794-5484-a1b3-59518fd10b56" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186468880} {:answer/id #uuid "52e13f5f-43af-5535-afd2-29e2add95b4c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186468881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ae47d9b-d794-5484-a1b3-59518fd10b56" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186468880} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d70e912-f00f-557a-98ae-ac4eb7d47693" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186470052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92a6911e-532d-5f89-96e6-761d0ceb55f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109487" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id 17592186362682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aedcb32b-f84c-5aa2-b3a4-f7e83746b836" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186468882} {:answer/id #uuid "aed73398-59c2-51b4-9371-f0f65e98014f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186468883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aedcb32b-f84c-5aa2-b3a4-f7e83746b836" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186468882} :answer-field/id #uuid "9958dde5-b255-5d95-ae9b-baca80f743d5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186470053} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3915fd0e-f480-5ebd-983a-af8001f5e607" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186468884} {:answer/id #uuid "23790615-5ac5-5c45-97e2-001e01eff0a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186468885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3915fd0e-f480-5ebd-983a-af8001f5e607" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186468884} :answer-field/id #uuid "03f1a276-3cb9-581b-9099-1990fdc1c513" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186470054} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a54f6205-db26-5c00-8932-ebcca124f521" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186468886} {:answer/id #uuid "40b5715b-352e-56b1-a124-d548af347d96" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186468887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a54f6205-db26-5c00-8932-ebcca124f521" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186468886} :answer-field/id #uuid "1659548f-da18-5fc1-9233-0906388e6725" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186470055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "83c9089c-246d-51fb-b493-4538acc22376" :question/math-academy-id "q-109574" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+I:$\n\n$$\nA+I=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A+I:$\n\n$$\n(A+I)x=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} {:db/id 17592186362750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e1da77-a5dc-5f78-977e-0d6dae99c97e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Images of the Four Fundamental Subspaces of the Matrix Under Its Action"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ed2ea04-17c5-525d-bb8b-a73bdb48fa90" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Null}({B}^{T})" :db/id 17592186362752} {:answer/id #uuid "81d7a34a-2311-5d45-891e-5cd69b89006d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}(B)" :db/id 17592186362753} {:answer/id #uuid "1ed9ddc3-ffd8-53ba-802f-ad779bcf8c3e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}({B}^{T})" :db/id 17592186362754} {:answer/id #uuid "7b88adee-5c88-5a21-99be-baee1cf260db" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186362755} {:answer/id #uuid "7aed3ab2-0476-5b02-8570-9d68a6779e1a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Null}(B)" :db/id 17592186362756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81d7a34a-2311-5d45-891e-5cd69b89006d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}(B)" :db/id 17592186362753} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b3fad67-e441-547e-bcca-af1012111f8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "63c0c3aa-6cd0-5913-9f45-9198721d3066" :question/math-academy-id "q-109850" :question/problem "Let $B$ be a $m\\times n$ matrix. What is the image of $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ under the action of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "Pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ by $B$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $B:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{B}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {b}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+⋯+{x}_{n}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {b}_{n} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{n}$ under the action of $B$ must be $\\text{Col}(B),$ the column space of $B.$\n\nFurthermore, since $\\text{Null}(B)$ is mapped to the zero vector $0$ by the definition of the null subspace, the image of its orthogonal complement $\\text{Col}({B}^{T})$ must be $\\text{Col}(B).$\n\nThis can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ad/ad1557fbd48206baf7239046ada69f60d8cd9ee4916b4f8e37741903ef7d1e9b.png)"} {:db/id 17592186362770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e1da77-a5dc-5f78-977e-0d6dae99c97e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Images of the Four Fundamental Subspaces of the Matrix Under Its Action"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab280a44-737b-5327-b8b9-37dc6ef7b3be" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{6}" :db/id 17592186362772} {:answer/id #uuid "d5d452ec-369b-54d1-8357-79ad02025a07" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{4}" :db/id 17592186362773} {:answer/id #uuid "99ca8573-a2e6-558c-b3c7-031132e63ac3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Null}({A}^{T})" :db/id 17592186362774} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e2aca3-e8c2-5d00-b0bc-0cf40e3489e1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}({A}^{T})" :db/id 17592186362775} {:answer/id #uuid "887d2b50-662a-542d-9779-a9834c4a9b0e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186362776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3e2aca3-e8c2-5d00-b0bc-0cf40e3489e1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}({A}^{T})" :db/id 17592186362775} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb6ed2ff-8856-5c19-ae37-a59672f92f3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186362771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f5df99f-1d28-5975-9513-4422bec5dfb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-109860" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $4\\times 6$ matrix. What is the image of ${R}^{4}$ under the action of ${A}^{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${A}^{T}$ is a $6\\times 4$ matrix.\n\nPre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ by ${A}^{T}$ gives a linear combination of the columns of ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} & {a}_{2}^{T} & {a}_{3}^{T} & {a}_{4}^{T} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{T}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{2}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{3}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{4}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ {a}_{4}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{4}$ under the action of ${A}^{T}$ must be $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}),$ the row space of $A.$"} {:db/id 17592186363161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d177b305-cdd1-5f91-b9d5-90623e52ad10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02554daa-311a-521d-8bb7-985c7d0c82c7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186363163} {:answer/id #uuid "00588159-608b-585c-b2b5-2b836308f12c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186363164} {:answer/id #uuid "3a499beb-4e4c-5adf-9db1-91ff32f20121" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186363165} {:answer/id #uuid "efdc2092-f8a4-5b77-a120-3add5597c886" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186363166} {:answer/id #uuid "974a9e55-1080-5bb8-aaab-2072bf03f674" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186363167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02554daa-311a-521d-8bb7-985c7d0c82c7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186363163} :answer-field/id #uuid "846e397b-48d4-5641-a9ae-b50762794665" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186363162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bd77d9db-96bd-55f7-b4d3-fb162ec30ec6" :question/math-academy-id "q-139786" :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2 & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -4\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=20 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2} &=-4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-(-2) & =20 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =18 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"} {:db/id 17592186363168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d177b305-cdd1-5f91-b9d5-90623e52ad10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb9d78f4-aa3e-5f6c-9e00-cb88d8c0a1a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186363170} {:answer/id #uuid "61095090-5603-5eb4-acb6-44d1a726d375" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186363171} {:answer/id #uuid "54e79a64-d102-5ec7-92fb-704283cbd246" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186363172} {:answer/id #uuid "d091dad6-c30c-5c5a-93cb-462966115e3d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186363173} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5d8ca3-4cb5-5f45-9f6b-8f05c6c59c66" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186363174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb9d78f4-aa3e-5f6c-9e00-cb88d8c0a1a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186363170} :answer-field/id #uuid "67e03da2-3c94-5901-94b3-848cec26cc95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186363169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1989d3b4-7a5e-5b18-8a4f-6c042b9559dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-139795" :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ -\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} & 0\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}15\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 6\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2} &=15\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2} &=6\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-3\\sqrt{2}(3) & =15\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1} & =24\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"} {:db/id 17592186363217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce45e94d-baf5-5098-ac2d-d794c6429bb9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id 17592186363219} {:answer/id #uuid "448670c4-632d-5013-a236-b59dc6cbcc16" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186363220} {:answer/id #uuid "cb4bc888-dff7-59f0-a773-62a45b1023fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186363221} {:answer/id #uuid "ccaa65a2-e9e6-5cda-b206-709db184a16e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186363222} {:answer/id #uuid "c20f970a-bd81-5297-9fcb-5e40ddbce489" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id 17592186363223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb4bc888-dff7-59f0-a773-62a45b1023fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186363221} :answer-field/id #uuid "4547ff35-0e1d-5d8f-b076-6bc2886e7c0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186363218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d67166f-3bff-552c-8a7c-19cfbf3fd7f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-140900" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{3}+2{q}_{1}-{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{3}+2{q}_{1}-{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=-2{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=-6{q}_{1}+3{q}_{2}+3{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}=-6+2+3=-1.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186363224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a027607c-b49e-5d51-8e9f-696efe340642" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id 17592186363226} {:answer/id #uuid "66dfc0c2-4c41-5b5c-8ca9-8963c9494355" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186363227} {:answer/id #uuid "92170d95-d6d3-5882-96d9-9f06a90067ee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186363228} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8b6e55-0d08-5b04-9194-57e762fe0902" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186363229} {:answer/id #uuid "25a800e8-0c9b-5cbf-9949-6d4a63eb0a1c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186363230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66dfc0c2-4c41-5b5c-8ca9-8963c9494355" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186363227} :answer-field/id #uuid "48f82e91-6709-5adc-99bd-29df5cbf4521" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186363225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "836a6cda-4c5e-5dc1-9726-f5d184348fd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-140902" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=2.$"} {:db/id 17592186363231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4f9e469-7eec-5401-8986-a4974df572b5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id 17592186363233} {:answer/id #uuid "bbffa08b-7606-549f-bc87-1f500316ebd0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id 17592186363234} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ab06c1-b10b-5935-bf2d-75749c31b94d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id 17592186363235} {:answer/id #uuid "85bd57ed-0fc2-5736-8094-14f443d99ae9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id 17592186363236} {:answer/id #uuid "c5eaeb1b-839d-5f69-b76b-0cdf9c511c2e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id 17592186363237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85bd57ed-0fc2-5736-8094-14f443d99ae9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id 17592186363236} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8043045-7145-51b3-822c-f2d744e6043c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186363232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95a58504-d1c2-5301-9dd6-5bea44e00bd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-140903" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{22}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{32}\\cdot {r}_{22}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}=-2.$"} {:db/id 17592186364171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94bbd4be-9e16-5ff7-86f5-9deb90ba452b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id 17592186364173} {:answer/id #uuid "c291dfba-6379-5676-a6b4-e3cf0f6b1b78" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id 17592186364174} {:answer/id #uuid "99202a3d-788d-55c3-8504-be24ed646fdc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id 17592186364175} {:answer/id #uuid "baadede6-09ca-5ce7-809c-fce926ea02ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id 17592186364176} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f1a527-b1dc-5cc6-a3e9-53c8168ec278" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id 17592186364177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99202a3d-788d-55c3-8504-be24ed646fdc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id 17592186364175} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4e06997-ff6b-5818-9b5e-fa3e71c896a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186364172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0bac4d5-3752-5a5d-b6fe-39c8ef101bd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-41485" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=3,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=4,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=2,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-3{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-5{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{3}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}=3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{4}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=4{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=2{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set ${{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} &={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} &={a}_{2}-3{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3} &={a}_{3}-5{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &={v}_{1}=3{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=3{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=9{q}_{1}+4{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=5{v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=15{q}_{1}+8{q}_{2}+2{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{33}=15+4+2=21.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186364178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74849d77-3f75-57fb-9eda-c7eb146ef089" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id 17592186364180} {:answer/id #uuid "ae17bbad-6406-590f-9037-515c2d2e3302" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id 17592186364181} {:answer/id #uuid "80193887-56ef-5f55-9899-81a4ca2a5bfa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id 17592186364182} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ed3821-6db7-500c-afa6-af890beb1af8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186364183} {:answer/id #uuid "ee368a24-e35e-5530-bc4c-c0b11699a84f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186364184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80193887-56ef-5f55-9899-81a4ca2a5bfa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id 17592186364182} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfd9129a-1c55-5b4e-92c4-8f9a2d89199f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186364179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0711fb88-caa5-5f45-81df-dbacaf0d183c" :question/math-academy-id "q-41486" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+8{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{3}-3{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+8{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{3}-3{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &=6{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=-16{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=9{q}_{1}-6{q}_{2}+3{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -16 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{33}=9+2+3=14.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186364599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6541fae-ca98-5011-918c-a2e113b5f4a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id 17592186364601} {:answer/id #uuid "bf8150ed-2bbb-58ff-9b44-a56ebd9adcf2" :answer/type {:db/id 1759