-598ddb002aea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186370731} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a03f8a6-cfa6-5f4a-a2c7-03d61bf02197" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{3}" :db/id 17592186370737} {:answer/id #uuid "1184cd13-1b7d-50c5-8776-0df641d5a57c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}" :db/id 17592186370738} {:answer/id #uuid "8f83a2a3-0c4b-5b74-a2ae-40de45652ace" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}_{3}" :db/id 17592186370739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f83a2a3-0c4b-5b74-a2ae-40de45652ace" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}_{3}" :db/id 17592186370739} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba206edd-58a0-5ed4-ba95-982b0a2c35ba" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186370736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5a19d61-895b-51bb-abe5-e33bbbd340f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-363836" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{10}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | ${x}_{3}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n| | ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |\n|  | {{field-7}} |  |  |  | | | ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-8}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}$ |  |  |  |\n|  | {{field-9}} |  |  |  | |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ |  |  |\n|  | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |\n|  | {{field-7}} |  |  |  |\n| ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-8}} |  |  |\n|  | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}$ |  |  |  |\n|  | {{field-9}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{10}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186370740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162045 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "566bda0f-f62d-581d-936d-baa8ae474e50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix: Cases Requiring Orthogonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f74aec7-8abe-5cb4-aef9-610ac59ad5e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id 17592186370742} {:answer/id #uuid "bddd798e-f9eb-52af-894c-1535f9fc9ff6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id 17592186370743} {:answer/id #uuid "26467df6-1671-56fb-9dcc-328835e089a0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id 17592186370744} {:answer/id #uuid "e2187134-218f-5990-b053-e8207628cb34" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id 17592186370745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2187134-218f-5990-b053-e8207628cb34" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id 17592186370745} :answer-field/id #uuid "a22726aa-562f-5f3f-81dc-3afdd76e124b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186370741} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b11a6ee-36d3-50d0-96a0-e25b67a0a5a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id 17592186370747} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb23c3e-67c9-5ed2-81fd-3a313a62abbb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id 17592186370748} {:answer/id #uuid "e29c893b-cda6-58f4-9970-09a2b7542588" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id 17592186370749} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a6fc87-def5-5da8-b6f0-ee1ff74