$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$8$** | **$12$** | **$17$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$1$** | **$3$** | **$6$** | **$10$** | **$15$** |\n| $y$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n\n \n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 10 \\\\ 5 & 17 & 15\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186373883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e33c97-0499-5829-9886-be30b00989ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8c86c13-a427-59a3-946f-d3068d919dd3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186373885} {:answer/id #uuid "54b286e1-c405-5b21-9223-66984efb053a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186373886} {:answer/id #uuid "d42c4d75-4ef4-5816-92a8-830d464a623e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 12 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 16 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 20 & 11 \\\\ 5 & 23 & 14\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186373887} {:answer/id #uuid "69f76b52-75e9-55d0-8c86-7b2109743f31" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 12 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 16 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 20 & 11 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 23 & 14\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186373888} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc00856-9323-5cf8-ac0f-252011e8f888" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 12 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 16 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 20 & 11 \\\\ 8 & 5 & 23 & 14\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186373889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69f76b52-75e9-55d0-8c86-7b2109743f31" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 12 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 16 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 20 & 11 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 23 & 14\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186373888} :answer-field/id #uuid "cae51f65-4154-520b-a9a6-7c108db09fe0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186373884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fffe5652-3444-59b4-879a-345eb3040a45" :question/math-academy-id "q-159726" :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$9$** | **$12$** | **$16$** | **$20$** | **$23$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$1$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$11$** | **$14$** |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $8$ | $12$ | $14$ | $18$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$9$** | **$12$** | **$16$** | **$20$** | **$23$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$1$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$11$** | **$14$** |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $8$ | $12$ | $14$ | $18$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane  \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 12 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 16 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 20 & 11 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 23 & 14\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186373972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198379 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaa6b152-86d3-5f96-8a94-57f86a17a84d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Flux Over a Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35f2e086-31b9-544a-9b9a-99a08a27f04e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id 17592186373974} {:answer/id #uuid "291af077-cf81-5322-8799-9c4d0a56816f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id 17592186373975} {:answer/id #uuid "84fcb1f0-4936-5fbf-afb7-a71a059f4960" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186373976} {:answer/id #uuid "c8544813-339e-5ed8-a745-c0b3a602e975" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186373977} {:answer/id #uuid "c730413b-a101-500a-a942-8a345613f8ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186373978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "291af077-cf81-5322-8799-9c4d0a56816f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id 17592186373975} :answer-field/id #uuid "05d45f19-fa05-53a9-92e1-77a6078e07fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186373973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a86f1df-9194-5ee6-ac19-2bfbeca02663" :question/math-academy-id "q-161447" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,z+2x⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=y-2x$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-2x)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,z+2x⟩{|}_{z=y-2x} \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,x+(y-2x),\\,y-2x+2x⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{y-x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{y}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,z+2x⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (-2)-(y-x)\\cdot (1)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y-y+x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2y+x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[{y}^{2}+xy]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(1+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2+2-1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186374931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147487 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e92641d-b0bf-579b-960b-d6024d4b99b7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a VOP Equation by Substituting the Ansatz into the Original ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4792a4b-2e9a-5f29-bbb4-2f8c8f8e0323" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}={g}^{′}(x)" :db/id 17592186374933} {:answer/id #uuid "dc08249d-d196-55f5-98d4-e65ff35ddd92" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=g(x)" :db/id 17592186374934} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2eb33e-f29a-53ad-81d9-774060a57c5e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}={g}^{′}(x)" :db/id 17592186374935} {:answer/id #uuid "ef701104-0e38-51f8-bd19-76fc5e9409b7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=g(x)" :db/id 17592186374936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc08249d-d196-55f5-98d4-e65ff35ddd92" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=g(x)" :db/id 17592186374934} :answer-field/id #uuid "2daf61ca-2864-5046-9a57-41a4be211613" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186374932} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbf02e4e-5e71-5569-923d-cf704b948e8b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id 17592186374938} {:answer/id #uuid "124f89fa-5f05-54fe-8d9c-801391524917" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id 17592186374939} {:answer/id #uuid "94dedf04-0af3-5ebe-9896-ad9c7aed08cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-3x})^{′}" :db/id 17592186374940} {:answer/id #uuid "aa087f5f-60f4-55f9-904c-44d0ec5f3753" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{-3x})^{′}" :db/id 17592186374941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "124f89fa-5f05-54fe-8d9c-801391524917" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id 17592186374939} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb40b144-4943-51a4-bfb8-4f0136ae2fde" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186374937} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f4ed9f7-83d2-57e4-9946-9f08c5ba8a55" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id 17592186374943} {:answer/id #uuid "80649e7c-ea15-5bb0-b71b-2016f608b537" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id 17592186374944} {:answer/id #uuid "ab306f37-0281-5596-857b-4e0d1ad46360" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-3x})^{′}" :db/id 17592186374945} {:answer/id #uuid "5883d48f-dcc8-5819-85c8-313c3cbeb50c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{-3x})^{′}" :db/id 17592186374946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab306f37-0281-5596-857b-4e0d1ad46360" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-3x})^{′}" :db/id 17592186374945} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff27260b-9fcc-5168-8c3c-fbea49824fda" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186374942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f89bf15e-170f-57b4-987e-95e2136f7222" :question/math-academy-id "q-337751" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-3y=g(x).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}:$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y={y}_{p}$ and ${y}^{′}={y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between ${u}_{1}^{′},{u}_{2}^{′},$ and $g(x):$\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\n$$\n\nHere, ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-3x})^{′}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y={y}_{p}$ and ${y}^{′}={y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-3y & =g(x) \\\\ ({y}_{p}^{′})^{′}+2{y}_{p}^{′}-3{y}_{p} & =g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our expressions for ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-3x})^{′})}^{′}+2({u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-3x})^{′})-3({u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}) & =g(x) \\\\ {({u}_{1}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x})}^{′}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}-3{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x} & =g(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+9{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}-3{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x} & =g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group all terms proportional to ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}{{({e}^{x}+2{e}^{x}-3{e}^{x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{2}{{(9{e}^{-3x}-6{e}^{-3x}-3{e}^{-3x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the expressions in parentheses are both zero. Therefore, the equation reduces to\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=g(x).\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186375659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa9fbb8-b2bc-5c93-bb07-bc8c7ef7c115" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution of an Intermediate System Using the LU Factorization of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f15ae35b-caa0-5046-aede-97653a8e60b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 13 \\\\ -41\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186375661} {:answer/id #uuid "10c1ca60-0681-50e2-b5ae-c2e8ab738004" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -48\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186375662} {:answer/id #uuid "a42f494b-6dcc-50bf-9247-0e4973fed546" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 13 \\\\ -53\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186375663} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac99b1d-b6c1-53af-aff9-40ffda63e8fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 17 \\\\ -41\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186375664} {:answer/id #uuid "e17aa4c8-d7ce-582c-8862-abcf25cd4ea8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 15 \\\\ -35\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186375665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10c1ca60-0681-50e2-b5ae-c2e8ab738004" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -48\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186375662} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ef7605f-f350-5544-965c-ff2ab358ba12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186375660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f222a20b-2bd2-5f36-87f7-762e6e1190b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-105790" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=5 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 7{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =5, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =2+2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =2+2(5) \\\\  & =12, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =-1-7{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-1-7(5)-(12) \\\\  & =-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -48\\end{bmatrix}.$"} {:db/id 17592186375694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0152189d-69e9-550a-aa74-d823899ff99c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id 17592186375696} {:answer/id #uuid "7339e9e1-c70f-5d2c-8de4-9188d1f4ee9d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id 17592186375697} {:answer/id #uuid "e1168d16-8053-595d-9565-1791421a6583" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id 17592186375698} {:answer/id #uuid "0004ccd6-0cb7-5f4b-835c-3676eacd47a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26" :db/id 17592186375699} {:answer/id #uuid "2bae85db-f4f9-5bea-95a2-d65c8771db89" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-22" :db/id 17592186375700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1168d16-8053-595d-9565-1791421a6583" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id 17592186375698} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea4b0ad7-9760-505e-a4ce-93c955f11853" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186375695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0fd7a569-3ee6-5b8c-8dc0-3a8c7fdaf1b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-106759" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -9 & -16\\end{bmatrix}$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -9 & -16\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+9{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-9,{u}_{12}=2,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=-18.$"} {:db/id 17592186375701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4ac8989-70de-5a59-8927-a8191c1b28f4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186375703} {:answer/id #uuid "2a988cae-f39c-52de-af03-1e261ea4534c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id 17592186375704} {:answer/id #uuid "aebb0087-f097-5d22-bc5b-51a65efc1f1e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186375705} {:answer/id #uuid "fd781605-6d78-5c74-a071-c48559c58cf2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186375706} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff4b7e2-bd8e-5b9d-be90-7b9784dc2be7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id 17592186375707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aebb0087-f097-5d22-bc5b-51a65efc1f1e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186375705} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5d9e958-0607-5eef-abc1-a1fc5cfc1f7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186375702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b38dc247-b2e9-5a16-96c0-79d622a0c053" :question/math-academy-id "q-106768" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 12 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 12 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=4,$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=4.$"} {:db/id 17592186375783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186100850 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f41b2bd-0c1d-5267-b91d-7aba35ea7efd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e664ad24-8ca1-511a-80f7-5e76f72ca253" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id 17592186375785} {:answer/id #uuid "34d8d4ed-90c4-5bcd-9737-a72ba54a6e72" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id 17592186375786} {:answer/id #uuid "215877d4-b476-5844-9820-67baef24ec61" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{2}" :db/id 17592186375787} {:answer/id #uuid "b15031f2-ce95-5dfb-93c3-0f0a22e2d42f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id 17592186375788} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b216b6-694f-5587-92f4-a6a6f426e567" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id 17592186375789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "215877d4-b476-5844-9820-67baef24ec61" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{2}" :db/id 17592186375787} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba4790c5-228d-5a10-8a33-e98cd44849fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186375784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1fa6a739-b482-5f96-a80d-3d661a2b2ff2" :question/math-academy-id "q-117644" :question/problem "![](images/ee/ee7e86838a15a0a5c223beaca7e02628123937afb921408d602ad135fbdc0584.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,2]$ and the line segment for $x\\in [2,5].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,5].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =5+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186375790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186078598 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cd9df66-421a-5f07-bf23-4a6ad42b8ce6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id 17592186375792} {:answer/id #uuid "b1023bbb-194a-5d8e-aba1-bcbc3ff2acfb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186375793} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d842a8-cba7-55bf-8397-53b66f65094c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id 17592186375794} {:answer/id #uuid "a9bef28a-6d49-59b6-9ec8-eb7233a81007" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id 17592186375795} {:answer/id #uuid "acb83df3-1fba-5116-ad8e-c796663c4ae1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186375796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9bef28a-6d49-59b6-9ec8-eb7233a81007" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id 17592186375795} :answer-field/id #uuid "90b0d283-2886-5977-9bb3-c8d7120e94e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186375791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b27fcc87-93b0-5936-a3e8-30ac2641ea7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-117711" :question/problem "![](images/6f/6f1cd478f596aa904dc9b1e254c2eb2873b264935cc11b2c3480fc3560d08d55.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We divide the interval of integration into $4$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-3),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-3,-2],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-2,1],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-3).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-1.5.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,-2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,1].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+1)(3) \\\\  & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,4].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{4}=-6.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}-{A}_{4} \\\\  & =-1.5+1.5+7.5-6 \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186375797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186078599 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "094e0fc9-bdff-5e24-b715-f7c14bc5dde8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id 17592186375799} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2b94a5-58c6-586b-a02c-019dbfdbc403" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id 17592186375800} {:answer/id #uuid "bce65182-c725-57e6-9a15-bcf43f1379e0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6+\\pi " :db/id 17592186375801} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c5b058-6eca-58f7-8eaf-0b48065d76b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+\\pi " :db/id 17592186375802} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f43032-d6bf-5bfa-a617-e942203c003f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6+2\\pi " :db/id 17592186375803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8f43032-d6bf-5bfa-a617-e942203c003f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6+2\\pi " :db/id 17592186375803} :answer-field/id #uuid "b76a6639-351e-5331-ba79-8b07d8f59787" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186375798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ecd0f3e-3a2e-5e85-81c3-9438923337bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-117981" :question/problem "![](images/52/5203ef7c7c2bd96ff42d3388682fcb1b2c8fd57697c6d1185937eeef74fde209.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,1)$ and the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in (1,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,1).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,5].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =6+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186375979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186100851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75f1ac34-ae96-53af-b8ae-d465af28f75d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Nonlinear Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7419bfed-a2af-55fa-8052-9352fd0bced0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\pi " :db/id 17592186375981} {:answer/id #uuid "987b00a1-c223-5380-8f96-007de6ba9f86" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+\\pi " :db/id 17592186375982} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb0d65d-25f4-5e04-9817-b8718b99cd38" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id 17592186375983} {:answer/id #uuid "b9203aac-49c8-5228-bfbf-a3f92c253b34" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id 17592186375984} {:answer/id #uuid "f369676f-91b1-5fcc-a14a-9c0e76b74d8e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id 17592186375985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f369676f-91b1-5fcc-a14a-9c0e76b74d8e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id 17592186375985} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5eb3575-092a-56fe-a57b-60125786a215" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186375980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4600d16d-6c96-5d04-80e6-8eca78510bd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-14607" :question/problem "![](images/33/331abc8bb1e445dd27a8d888872d3292786f0d9bf8d9e0e258cdd15f041afba4.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of a line segment and an arc of a semi-circle centered at $(4,0)$ with radius $1.$ Find the value of \n${\\int }_{-1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-1,3]$ and the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [3,5].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,3].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,5].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =5+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186376000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186100851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75f1ac34-ae96-53af-b8ae-d465af28f75d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Nonlinear Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ee44ca8-aeba-5390-b2e6-351810b88c0d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}+2\\pi " :db/id 17592186376002} {:answer/id #uuid "4acb367e-9e8b-514d-ab46-5986a827f3b9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+\\pi " :db/id 17592186376003} {:answer/id #uuid "edae3abb-a831-54c6-8eeb-1a37c2dd5097" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+2\\pi " :db/id 17592186376004} {:answer/id #uuid "ba222052-c3c8-55d3-91b4-a44f89be583d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/id 17592186376005} {:answer/id #uuid "ed7aed60-5627-592d-a221-9d6549c4d03f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}" :db/id 17592186376006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edae3abb-a831-54c6-8eeb-1a37c2dd5097" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+2\\pi " :db/id 17592186376004} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9567c7d-c882-5493-a6d3-a199ce93f839" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186376001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fc1ba0c-3eb8-5ea4-ae3c-9f6bf9148071" :question/math-academy-id "q-14781" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6fda8f25a1b25a0cbb128e2b5f1bf391d3a49d77ce81175ed85e362d8d89d5d.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments, an arc of a quarter-circle centered at $(0,0)$ with radius $1$, and an arc of a quarter-circle centered at $(3,0)$ with radius $3.$ Find the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $4$ parts: the line segment for  $x\\in [-2,0]$,  the arc of a quarter-circle for $x\\in [0,1]$, the line segment for  $x\\in [1,3]$, and  the arc of a quarter-circle for $x\\in [3,6].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,1].$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,6].$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+3+\\frac{9\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =2+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186376021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064321 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c7b271b-dd60-5a95-9dcf-9ff45d54f327" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id 17592186376023} {:answer/id #uuid "bf643b5d-aca2-5cd4-841c-3f655593a72c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id 17592186376024} {:answer/id #uuid "0e1138db-ea0a-55f9-9807-c20a4ab56d92" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id 17592186376025} {:answer/id #uuid "9906a4c6-8bcc-5069-a654-1bc4fa81b26f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id 17592186376026} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac4eacd-b561-52af-a332-5511c03a946f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186376027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e1138db-ea0a-55f9-9807-c20a4ab56d92" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id 17592186376025} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4c8c7f4-110a-5ca7-b2cf-923b8df55f72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186376022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77e6bc60-fb00-5a58-8c6e-b928f85cd98a" :question/math-academy-id "q-155954" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1c42152de7cf176fa122ddf48aad7d93fe982ec1bc2717e3048d9ff5ea45f302.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{1}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(6).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=6,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(6)={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/14/145d9c16e27c67876d1ae2a00dce15b3cb7437f6a9121971fe4434e6c1fe943c.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [1,2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [2,6],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(6)={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [1,2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (1+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [2,6],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(6)={\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}+4=\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186376028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186064321 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3486c54-da95-531b-8b53-26b12acef4be" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id 17592186376030} {:answer/id #uuid "ec0d466f-06cc-5399-aa49-83f879252fed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id 17592186376031} {:answer/id #uuid "4f872bc9-6bfc-5fc9-89e0-cfae2c57617d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186376032} {:answer/id #uuid "af2265dc-94b0-529d-a040-684ebd2ed337" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{2}" :db/id 17592186376033} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd3c6a1-4665-5022-925f-5aadc0c7b6c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id 17592186376034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f872bc9-6bfc-5fc9-89e0-cfae2c57617d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186376032} :answer-field/id #uuid "10d0807a-2d70-5f5a-a226-1c7e5c987a18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186376029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "827ecb6b-b2a5-50bb-bb63-7dac8fe852fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-155955" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec8492d2d545c54f8af112d2221c09eeb0201ca9cdc199d1c6b2cab768c282ea.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-4}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(1).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=1,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(1)={\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab9a16779fc4b2d4578df9bbb74c3df34c958bdb2bdd25d5db30910da7f1d9d6.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-4,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [0,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a rectangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(1)={\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-4,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot (1+2) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [0,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the rectangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1)={\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=6+2=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186376514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02a1ebb1-37f3-5305-99b5-cf6ddc62e037" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Parity of a Nonlinear Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e33f66d1-bb0f-5f9b-b610-c8a6d2c6b464" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "even" :db/id 17592186376516} {:answer/id #uuid "27922783-4885-5f1f-8a8c-d505b39e4e99" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "odd" :db/id 17592186376517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27922783-4885-5f1f-8a8c-d505b39e4e99" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "odd" :db/id 17592186376517} :answer-field/id #uuid "f451b86a-0266-5bde-ad24-f61785db8f02" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186376515} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid