-field/id #uuid "26f6dddc-207b-5ada-a853-8f975e7c1162" :answer-field/key "field-9" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186382018} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93229c20-24ce-5d7e-bcb8-de40cff8311c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id 17592186382024} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1a7775-fa89-5a70-99d7-50a32a2340da" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186382025} {:answer/id #uuid "df827696-cccc-524f-8dd7-9a486d655d35" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id 17592186382026} {:answer/id #uuid "57eef191-29b6-557c-bb0d-c552c0ee5cd4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186382027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57eef191-29b6-557c-bb0d-c552c0ee5cd4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186382027} :answer-field/id #uuid "82392c3a-f0f6-5951-a926-f66c0787e9b1" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186382023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d1f0a35-56cb-5370-99fa-b599f7ccb0a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-363840" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | $\\frac{3}{5}$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | ${x}_{3}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n| | ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-7}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $0$ |  |  |  | | | ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-8}} |  |  |  |\n|  | {{field-9}} |  |  |  | |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-7}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $0$ |  |  |  |\n| ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ |  |  |\n|  | {{field-8}} |  |  |  |\n|  | {{field-9}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{1}+\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186382559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dc9412d-db1d-5f0b-bf46-1547fc9bc9d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186382561} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ab9fcd-0d01-5a7c-a562-b332d1fa753b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186382562} {:answer/id #uuid "7a397f58-508e-5d63-aeae-854fad3a6043" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186382563} {:answer/id #uuid "2756ce9d-d692-57b4-88bb-5f748720577f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186382564} {:answer/id #uuid "3f5b1550-1387-5406-a483-f580bc72d844" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186382565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a397f58-508e-5d63-aeae-854fad3a6043" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186382563} :answer-field/id #uuid "38cb7faf-1c5d-5786-81e1-1833c5631b56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186382560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbf886ad-eced-5102-99df-0e48cd09970a" :question/math-academy-id "q-85905" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=8 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3} &=-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}36 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 36 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 36\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -36 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186382566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e98d845-002f-583e-8e2e-c38bbe7de8f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id 17592186382568} {:answer/id #uuid "7786ed39-2dbb-5f7d-bf08-6bec28171c31" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{26}" :db/id 17592186382569} {:answer/id #uuid "cb964b5b-bac3-57a5-8203-f9e1ae78d687" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id 17592186382570} {:answer/id #uuid "c739a3f6-5a92-5370-933d-3523a77208ee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{26}" :db/id 17592186382571} {:answer/id #uuid "47cab409-32b1-5e78-9160-cf977247036e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id 17592186382572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c739a3f6-5a92-5370-933d-3523a77208ee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{26}" :db/id 17592186382571} :answer-field/id #uuid "239e5903-65ea-585b-898e-25c3e72e9d67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186382567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81030c04-8049-5124-9acd-0f1fecb2eec0" :question/math-academy-id "q-85910" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are shown below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=2\\sqrt{26}.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186382573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Vector to a Subspace of Solutions of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db34a3a7-82db-516e-9b5f-f74695de47f9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{19}" :db/id 17592186382575} {:answer/id #uuid "231e106c-0303-5b69-9237-341c2dc82244" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{17}" :db/id 17592186382576} {:answer/id #uuid "b1264692-a197-5024-84dd-8409773b4cef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{6}" :db/id 17592186382577} {:answer/id #uuid "63c436ce-c5cd-5684-9714-4a097df7fdbc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{19}" :db/id 17592186382578} {:answer/id #uuid "4dce82c7-9228-540c-b08c-9cc8383746e2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}" :db/id 17592186382579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db34a3a7-82db-516e-9b5f-f74695de47f9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{19}" :db/id 17592186382575} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f3514e1-3487-5717-8374-c6a14d4839ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186382574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7ed86ff9-5c1b-5f36-88cb-573447ebe9b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-85919" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are shown below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\hat{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\hat{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\hat{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\hat{x}-b & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\hat{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+(-9)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-8)^{2}}=3\\sqrt{19}.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186383568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129282 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82be73-0720-5267-8698-427641b0e66e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Area Scale Factor of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f94640a-439d-5450-b89c-214d6351fa55" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\delta A" :db/id 17592186383570} {:answer/id #uuid "22b7af57-2308-539f-b4dc-7bf734738b88" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\delta A" :db/id 17592186383571} {:answer/id #uuid "305c7067-b121-52de-832b-2cd582abe378" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\delta A" :db/id 17592186383572} {:answer/id #uuid "0e57c966-7e8f-5c20-b14e-3fff17deba68" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\delta A" :db/id 17592186383573} {:answer/id #uuid "c14d8db9-1f54-501a-a0d4-dbdd47484709" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\delta A" :db/id 17592186383574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "305c7067-b121-52de-832b-2cd582abe378" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\delta A" :db/id 17592186383572} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fd0ed70-8ad1-5612-8701-9b382b52743e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186383569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f0941ca-edd5-5538-8a0c-b3d25f8e41f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161877" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(2,1)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u-v) \\\\ v-{u}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{u-v} & -\\frac{1}{u-v} \\\\ -2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u-v}\\cdot 1-(-2u)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{u-v}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2u}{u-v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,1)} & ={(\\frac{1-2u}{u-v})\\,|}_{u=2\\,v=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2(2)}{2-1} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-3|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =3\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186383575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129282 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82be73-0720-5267-8698-427641b0e66e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Area Scale Factor of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61164a03-d653-5509-8896-92a9f5d833f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\delta A" :db/id 17592186383577} {:answer/id #uuid "5766d69f-6242-5cfa-96ee-c8cd587905ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\delta A" :db/id 17592186383578} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec2c14a-52b6-5478-a60f-1f678392ec7a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\delta A" :db/id 17592186383579} {:answer/id #uuid "5317cbac-448e-539f-b6c8-0a7df3081b44" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\delta A" :db/id 17592186383580} {:answer/id #uuid "18259f51-378f-592e-be8d-c8109a57fd90" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\delta A" :db/id 17592186383581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5766d69f-6242-5cfa-96ee-c8cd587905ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\delta A" :db/id 17592186383578} :answer-field/id #uuid "c852d323-c95a-5a6d-9333-8d677385a559" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186383576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a2efb4f-04d0-536e-850b-89df0bdf4245" :question/math-academy-id "q-161878" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-3,3)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+{v}^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2v \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\cdot 2v \\\\  & =(1-2v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-3,3)} & =(1-2v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\,{|}_{u=-3\\,v=3} \\\\  & =(1-2(3))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3+3) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-5|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =5\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186384684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb4d348b-1703-51a1-be05-2e66295cacaa" :knowledge-point/title "Completing Trivial Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3868de94-f40b-55e1-bdb2-a7150e6b6c40" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186471311} {:answer/id #uuid "631e930d-695f-5af5-aab9-dd585c953e99" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186471312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "631e930d-695f-5af5-aab9-dd585c953e99" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186471312} :answer-field/id #uuid "66365a99-0b60-58bb-94f2-ccec01ad35dc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186472161} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b15f1878-c363-590e-a18b-cef0bf952481" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186471313} {:answer/id #uuid "e1261589-bf12-56e1-ba17-0ecce5d607fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186471314}] :answer-fi