17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id 17592186396832} {:answer/id #uuid "fedcf2a1-58c2-5fe0-abf5-3ba83b146f86" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id 17592186396833} {:answer/id #uuid "ff395391-34ba-5fcb-a35c-97965c0d981e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id 17592186396834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9422130e-7d41-59c0-9c3b-e82a870fd3e0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id 17592186396831} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3bbb676-81e2-50e3-a3bb-87f4e8b01e41" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186396829} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adcbfc07-80e8-5ea1-b84e-13f16a7e0b7a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the absorption property" :db/id 17592186396836} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb130c3-0aff-5067-91a1-ee45706f1245" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the constant multiple property" :db/id 17592186396837} {:answer/id #uuid "8054b0b3-df38-5516-9370-a63b32150442" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the sum property" :db/id 17592186396838} {:answer/id #uuid "8f721fc4-0680-57ca-bbe6-1e1378545e6c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the transitive property" :db/id 17592186396839} {:answer/id #uuid "b80f8558-60e8-5dc7-9392-0ec85bf1101f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id 17592186396840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b80f8558-60e8-5dc7-9392-0ec85bf1101f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the reflexive property" :db/id 17592186396840} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b22cd9c-5cf4-532f-a1ca-970ec9a3d464" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186396835} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "096d8579-05d7-55b7-ab38-419cac3562a5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L2 and L3}" :db/id 17592186396842} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebff648-bb6f-5ff2-84af-8d82f7000bf3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1 and L3}" :db/id 17592186396843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ebff648-bb6f-5ff2-84af-8d82f7000bf3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1 and L3}" :db/id 17592186396843} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d4d43f0-56f8-5977-b48a-2665291f0625" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186396841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac4293b7-4265-5467-bc7b-b86d5611daed" :question/math-academy-id "q-335445" :question/problem "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)=2+{e}^{-x},$ then $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,f=O(2+{e}^{-x})$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2}=0$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,{e}^{-x}=O(2)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,f=O(2)$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(1)$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from lines  \n{{field-2}}  \nand  \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line  \n{{field-4}}  \nand  \n{{field-5}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property. \n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If \n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\n \nthen \n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O({{2+{e}^{-x}}_{⏟}}_{f}),\n$$\n\nand this is true by the $\\text{reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(2+{e}^{-x}),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-x}=O(2).\n$$\n\nThus, by lines   $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and the  $\\text{absorption property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=O(2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4},$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(2)\n$$\n\nand by the $\\text{constant multiple property}$ with $c=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(1).\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186397950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e1da77-a5dc-5f78-977e-0d6dae99c97e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Images of the Four Fundamental Subspaces of the Matrix Under Its Action"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4a3e2b6-7b29-5528-96b5-7573a1aadd7f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Null}(A+B)" :db/id 17592186397952} {:answer/id #uuid "4bbf2858-8656-5cf8-89b6-f411004b18f5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})" :db/id 17592186397953} {:answer/id #uuid "343e1966-1342-5cc5-9cd1-08091ba5d293" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Null}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})" :db/id 17592186397954} {:answer/id #uuid "ce963992-d45b-5324-bb97-8def95d4a96a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}(A+B)" :db/id 17592186397955} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6b2f33-a870-578b-9b44-33252827ba71" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186397956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce963992-d45b-5324-bb97-8def95d4a96a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}(A+B)" :db/id 17592186397955} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f937f66-6317-5bbf-a1a5-7a91aa314279" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186397951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3b884b20-b24f-567b-b7d3-aeaaf2084dca" :question/math-academy-id "q-109865" :question/problem "Let $A$ and $B$ be $m\\times n$ matrices. What is the image of $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$ under the action of $A+B?$" :question/worked-solution "Pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ by $A+B$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A+B:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ (a+b)_{1} & (a+b)_{2} & ⋯ & (a+b)_{n} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A+B}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ (a+b)_{1} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ (a+b)_{2} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}+⋯+{x}_{n}\\begin{bmatrix}| \\\\ (a+b)_{n} \\\\ |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{n}$ under the action of $A+B$ must be $\\text{Col}(A+B),$ the column space of $A+B.$\n\nFurthermore, since $\\text{Null}(A+B)$ is mapped to the zero vector $0$ by the definition of the null subspace, the image of its orthogonal complement $\\text{Col}((A+B)^{T})=\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$ must be $\\text{Col}(A+B).$\n\nThis can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/02/02a739d8888f2b46b81075218693e6b2cc87e3c5b81b522abfc9246dcd7c870d.png)"} {:db/id 17592186399037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba58499f-83c5-5590-b067-5c72d5b139a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186399039} {:answer/id #uuid "61a41175-cafd-5d7f-88b5-4b344125cf85" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186399040} {:answer/id #uuid "991289dd-6e89-58ad-98ec-431b03eb6134" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186399041} {:answer/id #uuid "46a45cff-fcc3-53f3-b793-ae6574c91421" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186399042} {:answer/id #uuid "ff494868-b717-56d0-b9b8-ba8b8cadd615" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id 17592186399043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba58499f-83c5-5590-b067-5c72d5b139a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186399039} :answer-field/id #uuid "63a6c2d7-95e4-53da-98cc-2de47ce76700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186399038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30a5731c-2a02-53f9-9c62-2c24fdfa7142" :question/math-academy-id "q-75177" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & \\,\\,\\,-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-\\frac{1}{2},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id 17592186399552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4d10f94-98d4-53d8-a35c-e65a06dd2c55" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using a Single Elementary Row Operation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bb68e1d-d899-5edf-a1fd-ffb8a94a6684" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399554} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd9a95f-29f2-5eb4-9ab2-b3bcac27f74e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399555} {:answer/id #uuid "c722f147-0203-5a71-936b-072e37a9b502" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399556} {:answer/id #uuid "04f5e601-e8f1-5165-8c77-5f70ab657db3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399557} {:answer/id #uuid "14770830-03c3-5a6c-94ae-f9b8f3466ab3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbd9a95f-29f2-5eb4-9ab2-b3bcac27f74e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399555} :answer-field/id #uuid "d50f7ea4-15ce-5b02-8fb1-f1bba0bb77a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186399553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "682029cb-a24a-5772-887f-fb965b3c043f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106842" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186399559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c91fb2b-5607-555d-b897-f0e76765dfa6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lower Triangular Matrix Given the Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03772422-1c36-5538-ad98-2c341599f95b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399561} {:answer/id #uuid "c2fca63d-f8ed-5f29-879b-652f96b8979f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399562} {:answer/id #uuid "d42309d2-7714-52af-b242-8be64341a7ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399563} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef8e9e4-6087-5646-8666-05b609a8e68f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399564} {:answer/id #uuid "0d7c55b5-02cc-530a-a0d0-c4f52cb2cc8d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d7c55b5-02cc-530a-a0d0-c4f52cb2cc8d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399565} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaa733b0-227a-5a23-8495-605d957fadaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186399560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7278f295-d94d-5655-a394-3d2aeae2f280" :question/math-academy-id "q-106847" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $H$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $H$ has the LU factorization $H=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ H & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186399566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c91fb2b-5607-555d-b897-f0e76765dfa6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lower Triangular Matrix Given the Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fed22c0d-0f9b-57d1-92a2-caecdc72420b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399568} {:answer/id #uuid "3dfb8aa6-6f92-55b7-84d4-1a32fd997449" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399569} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6e2e5e-e175-5321-89f2-b8167e85c478" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399570} {:answer/id #uuid "b3fa4db5-51d1-519e-a890-420dd4b64ea4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399571} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8948a3-a5b2-529f-a83b-2897c1197641" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e8948a3-a5b2-529f-a83b-2897c1197641" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399572} :answer-field/id #uuid "35865859-bc92-5d3f-aac6-8f0761f92bd8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186399567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4eefc328-7753-58ac-9913-74a31d74d60d" :question/math-academy-id "q-106849" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $H$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $H$ has the LU factorization $H=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ H & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186399573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cf06c8c-fa37-5fb4-9bae-97c3d61f33d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399575} {:answer/id #uuid "21c0bd19-0548-5d99-9e33-6c9f927b3b22" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399576} {:answer/id #uuid "703a2c37-9c3b-5152-85d4-282f6208240c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399577} {:answer/id #uuid "79134b3c-db1d-513d-a8c1-a59e34a636a6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399578} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a10085-0e10-5d8b-b074-4aa9f88186b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3a10085-0e10-5d8b-b074-4aa9f88186b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186399579} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2955c7f-5f50-5316-9dc4-5bd9b60c3d48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186399574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1feecf50-2291-54b0-bdd0-e3e3488def82" :question/math-academy-id "q-106851" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186399594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119884 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c159d750-ab1f-538b-b8cc-ae66ef1ae414" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Numbers in the Matrix U of an LU Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "376a79e9-d0ed-5d98-b798-72ca9227f724" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,\\,3,\\,2" :db/id 17592186399596} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd98211-258a-5e8b-9826-333a09416ffc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,\\,11,\\,9" :db/id 17592186399597} {:answer/id #uuid "e5de46c8-2b46-59f3-a61a-c8717e914bc4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,\\,5,\\,2" :db/id 17592186399598} {:answer/id #uuid "f7363fb5-4228-5670-abac-cfd957f39d4a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,\\,3,\\,9" :db/id 17592186399599} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ba1f0a-e0cd-5a53-91e0-55dc2a65a9d6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,\\,5,\\,9" :db/id 17592186399600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7363fb5-4228-5670-abac-cfd957f39d4a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,\\,3,\\,9" :db/id 17592186399599} :answer-field/id #uuid "841df764-7b9e-514d-aafe-cc62e02035ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186399595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3e8fc64-f987-59d3-bd53-7963fa833f0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106960" :question/problem "$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 7 & 9 \\\\ 40 & -13 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ \\boxed{} & \\boxed{} & \\boxed{} \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}.\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. From left to right, what are the missing elements from the second row of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make $0$ at the position ${a}_{21}$ of the matrix $A,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 7 & 9 \\\\ 40 & -13 & 8\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 40 & -13 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the elements from the second row of $U$ are $0,3,$ and $9.$"} {:db/id 17592186399713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f892805c-2c2c-5105-91ba-353aefe7b7f2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors Using Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "269a38f4-d50d-507d-a2fe-b6b9552ccaae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id 17592186399715} {:answer/id #uuid "e06b713f-a822-5e21-a364-ba83171f6e15" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id 17592186399716} {:answer/id #uuid "5a1fb2b4-2519-5080-98a6-8275a55a992e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id 17592186399717} {:answer/id #uuid "c53343ca-8e57-512e-ba07-1f08dda987cc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id 17592186399718} {:answer/id #uuid "97ab7ea6-d839-514b-b71d-a9aadeb68fff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id 17592186399719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a1fb2b4-2519-5080-98a6-8275a55a992e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id 17592186399717} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c3251d1-6964-5600-84fe-fbb97c423ff0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186399714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b32c3e49-95fd-5233-9aa7-ff2e20c39b2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-110754" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$  by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}}.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186399720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f892805c-2c2c-5105-91ba-353aefe7b7f2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors Using Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f726da6-7b87-558e-8b51-b7840bdc0abe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id 17592186399722} {:answer/id #uuid "dfad63f4-b83c-5439-a2d9-6fc118ca836e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id 17592186399723} {:answer/id #uuid "30b8bb0c-f3dd-57f2-a3a9-f151350734f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id 17592186399724} {:answer/id #uuid "421e6c1d-b7d5-5d36-a5a5-f3ccd5c75076" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id 17592186399725} {:answer/id #uuid "dbfb14db-bf18-5acc-8ed0-cb564150d8a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id 17592186399726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30b8bb0c-f3dd-57f2-a3a9-f151350734f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id 17592186399724} :answer-field/id #uuid "af12360f-3ae7-5782-b9f9-7e9d6d3e8888" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186399721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4400c4d4-e4b9-52ce-9f78-148918755004" :question/math-academy-id "q-110756" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$  by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}}.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186400777 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa5bf185-de29-5185-ba57-c64250332dfa" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Elements of a Polynomial Regression Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44e868f2-af50-5226-bb61-3693a937dfd4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400779} {:answer/id #uuid "68e79faf-a36b-5872-9d97-d636809b2681" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400780} {:answer/id #uuid "db78fb33-d258-5300-9db0-58175eca891e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400781} {:answer/id #uuid "77464a04-b101-539f-89a0-7d882708a243" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400782} {:answer/id #uuid "672eb4e7-7868-557f-bb9b-53d4ff3618b4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "672eb4e7-7868-557f-bb9b-53d4ff3618b4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400783} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d82c4fc-397c-5ccb-b692-70512b0dcfc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ad69a69-e154-5068-a0be-7ed17edd11f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-139603" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186400784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa5bf185-de29-5185-ba57-c64250332dfa" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Elements of a Polynomial Regression Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e79a8ef-5574-525a-bc92-d7875e606665" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400786} {:answer/id #uuid "93565ded-0b86-517b-8d29-2b2eac76a7fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400787} {:answer/id #uuid "f4804fe2-c53b-5c8b-8796-cab3a1ffedb1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400788} {:answer/id #uuid "af47c3cc-b20a-53d2-a08c-315ffc3ab192" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400789} {:answer/id #uuid "1e18fa5d-c482-57ce-9a72-c1e5d3fe3db5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} 