+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-20$ | $-10$ | $40$ | $20$ | $10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186400812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b2c90d8-0f2e-5f84-bf67-7620d6f1d308" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Design Matrix When the Regression Curve Contains Zero Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e69016b8-0214-5aed-b3cc-7bc5d7371512" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400814} {:answer/id #uuid "cb761260-5f2f-5b88-ba3d-a4e0d8034d21" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 1 & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400815} {:answer/id #uuid "2f3227e3-b9ca-5f18-a7aa-a7778a3fb97d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400816} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf1db50-ba7c-586c-8a20-e703c6d2d32b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400817} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee8d920-e9fd-5c10-aea9-75030df642e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb761260-5f2f-5b88-ba3d-a4e0d8034d21" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 1 & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400815} :answer-field/id #uuid "f96b5da9-4c79-534f-bef1-451b5a0100df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6ff8577-38cb-5956-bb74-df790a451f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-139659" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $15$ | $-13$ | $-5$ | $-4$ | $10$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $15$ | $-13$ | $-5$ | $-4$ | $10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 1 & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -13 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186400819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b2c90d8-0f2e-5f84-bf67-7620d6f1d308" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Design Matrix When the Regression Curve Contains Zero Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "856ff123-4b95-5ec9-8fcf-0c1338ac715a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & (-4)^{2} & (-4)^{3} \\\\ 10 & (-2)^{2} & (-2)^{3} \\\\ -2 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ -5 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400821} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f9190f-9b88-5168-a24a-d3aac5955ec9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & (-4)^{2} \\\\ 1 & -2 & (-2)^{2} \\\\ 1 & 0 & {0}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400822} {:answer/id #uuid "e47ef886-152c-5f78-9831-3f3cb3c15c7b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & (-4)^{3} \\\\ 1 & -2 & (-2)^{3} \\\\ 1 & 0 & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400823} {:answer/id #uuid "3047032f-9054-5717-87ca-d155199e4325" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & (-4)^{2} & (-4)^{3} \\\\ -2 & (-2)^{2} & (-2)^{3} \\\\ 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400824} {:answer/id #uuid "0bc29990-a86e-5375-a3ea-760496952de0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & (-4)^{2} & (-4)^{3} \\\\ 1 & (-2)^{2} & (-2)^{3} \\\\ 1 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3047032f-9054-5717-87ca-d155199e4325" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & (-4)^{2} & (-4)^{3} \\\\ -2 & (-2)^{2} & (-2)^{3} \\\\ 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186400824} :answer-field/id #uuid "43c7c904-76e8-5191-8e57-3ea13b3d74fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3dd33adc-83c8-557b-8748-a617424150b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-139672" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-4$** | **$-2$** | **$0$** | **$2$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-7$ | $10$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $-5$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$-4$** | **$-2$** | **$0$** | **$2$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-7$ | $10$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $-5$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & (-4)^{2} & (-4)^{3} \\\\ -2 & (-2)^{2} & (-2)^{3} \\\\ 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186400826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24daf051-a969-5853-92c4-9336c99d9eac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.75$ meters" :db/id 17592186400828} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c5c0b9-e64a-579b-9d0a-5faca271c7fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.15$ meters" :db/id 17592186400829} {:answer/id #uuid "b25e0551-5bff-5a95-b67a-7ec0fc2e22ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.25$ meters" :db/id 17592186400830} {:answer/id #uuid "4166a757-6ff8-5e2d-b4fe-2e6fc3e044b7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.25$ meters" :db/id 17592186400831} {:answer/id #uuid "00805968-8b23-52f9-a5f4-d3708a27362f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.75$ meters" :db/id 17592186400832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24daf051-a969-5853-92c4-9336c99d9eac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.75$ meters" :db/id 17592186400828} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b23bec-c550-5bbf-bae4-d3fc66059766" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186400827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3ba26ac4-59dd-55c3-b644-94e5b96251c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-139749" :question/problem "A bungee jumping instructor measures how much a bungee rope stretches (in meters) when one end of the rope is attached to the bridge railing while a weight of $x$ kilograms is hung on the other. This data is shown below.\n\n| Weight $(x)$ | **$10$** | **$20$** | **$30$** | **$40$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Stretch $(y)$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, how much will the rope stretch if the instructor uses a $65$-kilogram mass, assuming the trend continues?  \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* \n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{500}\\begin{bmatrix}750 & -25 \\\\ -25 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 200\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 20 \\\\ 1 & 30 \\\\ 1 & 40\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2.5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{500}\\begin{bmatrix}750 & -25 \\\\ -25 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 200\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ 0.05\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.5+0.05x.$\n\nFinally, to predict how much the rope will stretch if a $65$-kilogram mass is hung from it, we substitute $x=65$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5+0.05(65) \\\\  & =3.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the rope will stretch $3.75$ meters if a $65$-kilogram mass is hung from it.\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=65$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."} {:db/id 17592186400839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e33c97-0499-5829-9886-be30b00989ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear 