ta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$30$** | **$20$** | **$10$** | **$5$** | **$2$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | $45$ | $54$ | $65$ | $77$ | $90$ |\n| $y$ | $-10$ | $-4$ | $0$ | $5$ | $12$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 30 & 45 \\\\ 2 & 20 & 54 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 65 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 77 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 90\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186401130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e33c97-0499-5829-9886-be30b00989ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac9b6b53-5c92-51b1-b36f-2a3cc651f319" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 22 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 17 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 15 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 11 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 8 & 25\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186401132} {:answer/id #uuid "fbcb6806-97c3-5c5b-a067-e8db96db6a51" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186401133} {:answer/id #uuid "c58e92d6-356e-5987-82a0-c4ca864d02f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 22 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 17 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 15 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 11 & 13 \\\\ 5 & 8 & 25\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186401134} {:answer/id #uuid "2efd5c91-0f33-5144-8814-8a5bf4712f0d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186401135} {:answer/id #uuid "47e7f5fa-1b69-57b5-bdb7-eab59dcc0807" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 22 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 17 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 15 & 9 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 11 & 13 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 8 & 25 & -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186401136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2efd5c91-0f33-5144-8814-8a5bf4712f0d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186401135} :answer-field/id #uuid "55328507-f46f-5e80-84e7-a9cf12738872" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "646237af-1831-56a9-a4f0-279fefdaeaf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-159730" :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$22$** | **$17$** | **$15$** | **$11$** | **$8$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$5$** | **$6$** | **$9$** | **$13$** | **$25$** |\n| $y$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-6$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.  Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$22$** | **$17$** | **$15$** | **$11$** | **$8$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$5$** | **$6$** | **$9$** | **$13$** | **$25$** |\n| $y$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-6$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane  \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 22 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 17 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 15 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 11 & 13 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 8 & 25\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186401261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198379 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaa6b152-86d3-5f96-8a94-57f86a17a84d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Flux Over a Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aab621f-8cde-5ff7-83da-d05fc13356ba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-\\frac{17}{8}" :db/id 17592186401263} {:answer/id #uuid "1a1077bb-f87e-5cca-9aef-16e4fb33aef6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+e-\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id 17592186401264} {:answer/id #uuid "2adea1b0-35c6-5e84-940d-c02c34a5479d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id 17592186401265} {:answer/id #uuid "6170ad11-316c-53bb-b473-67e1f8f56f3b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id 17592186401266} {:answer/id #uuid "cccdc272-4ba4-53bf-8e7e-06633eea609a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-e-\\frac{13}{8}" :db/id 17592186401267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2adea1b0-35c6-5e84-940d-c02c34a5479d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id 17592186401265} :answer-field/id #uuid "67d473e4-fb62-570e-a504-252ea46fe0d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "682d2ef7-9cff-5a52-921d-70cd49bbb080" :question/math-academy-id "q-161476" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨\\frac{1}{z},\\,\\frac{xy}{z},\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z={e}^{x-y}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le x.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}{e}^{x-y}={e}^{x-y} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}{e}^{x-y}=-{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{z},\\,\\frac{xy}{z},\\,z⟩{|}_{z={e}^{x-y}} \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{-(x-y)},\\,xy{e}^{-(x-y)},\\,{e}^{x-y}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{{e}^{y-x}}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{xy{e}^{y-x}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{{e}^{x-y}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨\\frac{1}{z},\\,\\frac{xy}{z},\\,z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-{e}^{y-x}\\cdot {e}^{x-y}-(xy{e}^{y-x})\\cdot (-{e}^{x-y})+{e}^{x-y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+xy+{e}^{x-y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-1+xy+{e}^{x-y})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-y+\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}-{e}^{x-y}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}-1+{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}-x+{e}^{x}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{8}-1+e)-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{8}+e\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186401336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0629bb-3a37-5ab8-8449-50789b295c7a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69eaa3fe-4c03-532c-9d1a-cd84b8703ad2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{4}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id 17592186401338} {:answer/id #uuid "460112aa-e36b-52a6-a02b-783fee9d05b2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id 17592186401339} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2fbcee-821a-5cd9-8a6e-f9412bd4ab5e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id 17592186401340} {:answer/id #uuid "e12cffd5-0b22-5b0b-a3e3-7270fff61900" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id 17592186401341} {:answer/id #uuid "1540b931-3add-5e5c-99b4-77aa9c86e5f9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id 17592186401342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e12cffd5-0b22-5b0b-a3e3-7270fff61900" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id 17592186401341} :answer-field/id #uuid "17de6bba-7478-572d-9fe3-ada91a6e9edc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186401337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a28026ac-9427-5db7-b40f-31513274adc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162365" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x=\\pm 1,$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our surface $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})=\\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})=\\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩{|}_{z=1/({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =⟨{{x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot \\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-y\\cdot \\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b215ec1e3330f96e7e606e085721b2dd1f50d113f0151e36ec98e6d0a6f54e73.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186402035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d476e061-e62e-516d-a210-085f525da0f0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186402037} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6e6397-9397-52a6-ae26-dbbab796036e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186402038} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9ea285-612d-5a27-90ce-9aac6b9d11bb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186402039} {:answer/id #uuid "683b97cc-4e24-5dae-871f-704c3c57f91e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186402040} {:answer/id #uuid "936ed705-fd9f-5e57-9360-5bc6b86acdee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186402041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd6e6397-9397-52a6-ae26-dbbab796036e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186402038} :answer-field/id #uuid "10f01866-5785-5c51-bd4f-76009cfa054f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df166e56-b183-5016-9f58-5d60ce55786e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32546" :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186402054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eab33316-9a2a-5e12-b51c-b02a826d1b0c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186402056} {:answer/id #uuid "1bad7312-2e66-5700-9aae-eb7c85b9c383" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id 17592186402057} {:answer/id #uuid "7f572362-82aa-5062-a6d6-241eb9bbaaef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 18 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186402058} {:answer/id #uuid "a48759ab-5bd7-525d-8ee8-1aa323ee45c5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 32\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186402059} {:answer/id #uuid "55aa56c5-1a06-51f3-9787-5f99d9d8585f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186402060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55aa56c5-1a06-51f3-9787-5f99d9d8585f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186402060} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f4ecb30-087b-5664-9448-53b7642eb521" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7e3bf80-d71d-502c-8977-ed540b710cc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-32608" :question/problem "Given that  $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot D \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186402702 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2b6d9fc-27bd-59c7-939d-4c7647eba5f8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186402704} {:answer/id #uuid "14f389ba-b888-572b-b782-3154d75be19b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186402705} {:answer/id #uuid "740563d7-e5ec-56c2-ad21-71bebf1921ab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186402706} {:answer/id #uuid "7e800d1f-0a48-5f64-afa7-82d01d638a18" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186402707} {:answer/id #uuid "adfcc4ba-cd4b-5ff5-9647-ade01a07c322" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186402708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "740563d7-e5ec-56c2-ad21-71bebf1921ab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186402706} :answer-field/id #uuid "7901325e-ab13-5590-bad7-aa03b8ccdd1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82160b99-37f5-52f2-85ae-e1d38b048f40" :question/math-academy-id "q-40138" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ given that \n\n$$\nB\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $B:$ \n\n$$\nB={{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore $B$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"} {:db/id 17592186402736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e9ac6ff-6dc0-50d1-bcf0-e5924b445f0d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1\\,050$" :db/id 17592186402738} {:answer/id #uuid "fc84303a-bedd-5991-9237-200188eab34c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$950$" :db/id 17592186402739} {:answer/id #uuid "dc47e4af-c36a-5241-a8ef-baaa45ef2c51" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$875$" :db/id 17592186402740} {:answer/id #uuid "21e67319-25a2-5a84-a939-d04c90f738c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$900$" :db/id 17592186402741} {:answer/id #uuid "82ab20d8-e65e-5a0a-ba4c-f261ab35ac4d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$850$" :db/id 17592186402742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21e67319-25a2-5a84-a939-d04c90f738c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$900$" :db/id 17592186402741} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba3cb994-1bad-5429-8732-d77a5dada295" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0007be5-4b39-5b13-9d0c-f56e380ce0f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-41741" :question/problem "A small business collected data on the number of hours of telephone usage and the total amount (in hundreds of dollars) of the company's weekly operating expenses. This data is shown below.   \n\n| Hours $(x)$ | **$2$** | **$2.4$** | **$2.8$** | **$3.2$** | **$3.6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Expenses $(y)$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the company's weekly operating expenses if the telephone is used for a total of $4$ hours per week, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}40.8 & -14 \\\\ -14 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}30 \\\\ 88\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2.4 \\\\ 1 & 2.8 \\\\ 1 & 3.2 \\\\ 1 & 3.6\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}40.8 & -14 \\\\ -14 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}30 \\\\ 88\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2.5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-1+2.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the amount of the company's weekly operating expenses if the telephone is used for a total of $4$ hours, we substitute $x=4$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1+2.5(4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the company's weekly operating expenses will be $$900.$ \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=4$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."} {:db/id 17592186402743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ba73043-ba7f-5144-85cc-553d87d248c5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$24\\,600$" :db/id 17592186402745} {:answer/id #uuid "1584ef53-01f2-5061-8e58-39b6ee6c83df" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$37\\,300$" :db/id 17592186402746} {:answer/id #uuid "7cfaef46-165b-54fb-98ec-40334338b6c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$20\\,400$" :db/id 17592186402747} {:answer/id #uuid "01465c5f-aea0-556f-b1a7-2a55b583c3af" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$34\\,000$" :db/id 17592186402748} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5f7e29-cc12-59e3-bba6-2cee48d6debc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$39\\,200$" :db/id 17592186402749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cfaef46-165b-54fb-98ec-40334338b6c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$20\\,400$" :db/id 17592186402747} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd4ac2e5-74a0-528f-8078-d6aa415b8f9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e98728e8-7708-5748-ae2b-39ed7242bc51" :question/math-academy-id "q-41751" :question/problem "A store collected data about its profits (in thousands of dollars) for the year's first five months. This data is shown below.\n\n| Month $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $12$ | $15$ | $16$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the profit in the $6$th month, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}78 \\\\ 250\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}78 \\\\ 250\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10.8 \\\\ 1.6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=10.8+1.6x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the profit in the $6$th month, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =10.8+1.6(6) \\\\  & =20.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the profit in the $6$th month will be $$20\\,400.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."} {:db/id 17592186402789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e33c97-0499-5829-9886-be30b00989ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c53cc11-3a22-5a15-a8dd-b33f734a2f3e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 25 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 32 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 42 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 15 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186402791} {:answer/id #uuid "99519b6b-9050-5012-b6c5-d5d8e0bbbc78" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186402792} {:answer/id #uuid "8a060c18-e7bf-5256-a4d3-4d11ceeba5fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 25 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 32 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 42 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 15\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186402793} {:answer/id #uuid "46dbc0ac-c6ac-5ce3-876c-f60f5cc5ec0a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 25 \\\\ 3 & 32 \\\\ 4 & 42 \\\\ 5 & 15\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186402794} {:answer/id #uuid "e606b89e-3669-5122-b464-eb0f71535e5a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186402795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a060c18-e7bf-5256-a4d3-4d11ceeba5fd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 25 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 32 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 42 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 15\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186402793} :answer-field/id #uuid "acd9de2b-a79b-5dcb-ba52-03c0ffb69023" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186402790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "994b1abe-e439-523c-a6a9-bc0050eecdf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-42208" :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$25$** | **$32$** | **$42$** | **$15$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $7$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$25$** | **$32$** | **$42$** | **$15$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $7$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 25 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 32 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 42 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 15\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186403801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c91fb2b-5607-555d-b897-f0e76765dfa6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lower Triangular Matrix Given the Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "523c2229-b400-5c7a-8577-639c6b8af789" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186403803} {:answer/id #uuid "63eae5d2-78e3-5f74-b5e4-a5410101683d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186403804} {:answer/id #uuid "40fafdc2-d2ba-5785-a820-7bc80cae9803" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186403805} {:answer/id #uuid "53c00d30-d1f5-5321-b731-b01cf5c82916" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186403806} {:answer/id #uuid "69e79a65-9bd9-5bb6-9162-647d1a1434fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186403807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69e79a65-9bd9-5bb6-9162-647d1a1434fa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186403807} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9f95a1a-2498-5647-bcee-22f3ad54c88b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186403802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7bd6831-c61b-5654-9e45-1822cb85cf38" :question/math-academy-id "q-75099" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $H$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $H$ has the LU factorization $H=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ H & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186403808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c91fb2b-5607-555d-b897-f0e76765dfa6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lower Triangular Matrix Given the Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53040db9-250a-56a5-aaac-6f9de1208aeb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186403810} {:answer/id #uuid "38d13893-1722-5c2a-ae81-602f74f9eec5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186403811} {:answer/id #uuid "61f534ac-fe4c-590c-9a9b-8f99140fd7f4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186403812} {:answer/id #uuid "94317c8c-0c37-5d0c-8721-a8b615d86c75" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186403813} {:answer/id #uuid "be9b0408-7cf7-5c46-b6d4-9c65348a7946" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186403814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61f534ac-fe4c-590c-9a9b-8f99140fd7f4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186403812} :answer-field/id #uuid "f08b2c1b-6e45-549d-8e75-f22a5e13ebd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186403809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ec22232-a046-59e8-a23e-1122c9545c1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-75100" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $H$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $H$ has the LU factorization $H=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ H & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186403815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ef9d2af-2241-5be9-9fa4-a8e93a74b58c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186403817} {:answer/id #uuid "40956895-fd15-5548-bb48-c3389b38029e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186403818} {:answer/id #uuid "7a0c39e5-8073-5dcf-994d-9e5a575fc526" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186403819} {:answer/id #uuid "66df6fbd-34ea-5bc6-80a0-f363e4fe5e21" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186403820} {:answer/id #uuid "3d0496f0-a286-58c3-82dc-168d307fbe1a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186403821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66df6fbd-34ea-5bc6-80a0-f363e4fe5e21" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186403820} :answer-field/id #uuid "844b700b-f1e6-59c0-ad41-788e19bacce4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186403816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "884fc6a3-6df8-5fa0-a711-f28bd28480fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-75102" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186404149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124531 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f64437bc-258f-57d6-8252-86408694ed54" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{6}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186404151} {:answer/id #uuid "e9fce00d-d8dc-5d30-b228-f50bd9f70fa5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186404152} {:answer/id #uuid "ea65c7ad-6c2b-58e5-8658-7ac9d1dfed34" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186404153} {:answer/id #uuid "06abf95b-dbcf-5d63-a7e2-6b9ef8e25083" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186404154} {:answer/id #uuid "163ea62e-033a-5464-9c63-6deea7d57755" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186404155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "163ea62e-033a-5464-9c63-6deea7d57755" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id 17592186404155} :answer-field/id #uuid "630df7f3-d3b0-52b1-a138-2b8c01a3e3ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "811972db-6534-593a-aa1e-100258f3cf1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81236" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ given that \n\n$$\nT\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $T:$\n\n$$\nT={{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $T$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}$"} {:db/id 17592186404259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186087866 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d23ea584-4a5e-5d3f-b114-caef1a56d6ff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=-2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id 17592186404261} {:answer/id #uuid "178d8403-eb54-593d-abc3-5b52a35b7885" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id 17592186404262} {:answer/id #uuid "ee26e4a3-b2b4-56a4-a778-0012ee7e2935" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id 17592186404263} {:answer/id #uuid "38e4fc3c-40eb-5ddc-a361-e415766dbedb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}" :db/id 17592186404264} {:answer/id #uuid "0883a541-a5d0-5369-94ee-df510266f33b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id 17592186404265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0883a541-a5d0-5369-94ee-df510266f33b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id 17592186404265} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d75aab-799f-5138-9f8f-42b050f51ab1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef24453f-f045-5d0a-8ed3-0ca5b930bc74" :question/math-academy-id "q-8360" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=0\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$"} {:db/id 17592186404801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172028 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26871e46-51ca-5680-a064-bcee4ac984fd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "009d90b0-3f22-584e-98f4-8d3934ce65dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id 17592186404803} {:answer/id #uuid "ce86b767-8088-5585-afa8-b24d88783528" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\frac{1}{3}$" :db/id 17592186404804} {:answer/id #uuid "c873ec16-b4c9-5884-b7ad-177d79590deb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $1$ and $2$" :db/id 17592186404805} {:answer/id #uuid "59b49b93-ec95-536b-99fe-6609cc5c13db" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id 17592186404806} {:answer/id #uuid "4abfbe48-231a-52b0-b510-0e851cfb91d9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $3$" :db/id 17592186404807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "009d90b0-3f22-584e-98f4-8d3934ce65dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id 17592186404803} :answer-field/id #uuid "c636ffab-f93d-536a-8095-481a8e5251a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ac56a346-b90f-50d3-b0af-5c5bfbbf564d" :question/math-academy-id "q-95394" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,5{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}-4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}=8.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $6{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{5{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}-4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}=8\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}-4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}+4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2} & =8 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}-4\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2})+4\\sqrt{2}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2}) & =8 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+8{y}_{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+8{y}_{2} & =8 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2}) & =8 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2}+1-1) & =8 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+4{({y}_{2}+1)}^{2}-4 & =8 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+4{({y}_{2}+1)}^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}+1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{z}_{1}^{2}+4{z}_{2}^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{3}.$"} {:db/id 17592186404808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172028 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26871e46-51ca-5680-a064-bcee4ac984fd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c75de21-c317-5237-86ad-e88e440edb77" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $1$" :db/id 17592186404810} {:answer/id #uuid "84aa3f18-3554-54fd-ae6f-b39ef7e2bffa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of intersecting lines" :db/id 17592186404811} {:answer/id #uuid "6840aa9b-2e07-5c84-8108-3e6b1673c189" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $1$ and $2$" :db/id 17592186404812} {:answer/id #uuid "2fa9bf77-da54-502a-8bbc-e943b623178f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id 17592186404813} {:answer/id #uuid "41613630-2c51-5ec0-9667-5e99088f87b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id 17592186404814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41613630-2c51-5ec0-9667-5e99088f87b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id 17592186404814} :answer-field/id #uuid "394f4d35-8eb9-5319-8076-2545b5e06b52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186404809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c9fcd9c7-a9e3-5124-83fe-aab4333a0614" :question/math-academy-id "q-95399" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}-6\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $10{y}_{1}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+2\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}-6\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly: \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}-6\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 10{y}_{1}^{2}+2\\sqrt{10}(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2})-6\\sqrt{10}(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2}) & =0 \\\\ 10{y}_{1}^{2}-20{y}_{1} & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1}+1)-1 & =0 \\\\ {({y}_{1}-1)}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ {({y}_{1}-1)}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{z}_{1}={y}_{1}-1,\n$$\n\nwe get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the pair of parallel lines  ${z}_{1}=1$ and ${z}_{1}=-1.$"} {:db/id 17592186406631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186171925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba331138-93b8-5fb0-918b-17dc6d3e4029" :knowledge-point/title "Showing a Linear Expression Is Even"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fe62597-9426-5c36-8531-92b9216aa537" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6r+4" :db/id 17592186406595} {:answer/id #uuid "86b4e305-71a0-5b2c-a156-caa0bfa676d6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4r-6" :db/id 17592186406603} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0ae3d3-461e-518b-a2ef-6ebb72f47c66" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4r+6" :db/id 17592186406604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b0ae3d3-461e-518b-a2ef-6ebb72f47c66" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4r+6" :db/id 175