}n & =10m-2 \\\\  & =2({{5m-1}_{⏟}}_{a}) \\\\  & =2a,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have defined $a=5m-1,$ which is an integer."} {:db/id 17592186406840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186188281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a9a16cc-d807-5015-ba40-b4a15fe3a0db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Given a Description of the Image or Kernel"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f3fa2ee-eac1-54f7-a4c6-ec5b539510f4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not enough information." :db/id 17592186406810} {:answer/id #uuid "b15a9656-7038-5a55-bf9f-c3d46f455338" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186406823} {:answer/id #uuid "cc277bfe-8316-5960-ad00-41d18d0bb79e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186406826} {:answer/id #uuid "e7eb8255-65e0-55e8-9452-62dfb2f06a3d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id 17592186406828} {:answer/id #uuid "e84109bf-1001-5843-8805-1788b156098c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186406829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e84109bf-1001-5843-8805-1788b156098c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186406829} :answer-field/id #uuid "28b8502d-f01b-56da-9de3-fce2aa5353e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186406837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bb2599b-79c3-5637-9b4b-ca26bce210be" :question/math-academy-id "q-140098" :question/problem "Find $\\text{nullity}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ and $\\text{Im}(T)=\\{[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y-2z=0\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Solving $y-2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y=2z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=x{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186406841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8f9232e-9658-51ed-95f2-ac2fd1e78bf9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186454078} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a49195-0754-59e8-a736-789d713df706" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186454079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2a49195-0754-59e8-a736-789d713df706" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186454079} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fee3505-d7fb-511a-a74e-2a2d44598ed9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186454789} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33b58ec5-18ed-565a-8a4c-7a428ef3b660" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186454080} {:answer/id #uuid "fa80fb0a-3883-56b5-b6eb-070e8e31d145" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186454081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33b58ec5-18ed-565a-8a4c-7a428ef3b660" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186454080} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d6a4961-38f7-5264-b58e-a39849428f01" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186454790} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "642014a7-57ea-5677-bbda-439505c688fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186454082} {:answer/id #uuid "fbeac619-768a-5b27-a0a3-6f578395c470" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186454083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "642014a7-57ea-5677-bbda-439505c688fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186454082} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0f9063c-3f2e-5e1f-893e-6358b44768be" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186454791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "838322c6-867e-5a73-a18d-bbf81bd1218b" :question/math-academy-id "q-84081" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y=z=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$\n- **II.** If $Tx=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has more than one solution, then $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n- **III.** If $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$, then $T$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $y=z=0,$ any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=x{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $Tx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has more than one solution, there are ${u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}$ such that  ${u}_{1}\\ne {u}_{2}$ and \n\n$$\nT({u}_{1})=T({u}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means, $T$ is not a bijection and according to the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ So, $T$ is not a bijection or, which is the same, $T$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id 17592186406842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186188281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a9a16cc-d807-5015-ba40-b4a15fe3a0db" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Linear Transformation Given a Description of the Image or Kernel"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1470852b-03e9-5db4-8d33-ce40b9b312ff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not enough information." :db/id 17592186406812} {:answer/id #uuid "a80e2855-a004-5120-bf2a-4803a51aedf5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id 17592186406821} {:answer/id #uuid "af65c1f2-8ae8-5564-a484-7a9735e719d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186406822} {:answer/id #uuid "c038e33b-b13d-5ebb-aa6f-234d43c1d51b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186406825} {:answer/id #uuid "e9530934-50fc-57c8-a93e-515a75115d2d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186406830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9530934-50fc-57c8-a93e-515a75115d2d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186406830} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c728edd-0030-5f84-bc57-b18190d3fb0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186406838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94b1ad7b-577b-5c7c-8164-0f25bc8b5263" :question/math-academy-id "q-83446" :question/problem "Find $\\text{rank}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ and $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\{[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,x+y+z=0\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Solving $x+y+z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=-x-y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ -x-y\\end{bmatrix}=x{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186406969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179772 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b99071b9-d463-5e3e-9a0d-71711af836cb" :knowledge-point/title "Inverting Affine Transformations Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f998697-e815-585e-b0b5-3820b9ab9a03" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id 17592186406958} {:answer/id #uuid "51054506-a98c-59d1-82db-eab8461f2cb2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id 17592186406959} {:answer/id #uuid "6c76efec-c230-59b8-bab5-b44500517e1e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id 17592186406960} {:answer/id #uuid "80f4eee2-e47a-55c2-b5f0-e42eb70ff849" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id 17592186406962} {:answer/id #uuid "c3375fbb-f890-5cba-a4b4-7f53a73a78cb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id 17592186406963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c76efec-c230-59b8-bab5-b44500517e1e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id 17592186406960} :answer-field/id #uuid "20065c9e-4427-527a-a708-a46b3d2f2065" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186406966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e92cb69b-a679-593b-8882-9cab8d26a8fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-124289" :question/problem "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=u-v-1,\\,y=3v-2u-6.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=ax+y+9,\\,v=bx+y+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v-1 \\\\ 3v-2u-6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v \\\\ -2u+3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x+1 \\\\ y+6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\cdot (x+1)+1\\cdot (y+6) \\\\ 2\\cdot (x+1)+1\\cdot (y+6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3x+y+9 \\\\ 2x+y+8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=3x+y+9,\\,v=2x+y+8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=3+2+8=13.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186407092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186171928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd14b427-db87-5c61-bcd2-4ecec58450a3" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Parity of Non-Linear Expressions With Multiple Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1875316b-c298-57dd-81ef-a719c982df28" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5q" :db/id 1759