h} :answer/value "3{m}^{5}+4{m}^{4}-1" :db/id 17592186407041} :answer-field/id #uuid "e351293d-9061-577e-a773-504fba7cccd5" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186407090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56052108-5098-50da-941c-873f362e867d" :question/math-academy-id "q-184080" :question/problem "Suppose $n=6{m}^{5}+8{m}^{4}-1,$ where $m\\in Z.$ Complete the following argument.\n\nThe number $n$ is {{field-1}} since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =6{m}^{5}+8{m}^{4}-1 \\\\  & =2({{field-2}})+1 \\\\  & ={{field-3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=$ {{field-4}} is an integer." :question/worked-solution "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a+1,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nNow, note the following regarding the expression $6{m}^{5}+8{m}^{4}-1:$\n\n- All terms, apart from the constant term, have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- The constant term can be written as $-1=-2+1.$\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is $\\text{odd}$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =6{m}^{5}+8{m}^{4}-1 \\\\  & =(6{m}^{5}+8{m}^{4}-2)+1 \\\\  & =2({{3{m}^{5}+4{m}^{4}-1}_{⏟}}_{a})+1 \\\\  & =2a+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=3{m}^{5}+4{m}^{4}-1$ is an integer."} {:db/id 17592186407096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186171927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ee426ac-48be-52e8-a0bb-b48b9087cf97" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Parity of Non-Linear Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fdc2b84-07ea-5329-9d80-28e84904adfc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "even" :db/id 17592186407008} {:answer/id #uuid "a38033e1-5d10-54e3-b59d-05b53ca2b97d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "odd" :db/id 17592186407037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fdc2b84-07ea-5329-9d80-28e84