/id 17592186418979} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b789cdc-9c5a-5b92-ac14-c15783d43de0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+2" :db/id 17592186418782} {:answer/id #uuid "9da1a00f-0ce7-5ef9-bd4b-322d6ed09996" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2k+1" :db/id 17592186418868} {:answer/id #uuid "b32305ae-be7b-522f-bc0c-bedb31cde651" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+1" :db/id 17592186418884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b32305ae-be7b-522f-bc0c-bedb31cde651" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+1" :db/id 17592186418884} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfa77cfa-58e4-5b25-ae91-85c90631ea73" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186418981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22c3e851-1c76-57c4-8e64-d220300f1c79" :question/math-academy-id "q-183366" :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that ${2}^{n+1}>n+1$ for $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}>{{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-9}} & ={{field-10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by the inductive hypothesis, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {2}^{k+1} & >{{field-11}} \\\\  & ={{field-12}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & >{{field-13}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${2}^{n+1}>n+1$ is true for all\n{{field-14}}." :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{2}^{n+1}>n+1\\,\\text{for}\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{1+1}=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{2}^{k+1}>k+1.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{2}^{k+2}>k+2.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{(k+1)+1} & ={2}^{k+1}\\cdot {2}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {2}^{k+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by the inductive hypothesis, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {{{2}^{k+1}}_{⏟}}_{>\\,k+1} & >2(k+1) \\\\  & =2k+2 \\\\  & =k+2+{{k}_{⏟}}_{>\\,0} \\\\  & >k+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ is greater than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${2}^{n+1}>n+1$ for all $n\\ge 1.$"} {:db/id 17592186418994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186092190 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b83c640f-c7fd-5adc-94b8-b129d357b140" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Polynomial Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c396347-72fc-57f0-8f59-176c7a17e50f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186418784} {:answer/id #uuid "e9963c68-ccf4-5183-94e2-250f10549fd5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186418923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9963c68-ccf4-5183-94e2-250f10549fd5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186418923} :answer-field/id #uuid "1216ef77-e331-5d2d-a6bc-ad5097411412" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186418947} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0248bc7-e5ec-5c3d-aace-8e360631c72a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k+3+3{k}^{2}+5k" :db/id 17592186418881} {:answer/id #uuid "e17bb2b7-0dc2-52a9-8c5e-8014ce35a56a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(k+1)+3{k}^{2}+5k" :db/id 17592186418918} {:answer/id #uuid "f6845e6b-3cb1-5d0e-a4b4-adf630746bb7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({k}^{2}+1)+3{k}^{2}+5k" :db/id 17592186418935} {:answer/id #uuid "ff625ca4-53fd-5e08-94cf-fcf3b1896b9d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2k+3+3{k}^{2}+5k" :db/id 17592186418941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e17bb2b7-0dc2-52a9-8c5e-8014ce35a56a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident 