an integer $a$ such that" :db/id 17592186419260} {:answer/id #uuid "5816c43d-e0c2-53a0-82e9-d3ac0a6e278e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "for all integers $a,$" :db/id 17592186419277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37df7167-b541-5188-be06-5be8d70e881f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "there exists an integer $a$ such that" :db/id 17592186419260} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d9cba76-6933-5ee2-8dc9-ba097e1d10e7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186419374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db63c74b-9d62-5b22-9fe0-003a0ca7d1b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-221738" :question/problem "Disprove the following statement:\n\nFor any $a\\in Z,$ if $15∣6a,$ then $15∣a.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nLet's choose $a=$ {{field-6}}.\nThen, we have that \n\n$$\n15{{field-7}}{{30}_{⏟}}_{6a}\\,\\text{and}\\,15{{field-8}}{{5}_{⏟}}_{a}.\n$$\n\nSo, $a=$ {{field-9}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false." :question/worked-solution "By denoting $P(a)$ and $Q(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a):\\,15∣6a$\n\n- $Q(a):\\,15∣a$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(15∣6a)\\,∧\\,(15∤a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. \nIn other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n15∣6a\\,\\text{and}\\,15∤a.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $a=5$ suffices. \n\nLet's choose $a=5.$\nThen, we have that \n\n$$\n15∣{{30}_{⏟}}_{6a}\\,\\text{and}\\,15∤{{5}_{⏟}}_{a}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $a=5$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false."} {:db/id 17592186420695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152076 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6414abb9-00d3-55ea-a1e5-da99999c3ccc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary or Sufficient Conditions From Implications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca89b64b-5016-52db-ad10-13fc71298748" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186453656} {:answer/id #uuid "bf064bf2-82e9-5e43-b653-633779214409" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186453657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca89b64b-5016-52db-ad10-13fc71298748" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186453656} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a87b594-92ce-5651-a817-9591309b3dd4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186454578} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d697a21-38e2-577f-b759-4f3dfc003f64" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186453658} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a27ab7-0a90-5d43-b7e1-77d9b3615543" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186453659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1a27ab7-0a90-5d43-b7e1-77d9b3615543" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186453659} :answer-field/id #uuid "3886d4bc-30ff-5683-96ef-4bc49bde2d5a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186454579} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51996c26-0ab2-5c43-bc19-282ca02980ed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186453660} {:answer/id #uuid "00913fca-69ec-5edf-b839-fc5b920b5fa2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186453661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00913fca-69ec-5edf-b839-fc5b920b5fa2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186453661} :answer-field/id #uuid "62812c81-47e9-5031-9f8d-16bc56ddc0b8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186454580} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ede4ce41-af05-502d-be2d-e518a8a6f456" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186453662} {:answer/id #uuid "06e0d949-a5f7-5f02-82af-4fd19c0fd62a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186453663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ede4ce41-af05-502d-be2d-e518a8a6f456" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186453662} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a732aa4-8287-58c7-a11f-321a281488be" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id 17592186454581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5116bec0-cc13-5aa6-83e8-d90bc6884457" :question/math-academy-id "q-183415" :question/problem "$\\,$ $(m>0\\,∧n>0)⇒(m\\cdot n>0)$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the true implication above?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $m>0\\,∧n>0$ is a **necessary** condition for $m\\cdot n>0$\n- **II.** $m>0\\,∧n>0$ is a **sufficient** condition for $m\\cdot n>0$\n- **III.** $m\\cdot n>0$ is a **necessary** condition for $m>0\\,∧n>0$\n- **IV.** $m\\cdot n>0$ is a **sufficient** condition for $m>0\\,∧n>0$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{{(\\,m>0\\,∧n>0\\,)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{sufficient}}\\,⇒\\,{{(\\,m\\cdot n>0\\,)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{necessary}}\n$$\n\nis a true statement. Therefore:\n\n- Statement II is true. $m>0\\,∧n>0$ is a **sufficient** condition for $m\\cdot n>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. $m\\cdot n>0$ is a **necessary** condition for $m>0\\,∧n>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id 17592186420697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152076 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6414abb9-00d3-55ea-a1e5-da99999c3ccc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary or Sufficient Conditions From Implications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ee9453d-074a-5107-bbb4-b0abf0cb1738" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186453640} {:answer/id #uuid "522af7ae-7610-5307-bc48-8ff762788e73" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186453641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ee9453d-074a-5107-bbb4-b0abf0cb1738" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186453640} :answer-field/id #uuid "da8ef52f-8644-50f9-a33f-a8a2aaba5c4e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field