-e543090d3993" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative" :db/id 17592186422620} {:answer/id #uuid "bf787654-2c4e-5603-9689-885b32a9ae13" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an integer" :db/id 17592186422637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf787654-2c4e-5603-9689-885b32a9ae13" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an integer" :db/id 17592186422637} :answer-field/id #uuid "e103f0fa-dc26-5bdf-a01f-8707518326d3" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186422732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "594c61de-dccf-595b-8086-329e505979dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-184180" :question/problem "Prove that ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ is odd if $n$ is even.\n\nSince $n$ is even, it can be expressed as $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nNow, writing ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2n-3 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}. Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}+2n-3={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich is one more than a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ is {{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,{n}^{2}+2n-3\\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\nSince $n$ is even, it can be expressed as $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into our expression ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2,$ plus $1.$\n\nNow, writing ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2n-3 & =(2a)^{2}+2(2a)-3 \\\\  & =4{a}^{2}+4a-3 \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}+4a-4)+1 \\\\  & =2{{(2{a}^{2}+2a-2)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=2{a}^{2}+2a-2$ (highlighted above), we have that ${n}^{2}+2n-3=2b+1,$ which shows that ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=2{a}^{2}+2a-2.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2n-3 & =2{{(2{a}^{2}+2a-2)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one more than a multiple of $2.$ \n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ is odd."} {:db/id 17592186422740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157255 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2ec1dc-7365-5ba6-bc6d-6e9a13a29cd0" :knowledge-point/title "Proving a Linear Expression Is Odd"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c5e6433-e3e2-59c4-9e69-047bb20ba7ee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(9a+1)+1" :db/id 17592186422523} {:answer/id #uuid "44705236-9bc9-51f0-b1e1-c6d434331ad2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(3a+1)" :db/id 17592186422541} {:answer/id #uuid "4e94a78f-6843-5f08-b7f7-349dffba9bca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(9a)+1" :db/id 17592186422550} {:answer/id #uuid "8e445147-c866-5b04-91c3-47df6639fb19" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(9a)" :db/id 17592186422592} {:answer/id #uuid "b7274983-02a2-500b-90b2-41776eeb983d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(3a+1)+1" :db/id 17592186422625} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d2d068-2107-5c7c-a64d-61404e336153" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(9a+1)" :db/id 17592186422675}