ces [{:answer/id #uuid "41a95b4c-51e4-5805-b964-452a39e3faca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "odd" :db/id 17592186422537} {:answer/id #uuid "f2960c28-b6ce-5287-9382-2bf576f6e4ec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "even" :db/id 17592186422674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41a95b4c-51e4-5805-b964-452a39e3faca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "odd" :db/id 17592186422537} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf251810-fc22-5932-932b-54a908c024c8" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186422724} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51b73214-f3ac-5b64-b4d2-4e0618a85e91" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "positive" :db/id 17592186422553} {:answer/id #uuid "556bad22-1086-5c18-9000-3ca5d62f04b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a natural number" :db/id 17592186422556} {:answer/id #uuid "9463e670-378f-5ea8-bb92-c0c907645409" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an integer" :db/id 17592186422597} {:answer/id #uuid "ff82f1b0-a980-52ae-80af-83783a56df3c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "negative" :db/id 17592186422684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9463e670-378f-5ea8-bb92-c0c907645409" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an integer" :db/id 17592186422597} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3ec463e-4414-530a-9f98-5ebd6c06b1d9" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186422725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2a01950-32bd-52d0-9e4d-1134c0e8e132" :question/math-academy-id "q-184289" :question/problem "Prove that if $n$ is an odd integer, then $7n+4$ is odd.\n\nLet $n$ be an odd integer. Then, $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n+4 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}. Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n+4 & ={{field-6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one larger than a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, $7n+4$ is {{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is odd}\\,⇒\\,7n+4\\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\nLet $n$ be an odd integer. Then, $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into our expression $7n+4$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2,$ plus $1.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n+4 & =7(2a+1)+4 \\\\  & =14a+7+4 \\\\  & =(14a+10)+1 \\\\  & =2{{(7a+5)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=7a+5$ (highlighted above), we have that $7n+4=2b+1,$ which shows that $7n+4$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=7a+5.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n+4 & =2{{(7a+5)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one larger than a multiple of $2.$ \n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, $7n+4$ is odd."} {:db/id 17592186423858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162045 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "566bda0f-f62d-581d-936d-baa8ae474e50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix: Cases Requiring Orthogonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bb23bb5-3483-5614-b88a-de724ea