17592186423843} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cf39c55-ade5-5512-aeae-e8ce09883317" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186423691} {:answer/id #uuid "a1250c4b-23e3-59f3-a490-f89d27cddab2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id 17592186423767} {:answer/id #uuid "a39a1597-fd98-5261-9c1a-cffbe2cd582d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186423769} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e0e18f-beed-55ab-95b9-7b8cf6b2e8bb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id 17592186423815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a39a1597-fd98-5261-9c1a-cffbe2cd582d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186423769} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0b58e38-8d8a-545b-a564-39cbf09af580" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186423851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd607d3f-88f1-5057-a261-73a39b9023d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-363834" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=2$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${x}_{2}$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-6}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-7}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                $0$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186424110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87dc8398-fb5c-564b-a796-db15ff30d72c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Vector Is in the Column Space of a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "158fd3b6-b42a-5e9e-b83d-ee548a2f6fa5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Col}(A)" :db/id 17592186424104} {:answer/id #uuid "791e7d26-9d1f-5803-b774-f6e2358c4273" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186424105} {:answer/id #uuid "a421f5cb-8c63-56cc-847e-0b15d335d55e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id 17592186424106} {:answer/id #uuid "dab0c485-45d4-554d-af12-2b22d279fbd1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id 17592186424107} {:answer/id #uuid "ed81c627-15f5-5ac5-9a83-8720033ed743" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id 17592186424108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "791e7d26-9d1f-5803-b774-f6e2358c4273" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186424105} :answer-field/id #uuid "a69e7526-7c64-51e8-b0ec-23524a78858f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186424109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b249af0d-2baa-56c1-b03f-659223bf8b34" :question/math-academy-id "q-35572" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3},$ and ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and ${a}_{3}$ are the respective columns of $A.$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ we need to check if the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 12 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -12 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ -12{x}_{3} &=-6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2}+4(\\frac{1}{2}) & =2 \\\\ -{x}_{2}+2 & =2 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3(0)-8(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4 & =-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}=2\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0.$"} {:db/id 17592186424810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186138400 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a49001aa-bf42-5005-9bf0-940caab49b8f" :knowledge-point/title "Writing Larger Exponents as Repeated Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23948b42-b762-51e7-9468-ecef08521c35" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53,453" :db/id 17592186424653} {:answer/id #uuid "5247dc82-e291-5e70-a5e1-a18611a34b47" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53" :db/id 17592186424675} {:answer/id #uuid "7be5874e-a678-5f3a-8bc9-5fcf4ea6fa99" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "534" :db/id 17592186424702} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb628e0-3c1f-563b-bab3-4db236781bff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53+53+53+53" :db/id 17592186424747} {:answer/id #uuid "fb103597-39b6-5e4b-908b-9ef13f036e2d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53" :db/id 17592186424774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb103597-39b6-5e4b-908b-9ef13f036e2d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53" :db/id 17592186424774} :answer-field/id #uuid "774dcce1-bb39-5483-867c-6e3177bc6b84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186424790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0cbd63dc-7d38-513f-8022-cfd6f02074bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-55068" :question/problem "${53}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Here, ${53}^{4}$ is in exponent form. The base is $53$ and the exponent is $4.$ \n\nThis means that $53$ is multiplied by itself $4$ times:\n\n$$\n{{53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ times}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{53}^{4}=53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186424811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147413 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7701af1-73f4-5da0-a029-1737dfbec48d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Two-Digit Decimal Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04dc323b-3855-5701-bce8-78791ab1d56c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49.72" :db/id 17592186424631} {:answer/id #uuid "9909966f-06f0-584e-ba1a-60ae24edd70e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50.48" :db/id 17592186424720} {:answer/id #uuid "ae530de8-7965-5f20-9b26-cb84a5ce4853" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50.04" :db/id 17592186424733} {:answer/id #uuid "bd973cfc-77ca-57fc-bb29-255d3a6c2446" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "51.84" :db/id 17592186424738} {:answer/id #uuid "e76fe111-1d20-599a-8c68-889178b96f00" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52.28" :db/id 17592186424760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd973cfc-77ca-57fc-bb29-255d3a6c2446" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "51.84" :db/id 17592186424738} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ba4d5f3-6c7c-5fbc-964e-6c0b058db14b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186424791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0faa0bbf-a97d-52f3-9471-5236871deb2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-138343" :question/problem "$(7.2)^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, by writing the expression with multiplication signs, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(7.2)}^{2} & ={{7.2\\times 7.2}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{1} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now count the number of decimal places in the $2$ factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in $7.2.$\nTherefore, the exponent $(7.2)^{2}$ will have $1\\times 2=2$ decimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $5184$ and add a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n51\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{84}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $(7.2)^{2}=51.84.$"} {:db/id 17592186424812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8b307c7-79bc-58de-ab88-b0783857371b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Answer Is a Whole Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ce77225-6b10-59d8-94dc-a85b52516bce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id 17592186424673} {:answer/id #uuid "676b422f-6df7-54a6-9579-dc539d565f75" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id 17592186424691} {:answer/id #uuid "9ebff1b7-7f0a-519b-88c2-d7e903032e27" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id 17592186424724} {:answer/id #uuid "be90eeca-e1d6-5d59-9d9a-3e7963d6b69c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id 17592186424739} {:answer/id #uuid "e47c19c0-77ae-5ff2-9465-d0181a4fe8c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id 17592186424758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e47c19c0-77ae-5ff2-9465-d0181a4fe8c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id 17592186424758} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e429e22-014b-5313-ae56-7ef7411b61f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186424778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "152194d4-bfdf-57ac-b58d-963178280721" :question/math-academy-id "q-136918" :question/problem "$\\frac{32}{5}\\times \\frac{25}{8}=$" :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(32\\,÷\\,5)\\times (25\\,÷\\,8).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,5$ and $÷\\,8:$\n\n$$\n(32\\,÷\\,8)\\times (25\\,÷\\,5)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{32}{8}\\times \\frac{25}{5}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\frac{32}{8}}_{⏟}}_{4}\\times {{\\frac{25}{5}}_{⏟}}_{5}=4\\times 5=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186424826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186147406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8b307c7-79bc-58de-ab88-b0783857371b" :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Answer Is a Whole Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "077c81c4-2e53-51f1-a9bd-bd147e26c272" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186424635} {:answer/id #uuid "35591385-a9ca-5c99-b8b6-2cd819ca9c3d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id 17592186424663} {:answer/id #uuid "931f6469-3499-5d76-bd2f-82f08bec183f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id 17592186424717} {:answer/id #uuid "a085b754-358f-5308-9a51-fc57c78a2409" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id 17592186424725} {:answer/id #uuid "ec177e43-15bb-5468-8263-7c011259e1a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186424764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "931f6469-3499-5d76-bd2f-82f08bec183f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id 17592186424717} :answer-field/id #uuid "51c6e7c4-e97a-5ea3-85a6-0892f99515fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/