iven below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(C).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3},$ and ${c}_{1}$, ${c}_{2}$, and ${c}_{3}$ are the respective columns of $C.$\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(C),$ we need to check if the equation $Cx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[C\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[C\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -4 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & ↔{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -4 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -4 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -16 & -11\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{7}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 & -18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ -9{x}_{3} &=-18.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2}+3(2) & =-3 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =5-2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =5-2(-3)-3(2) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Cx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(C).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}=5\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{1}}+(-3)\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{2}}+2\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=5\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 2=-30.$"} {:db/id 17592186430359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162045 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "566bda0f-f62d-581d-936d-baa8ae474e50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix: Cases Requiring Orthogonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55a5e9b6-ba04-5596-85c3-98cbfa5749b8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id 17592186430282} {:answer/id #uuid "8d2736ce-0ded-5e27-8be4-d1b6b00a4c03" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id 17592186430301} {:answer/id #uuid "9c1f2ff8-006b-5ae9-afd4-cd25ca36cc9e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id 17592186430305} {:answer/id #uuid "f422fa6e-88a7-5efa-ab1c-7813698673fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186430336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c1f2ff8-006b-5ae9-afd4-cd25ca36cc9e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id 17592186430305} :answer-field/id #uuid "03b01ee8-41bf-5748-a07b-1c8fbdd71f8c" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186430339} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b6b6d3-3622-5981-8d1b-d99fe60550e6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/i