e {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{2}" :db/id 17592186545607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32ff85ef-d2d9-55eb-ad14-a552c92ca012" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{3}" :db/id 17592186545302} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6a4dee0-82de-5621-a8e8-93ce55061b38" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186545789} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da4de33-d31e-535e-8ef1-9058622f152f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id 17592186545258} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4acafd-ef69-5bad-9742-8f157db9faf9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186545297} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe66d81-a58f-5131-a76a-bdf41bd97bfd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id 17592186545383} {:answer/id #uuid "a5db6f71-d5bb-531a-adbe-06783eaedc8e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id 17592186545505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1da4de33-d31e-535e-8ef1-9058622f152f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id 17592186545258} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa7da9c9-345c-5889-a4c3-151cdf9c9850" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186545792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47b88835-9941-5b00-8cef-a75a2de591b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-363832" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | $\\frac{4}{5}$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | ${x}_{1}$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-6}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-7}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                $0$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{1}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,3{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186545827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186162045 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "566bda0f-f62d-581d-936d-baa8ae474e50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix: Cases Requiring Orthogonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35b4ca0a-9edf-58bb-9973-6bce1087bbbd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}_{2}" :db/id 17592186545309} {:answer/id #uuid "a59b3207-17fe-54db-93f1-48d5b2c6af0c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{2}" :db/id 1759218654550