solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 19 \\\\ 3 & 25 \\\\ 4 & 31 \\\\ 5 & 40\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 27\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186552500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133809 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eaee4e9-7be5-5c35-bc88-89697a15d75a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression That Passes Through the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "315beb70-a5ce-5c37-a5c9-c0eca737c77e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3 \\\\ 10 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186551783} {:answer/id #uuid "6d35d5af-1a30-585e-b04a-4b9d43448d86" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186551921} {:answer/id #uuid "71f24dc2-0e5f-5cd3-ba5c-65c375e4e4a1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3 \\\\ 10 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186551935} {:answer/id #uuid "79f6ba33-312c-5627-95ab-ce2b3d55ffe0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186551953} {:answer/id #uuid "bf8c68d4-b0dc-5d0b-a0df-3653df84c907" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 10 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186552124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71f24dc2-0e5f-5cd3-ba5c-65c375e4e4a1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3 \\\\ 10 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186551935} :answer-field/id #uuid "08728767-6e68-5192-a8ec-e2d8efeee549" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186552260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf8297d3-92c1-59c7-82f4-d5fa4db2f279" :question/math-academy-id "q-139932" :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** | **$10$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$7$** | **$5$** | **$3$** | **$2$** |\n| $y$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** | **$10$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$7$** | **$5$** | **$3$** | **$2$** |\n| $y$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3 \\\\ 10 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186553516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186176007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1a96a21-f658-590f-a38b-deffd2f74145" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability Geometrically Using a CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e1edfbb-97a6-5512-b70a-6e8d8202000f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id 17592186553039} {:answer/id #uuid "56c8630f-1621-5e8d-809b-4d2c5ad8b94f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id 17592186553054} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb7531a-12d9-526b-85d6-9ee848e15e25" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id 17592186553065} {:answer/id #uuid "8707bc17-722e-5ad2-b68e-45d04cefeabe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id 17592186553140} {:answer/id #uuid "b19432ec-9898-5227-bca5-d283658bf48c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id 17592186553224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b19432ec-9898-5227-bca5-d283658bf48c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id 17592186553224} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3273a24-dde6-50e8-9d77-b0d57e06622e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186553472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "392c7844-059b-541a-9161-f7e9d9b52570" :question/math-academy-id "q-143708" :question/problem "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(8,6)=0.8,\\,F(8,2)=0.5,\\,F(2,6)=0.4,\\,F(2,2)=0.2.\n$$\n\nFind $P(2\\le X\\le 8,2\\le Y\\le 6).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/15/1524afeeaea3cc0fd4ee51b9572222bdd846fba378e029e4fa20a272a4fe6353.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,6).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(8,2)$ and $(2,6).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to *add back* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(2,2).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le X\\le 8,2\\le Y\\le 6) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,2)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 2)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(2,6)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 6)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(2,2)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 2)}} \\\\  & =F(8,6)-F(8,2)-F(2,6)+F(2,2) \\\\  & =0.8-0.5-0.4+0.2 \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186554542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bbee7f0-8803-54e8-874c-e570323ea33a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a3fb017-e789-574b-a19f-04df69dcde8e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61.7\\pm 9.1" :db/id 17592186554012} {:answer/id #uuid "826dcf8a-f14a-5879-aae7-fcfae47a33b4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61.7\\pm 8.2" :db/id 17592186554130} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3c29cb-bcfa-5582-b197-0fb22fc641ff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61.7\\pm 9.5" :db/id 17592186554172} {:answer/id #uuid "bf205baf-60e7-57ea-b105-ae0c35dd73ab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61.7\\pm 10.6" :db/id 17592186554286} {:answer/id #uuid "ca5fe5a5-ce63-5f4a-900e-5dfd444db1db" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61.7\\pm 10.9" :db/id 17592186554314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca5fe5a5-ce63-5f4a-900e-5dfd444db1db" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61.7\\pm 10.9" :db/id 17592186554314} :answer-field/id #uuid "84de798d-98d3-5bb6-abaa-5ce3efd4cee8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186554486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14b7fe25-0e56-53c7-82f0-cba7c7a1a3e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-159581" :question/problem "| **Number of hours ($x$)** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Grade ($y$)** | $75$ | $82$ | $90$ | $95$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of hours four students spent studying for a math exam and the grades they obtained. The corresponding fitted linear regression line using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=61.7+6.8x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=3.5,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.9,$ find a $98%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>6.965)=0.01,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=61.7+6.8x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=61.7+6.8x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=61.7.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $98%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.98=0.02\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.01.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>6.965)=0.01.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.01}=6.965.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(2-3.5)^{2}+(3-3.5)^{2}+(4-3.5)^{2}+(5-3.5)^{2} \\\\  & =2.25+0.25+0.25+2.25 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=6.965\\cdot \\sqrt{0.9\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{3.5}^{2}}{5})}≈10.9\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=61.7\\pm 10.9\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186554550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7813df9e-9641-58a3-8537-e672e5fd079c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Intercept Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b96016e-6c70-5725-be4a-7c141b414f3f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.3" :db/id 17592186553977} {:answer/id #uuid "402a3262-d142-567d-bf7d-c8b89a4726dc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.0" :db/id 17592186553985} {:answer/id #uuid "83ff5ce8-83cc-52e2-94e9-f36326bca2f2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.4" :db/id 17592186554137} {:answer/id #uuid "9870a91b-b9ca-50af-8384-c34539b8a4b1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.6" :db/id 17592186554204} {:answer/id #uuid "f661769d-8896-57ab-83d7-39025d585319" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.9" :db/id 17592186554403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f661769d-8896-57ab-83d7-39025d585319" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.9" :db/id 17592186554403} :answer-field/id #uuid "c654e4fd-8bc7-5442-8ca4-e6531d76e850" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186554512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24ed500c-c9f9-51ed-ab79-e8e54e985d91" :question/math-academy-id "q-159566" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=12.6-3.9x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{18},{y}_{18})$ of $n=18$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=3.5,$ the mean square error of the model is $2.1,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=85,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.921)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $16$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=12.6-3.9x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=12.6-3.9x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=12.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.921)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{16,0.005}=2.921.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.921\\cdot \\sqrt{2.1\\cdot (\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{{3.5}^{2}}{85})}≈1.9\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=12.6\\pm 1.9\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186554563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4d10f94-98d4-53d8-a35c-e65a06dd2c55" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using a Single Elementary Row Operation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e7f22e7-8655-56ca-99a2-efa5c63acdee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id 17592186553952} {:answer/id #uuid "70be245a-94da-5521-81f7-b1eff4563a5f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id 17592186554097} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf259a9-c7cf-53af-a02f-d46c56183bb5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186554120} {:answer/id #uuid "d29b0f99-07ab-51ab-98fe-551c80011b17" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id 17592186554327} {:answer/id #uuid "ec561c11-e653-5aca-ba10-c9323edae82d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id 17592186554387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e7f22e7-8655-56ca-99a2-efa5c63acdee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id 17592186553952} :answer-field/id #uuid "e485ffe4-c9b3-59e1-9725-0e402d6b690f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186554524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "39a7d310-9046-5578-9f8d-31308eeefed9" :question/math-academy-id "q-75094" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -10 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{23}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -10 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-4,{u}_{23}=3,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{23}=-12.$"} {:db/id 17592186554569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a42fd5c8-9721-5bf4-a1d7-b4b752b09708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Slope Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52af52d6-c43f-5772-9249-56cb2e062ae2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8\\pm 0.5" :db/id 17592186554029} {:answer/id #uuid "804a8e29-21c2-50e1-85dd-8bf5ea96d9a6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8\\pm 0.4" :db/id 17592186554126} {:answer/id #uuid "8182d4e1-5154-5473-a21d-13951e34b26e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8\\pm 0.2" :db/id 17592186554129} {:answer/id #uuid "923c151e-4c0c-5138-872f-79d725e9b5a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.3\\pm 0.1" :db/id 17592186554191} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7cbdf0-9605-52c2-aebb-1c3a606aa1d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5\\pm 0.4" :db/id 17592186554217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52af52d6-c43f-5772-9249-56cb2e062ae2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8\\pm 0.5" :db/id 17592186554029} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dbbd93d-093b-57e4-bfc1-ad59a17bdad5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186554466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42368253-8090-5197-973b-1010d704c08f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159367" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4+1.8x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{12},{y}_{12})$ of $n=12$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $11.5,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=143,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.812)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $10$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4+1.8x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=3.4+1.8x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=1.8.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.812)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{10,0.05}=1.812.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=1.812\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{11.5}{143}}≈0.5\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=1.8\\pm 0.5\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186554571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a42fd5c8-9721-5bf4-a1d7-b4b752b09708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Slope Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c6ca714-23c9-5847-a9ba-02da4f1cff76" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.6\\pm 0.2" :db/id 17592186553950} {:answer/id #uuid "44edb47e-bcc6-56b2-a80f-28051a0a9b01" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.6\\pm 0.1" :db/id 17592186553996} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb3fbf6-a548-535d-b241-e4dfd3a43153" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.6\\pm 0.5" :db/id 17592186554048} {:answer/id #uuid "bd62c1f0-f5e8-5348-b782-6e34e684508d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.6\\pm 1.5" :db/id 17592186554280} {:answer/id #uuid "e596f17d-fc27-5a9d-baa8-083f85f564e5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.6\\pm 1.3" :db/id 17592186554369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c6ca714-23c9-5847-a9ba-02da4f1cff76" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.6\\pm 0.2" :db/id 17592186553950} :answer-field/id #uuid "f561bd09-f9f3-5087-9384-dc94b699a804" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186554533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b0fde5c-0530-5337-9e62-63e3ac799868" :question/math-academy-id "q-159393" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=1-2.6x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{11},{y}_{11})$ of $n=11$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $0.78,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=110,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.262)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $9$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=1-2.6x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=1-2.6x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-2.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.262)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{9,0.025}=2.262.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.262\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.78}{110}}≈0.2\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-2.6\\pm 0.2\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186554572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bbee7f0-8803-54e8-874c-e570323ea33a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62649176-19b3-5dc9-882a-69ef22eb63b6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 1.5" :db/id 17592186554061} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb2e354-67fd-5920-b3f1-8def0deb29e2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 1.1" :db/id 17592186554095} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec24db7-f765-5318-ae89-7d5a90748c37" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 1.8" :db/id 17592186554124} {:answer/id #uuid "94f6c867-15bb-5d02-aa0b-4ba6976af87a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 1.3" :db/id 17592186554198} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c741e7-dfd7-5769-bd67-c9d073965e46" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.2" :db/id 17592186554266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94f6c867-15bb-5d02-aa0b-4ba6976af87a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 1.3" :db/id 17592186554198} :answer-field/id #uuid "67039e40-5de3-570b-bbed-0d3b0e5de178" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186554471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f56dfe4-956b-5759-bcb7-ccce82fe1dbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-159556" :question/problem "| **Age ($x$)** | **$34$** | **$36$** | **$40$** | **$42$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Hours of sleep ($y$)** | $6.5$ | $7$ | $7.5$ | $8$ |\n\nThe table above shows the ages, in years, and the number of hours each of four randomly selected adults slept last night. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=0.6+0.2x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=38,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.0125,$ find an $80%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.886)=0.1,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=0.6+0.2x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=0.6+0.2x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=0.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $80%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.8=0.2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.1.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.886)=0.1.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.1}=1.886.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(34-38)^{2}+(36-38)^{2}+(40-38)^{2}+(42-38)^{2} \\\\  & =16+4+4+16 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=1.886\\cdot \\sqrt{0.0125\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{38}^{2}}{40})}≈1.3\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=0.6\\pm 1.3\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186554581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bbee7f0-8803-54e8-874c-e570323ea33a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15060daf-a4bc-5eb8-b79b-034a85e0bbf7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 18.1" :db/id 17592186553910} {:answer/id #uuid "71ae2911-c3b0-54dd-b735-0b92451fc7ac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 9.8" :db/id 17592186554099} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f1300c-5d71-5a55-8165-193b27ab2363" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 17.5" :db/id 17592186554302} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f2c162-946e-59cc-8842-379aa299e7ac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 16.2" :db/id 17592186554367} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ebf936-7757-56b0-a68f-58f3fcbd404b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 10.3" :db/id 17592186554370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15060daf-a4bc-5eb8-b79b-034a85e0bbf7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 18.1" :db/id 17592186553910} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f3ed31d-762f-57be-a238-32e2c1426527" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186554467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69c89ef7-3131-5d48-9709-9716ca24bd0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-159557" :question/problem "| **Height ($x$)** | **$67$** | **$70$** | **$73$** | **$76$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Shoe size ($y$)** | $8.5$ | $9$ | $11$ | $13$ |\n\nThe table above shows the heights, in inches, and corresponding shoe sizes of four randomly selected men. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-26.6+0.5x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=71.5,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.338,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.  \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.920)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=-26.6+0.5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-26.6+0.5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=-26.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.920)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.920.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(67-71.5)^{2}+(70-71.5)^{2}+(73-71.5)^{2}+(76-71.5)^{2} \\\\  & =20.25+2.25+2.25+20.25 \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.920\\cdot \\sqrt{0.338\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{71.5}^{2}}{45})}≈18.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=-26.6\\pm 18.1\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186554587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172120 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184883f1-85ea-5bd1-8ccd-88226f105da1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "337b83df-33f6-5e1a-bb43-103241a1e6f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\pm 0.3" :db/id 17592186553960} {:answer/id #uuid "812e97bc-01ca-5b39-9e67-dd0fed627e34" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\pm 0.5" :db/id 17592186554128} {:answer/id #uuid "95ee6076-b2f9-5bbc-b329-206a5101eee5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.8\\pm 0.4" :db/id 17592186554200} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a799fa-6ed7-5277-8e8a-013cdcf480ad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2\\pm 0.6" :db/id 17592186554223} {:answer/id #uuid "c235c63d-67de-5b7d-ba35-67ffec0716b1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2\\pm 0.3" :db/id 17592186554291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "812e97bc-01ca-5b39-9e67-dd0fed627e34" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\pm 0.5" :db/id 17592186554128} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0c08eb5-9c27-5ce5-b185-ad184aa151bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186554508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "764e52d5-673e-5136-a2ff-4fbb20c5aad9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159454" :question/problem "| **Age ($x$)** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$7$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Price ($y$)** | $10.2$ | $9$ | $8.1$ | $5$ |\n\nThe table above shows the ages, in years, and corresponding selling prices, in thousands of dollars, of four car models as advertised in a newspaper classifieds last month. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=12.7-x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=4.5,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.456,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.92)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=12.7-x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=12.7-x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.92)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.92.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(2-4.5)^{2}+(4-4.5)^{2}+(5-4.5)^{2}+(7-4.5)^{2} \\\\  & =6.25+0.25+0.25+6.25 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.92\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.456}{13}}≈0.5\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-1\\pm 0.5\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186554591 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7813df9e-9641-58a3-8537-e672e5fd079c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Intercept Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "489fcd75-9368-5e9b-acbf-94835400f3d5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4\\pm 0.9" :db/id 17592186554008} {:answer/id #uuid "92b0899f-5335-5dd0-a82e-12c93b11cd39" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4\\pm 1.1" :db/id 17592186554194} {:answer/id #uuid "93ebfe14-4c6e-5be6-8ef7-fc1066aec02c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4\\pm 0.7" :db/id 17592186554197} {:answer/id #uuid "b312c9b8-2b8a-552e-98f0-214625871779" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4\\pm 0.2" :db/id 17592186554265} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c71797-2619-523f-998b-4dc9f5e6a269" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.1" :db/id 17592186554375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93ebfe14-4c6e-5be6-8ef7-fc1066aec02c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4\\pm 0.7" :db/id 17592186554197} :answer-field/id #uuid "b86a25e5-3ce1-5477-9eb2-c277e7527b4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186554504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "812d00c8-ff0e-5dde-8e15-88d6aa199ddf" :question/math-academy-id "q-159561" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.4+0.2x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{12},{y}_{12})$ of $n=12$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=1.2,$ the mean square error of the model is $0.7,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=18.9,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.228)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $10$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.4+0.2x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=2.4+0.2x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=2.4.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.228)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{10,0.025}=2.228.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.228\\cdot \\sqrt{0.7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{12}+\\frac{{1.2}^{2}}{18.9})}≈0.7\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.4\\pm 0.7\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186554594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7813df9e-9641-58a3-8537-e672e5fd079c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Intercept Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19ea48dc-2bbd-5e4f-9e61-02d95414ac74" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 2.3" :db/id 17592186553918} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4f31a4-eeac-52ca-b025-6e0b059772dd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 2.5" :db/id 17592186553983} {:answer/id #uuid "60c1e936-44cd-56c8-8e97-44a0822f3d4c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 3.3" :db/id 17592186554057} {:answer/id #uuid "ce12ef9b-5ac2-5639-b917-770b6e5414d3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 2.7" :db/id 17592186554319} {:answer/id #uuid "dd53447b-e183-5dfb-8fe3-9f2284148e07" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 2.1" :db/id 17592186554351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f4f31a4-eeac-52ca-b025-6e0b059772dd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 2.5" :db/id 17592186553983} :answer-field/id #uuid "caf088fc-e014-5a09-8ceb-3ba512fc68e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186554513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "851fa16f-b92a-5672-9bbf-979f258829aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-159560" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=5+0.4x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{10},{y}_{10})$ of $n=10$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=4.3,$ the mean square error of the model is $3.7,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=330,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>3.355)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=5+0.4x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=5+0.4x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>3.355)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{8,0.005}=3.355.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.355\\cdot \\sqrt{3.7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{{4.3}^{2}}{330})}≈2.5\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=5\\pm 2.5\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186554599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bbee7f0-8803-54e8-874c-e570323ea33a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2629af92-040b-52b8-9b80-03dbd9c188f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-165\\pm 44.7" :db/id 17592186553938} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9eea9f-4d37-543c-9eaa-babf46254a0a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-165\\pm 43.4" :db/id 17592186554052} {:answer/id #uuid "8d6a69c6-a56f-5da5-a2f4-cda015f6c0cc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-165\\pm 43.2" :db/id 17592186554167} {:answer/id #uuid "91bc6383-5d46-59ca-b246-89d30592f06c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-165\\pm 41.7" :db/id 17592186554187} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d3d801-fda0-5c5b-82eb-e97ceb523307" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-165\\pm 42.8" :db/id 17592186554294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d9eea9f-4d37-543c-9eaa-babf46254a0a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-165\\pm 43.4" :db/id 17592186554052} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcab1227-d753-5449-9bf0-127c2147993a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186554536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c255cc5-a4ec-5d1f-be58-dbb45c0a4d57" :question/math-academy-id "q-159590" :question/problem "| **Height ($x$)** | **$64$** | **$68$** | **$72$** | **$76$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Weight ($y$)** | $130$ | $148$ | $164$ | $186$ |\n\nThe table above shows the heights, in inches, and the weights, in pounds, of four randomly selected men. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-165+4.6x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=70,$ and $\\text{MSE}=3.6,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.920)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=-165+4.6x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-165+4.6x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=-165.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.920)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.920.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(64-70)^{2}+(68-70)^{2}+(72-70)^{2}+(76-70)^{2} \\\\  & =36+4+4+36 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.920\\cdot \\sqrt{3.6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{70}^{2}}{80})}≈43.4\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=-165\\pm 43.4\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186554605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172120 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184883f1-85ea-5bd1-8ccd-88226f105da1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a86111c-7d04-5aa8-9f71-1296e12f0b47" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 1.2" :db/id 17592186553889} {:answer/id #uuid "4d17947d-e25a-5ba5-90a6-ea44648fe558" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 1.4" :db/id 17592186554019} {:answer/id #uuid "770545bb-976b-5b91-a99e-1a334310037a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 0.9" :db/id 17592186554109} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a2715c-198e-5860-9080-d610e3c815c7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 1.9" :db/id 17592186554228} {:answer/id #uuid "d95d02e9-5565-5b9e-8b79-42153a723931" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 2.2" :db/id 17592186554344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2a2715c-198e-5860-9080-d610e3c815c7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 1.9" :db/id 17592186554228} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce3aef4c-bd71-5e3d-813c-31f1dae5f512" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186554516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "939b2026-f5f3-5f3b-a15d-5ba46809dfda" :question/math-academy-id "q-159541" :question/problem "| **Height ($x$)** | **$60$** | **$62$** | **$66$** | **$68$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **IQ  ($y$)** | $110$ | $106$ | $108$ | $102$ |\n\nThe table above gives the heights, in inches, and the corresponding scores on an IQ test of four high school students. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=151.3-0.7x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=64,$ and $\\text{MSE}=7.7,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>4.303)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=151.3-0.7x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=151.3-0.7x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-0.7.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>4.303)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.025}=4.303.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(60-64)^{2}+(62-64)^{2}+(66-64)^{2}+(68-64)^{2} \\\\  & =16+4+4+16 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=4.303\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{7.7}{40}}≈1.9\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-0.7\\pm 1.9\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186554608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a42fd5c8-9721-5bf4-a1d7-b4b752b09708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Slope Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "278a0133-b0b4-5d1a-81b8-49903ddff8ed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.1" :db/id 17592186553944} {:answer/id #uuid "508865b1-77ef-551a-a36f-b6776f0a785e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.3" :db/id 17592186554025} {:answer/id #uuid "5db73e1c-4ac3-5171-b6a5-b6b7072792bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.6" :db/id 17592186554053} {:answer/id #uuid "80cf0bd5-fc8a-5eed-99c9-1117dae18dbf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.2" :db/id 17592186554127} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b5686d-bff3-5555-ae5d-0690a2f7e5ee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.4" :db/id 17592186554298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4b5686d-bff3-5555-ae5d-0690a2f7e5ee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.4" :db/id 17592186554298} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cfd90c1-e81f-5461-89ae-4d8978dbf8e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186554453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a0fa4ee-d860-5a97-91a9-24370dbc9085" :question/math-academy-id "q-159376" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.4+0.2x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{12},{y}_{12})$ of $n=12$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $0.7,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=18.9,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.228)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $10$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.4+0.2x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=2.4+0.2x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=0.2.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.228)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{10,0.025}=2.228.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.228\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.7}{18.9}}≈0.4\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=0.2\\pm 0.4\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186554613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bbee7f0-8803-54e8-874c-e570323ea33a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b209ccf-fac3-51cd-a24e-17d94342e720" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "151.3\\pm 183.6" :db/id 17592186554015} {:answer/id #uuid "7e50f150-1d03-5cdd-b228-0b83996b3433" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "151.3\\pm 193.8" :db/id 17592186554121} {:answer/id #uuid "9f303830-c78a-5b75-886c-4c70713deba0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "151.3\\pm 195.8" :db/id 17592186554221} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8ab9d8-4875-5fd3-8eff-2b8511dc815e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "151.3\\pm 175.2" :db/id 17592186554254} {:answer/id #uuid "e489115e-e8c1-5d14-a654-2c4be9ba3e26" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "151.3\\pm 173.1" :db/id 17592186554366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f303830-c78a-5b75-886c-4c70713deba0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "151.3\\pm 195.8" :db/id 17592186554221} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2fd0291-b761-5650-8e97-a72f4f5d21b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186554500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a77fcdca-ce6d-5371-a7a2-e886bf8d2462" :question/math-academy-id "q-159596" :question/problem "| **Height ($x$)** | **$60$** | **$62$** | **$66$** | **$68$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **IQ  ($y$)** | $110$ | $106$ | $108$ | $102$ |\n\nThe table above gives the heights, in inches, and the corresponding scores on an IQ test of four high school students. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=151.3-0.7x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=64,$ and $\\text{MSE}=7.7,$ find a $98%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>6.965)=0.01,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=151.3-0.7x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=151.3-0.7x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=151.3.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $98%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.98=0.02\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.01.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>6.965)=0.01.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.01}=6.965.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(60-64)^{2}+(62-64)^{2}+(66-64)^{2}+(68-64)^{2} \\\\  & =16+4+4+16 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=6.965\\cdot \\sqrt{7.7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{64}^{2}}{40})}≈195.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=151.3\\pm 195.8\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186554619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172120 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184883f1-85ea-5bd1-8ccd-88226f105da1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f5b1a94-4fcd-578f-9a4a-88a6dfb266da" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 0.4" :db/id 17592186554021} {:answer/id #uuid "82b33e0f-3da2-5ac3-84ca-7016f07baa0c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\pm 0.2" :db/id 17592186554131} {:answer/id #uuid "8eac7c38-eeac-5c2f-b55d-b4f3d6f40198" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\pm 0.4" :db/id 17592186554174} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd9b13d-b9d6-5c94-bbd9-97339852c840" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\pm 0.3" :db/id 17592186554210} {:answer/id #uuid "d4cd1b87-8a86-58a2-85a0-b0c99abe0e9a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.5" :db/id 17592186554331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bd9b13d-b9d6-5c94-bbd9-97339852c840" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\pm 0.3" :db/id 17592186554210} :answer-field/id #uuid "086dbd14-306d-5d79-b710-e38344655d09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186554433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2bc734f-9a4b-543f-ac01-84af782ea390" :question/math-academy-id "q-159518" :question/problem "| **Height ($x$)** | **$67$** | **$70$** | **$73$** | **$76$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Shoe size ($y$)** | $8.5$ | $9$ | $11$ | $13$ |\n\nThe table above shows the heights, in inches, and corresponding shoe sizes of four randomly selected men. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-26.6+0.5x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=71.5,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.338,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.  \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.92)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=-26.6+0.5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-26.6+0.5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=0.5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.92)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.92.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(67-71.5)^{2}+(70-71.5)^{2}+(73-71.5)^{2}+(76-71.5)^{2} \\\\  & =20.25+2.25+2.25+20.25 \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.92\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.338}{45}}≈0.3\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=0.5\\pm 0.3\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186554620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a42fd5c8-9721-5bf4-a1d7-b4b752b09708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Slope Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35ca07cb-5b22-501c-89bb-8a7db510076b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8\\pm 0.1" :db/id 17592186553966} {:answer/id #uuid "57ca5bd7-3818-541b-9864-07b885eecc3b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8\\pm 0.8" :db/id 17592186554041} {:answer/id #uuid "88714f63-0f79-5490-96fc-316d04ce4e71" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8\\pm 1.1" :db/id 17592186554152} {:answer/id #uuid "c637d326-e1bb-5f3b-830f-2255667712ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8\\pm 0.6" :db/id 17592186554303} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a2f733-69ad-5912-bdd1-d5b4f9a00a90" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8\\pm 0.7" :db/id 17592186554357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35ca07cb-5b22-501c-89bb-8a7db510076b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8\\pm 0.1" :db/id 17592186553966} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff946275-1749-5fd4-bd3f-74106924808a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186554538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c51957e8-149e-57cd-8ee2-fb199eb311cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-159379" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=1.5+3.8x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{18},{y}_{18})$ of $n=18$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $2.53,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=484,$ find a $90%$  confidence interval for the slope of the regression line. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.746)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $16$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=1.5+3.8x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=1.5+3.8x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=3.8.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.746)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{16,0.05}=1.746.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=1.746\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{2.53}{484}}≈0.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=3.8\\pm 0.1\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186554635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186105807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f6c0ca1-297a-5f25-bccf-c74d0c565c24" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Perimeter of a Polygon"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b28ace1-d2a4-5cf6-bd0b-d54c77a0ca31" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,cm" :db/id 17592186553892} {:answer/id #uuid "7e64ef2b-f990-540e-ab94-68e997b2823f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,cm" :db/id 17592186554123} {:answer/id #uuid "af42c5cf-a8f0-57e5-9328-b3b57686f2cb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,cm" :db/id 17592186554257} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ef95ce-2cfe-5f0c-9a38-55d3a654fdd3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,cm" :db/id 17592186554308} {:answer/id #uuid "d59ef339-bd7c-5cf1-8181-b3eea9c355cc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\,cm" :db/id 17592186554335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7ef95ce-2cfe-5f0c-9a38-55d3a654fdd3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,cm" :db/id 17592186554308} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac754ef8-8a92-57f1-83bb-568d71efea89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186554496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef8d594b-7df8-58f6-be78-cc667ee70380" :question/math-academy-id "q-67871" :question/problem "In a quadrilateral $ABCD,$ the measures of the sides are $AB=17\\,mm,$ $BC=14\\,mm,$ $CD=2\\,cm,$ and $DA=9\\,mm.$\nWhat is the perimeter of the quadrilateral?" :question/worked-solution "Most of the measures are given in $mm$ (millimeters), so we will start by converting all the measures to $mm$ and adding them up:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =17\\,mm+14\\,mm+\\overset{2\\,cm}{\\overset{⏞}{20\\,mm}}+9\\,mm \\\\  & =60\\,mm\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the perimeter is a large quantity of $\\text{mm},$ we will convert to $\\text{cm}:$\n\n$$\np=60\\,mm=6\\,cm.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186554638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186192682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7813df9e-9641-58a3-8537-e672e5fd079c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Intercept Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f6ae7e-79db-5a6a-9ada-f7fe426e2d90" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\pm 1.1" :db/id 17592186553937} {:answer/id #uuid "460c9059-6d0b-5494-9c9f-84881ad1f6f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\pm 2.1" :db/id 17592186554000} {:answer/id #uuid "868d4347-c27c-5b05-9b96-6bd24973a73c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\pm 1.3" :db/id 17592186554149} {:answer/id #uuid "b6fe6c79-a902-5fad-8af9-d0bbdf9b981a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\pm 2.5" :db/id 17592186554268} {:answer/id #uuid "e0159635-2a47-5355-a897-0b6a974a1d95" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\pm 2.8" :db/id 17592186554355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0159635-2a47-5355-a897-0b6a974a1d95" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\pm 2.8" :db/id 17592186554355} :answer-field/id #uuid "08326dd0-31c7-5254-9b62-aa5cdeae6bc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186554432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f24d9e6f-29a8-5c79-a6cb-e6a041461fd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159563" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.8-3.3x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{10},{y}_{10})$ of $n=10$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=2.7,$ the mean square error of the model is $3.6,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=82.5,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>3.355)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.8-3.3x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=2.8-3.3x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=2.8.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>3.355)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{8,0.005}=3.355.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.355\\cdot \\sqrt{3.6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{{2.7}^{2}}{82.5})}≈2.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.8\\pm 2.8\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186554639 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a42fd5c8-9721-5bf4-a1d7-b4b752b09708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Slope Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00f51304-e3b6-5932-ad51-bd19b9045d67" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6\\pm 0.1" :db/id 17592186553867} {:answer/id #uuid "0a29929b-8aeb-5e21-9031-f1ccdab60a1c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6\\pm 0.3" :db/id 17592186553887} {:answer/id #uuid "49fa42a9-46e5-5910-9c55-1b9972e31902" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6\\pm 1.1" :db/id 17592186554011} {:answer/id #uuid "d7bc35a1-e944-560a-a330-d31779067a9c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6\\pm 0.8" :db/id 17592186554340} {:answer/id #uuid "db665bb1-2442-5540-9fe3-cca6ff6884bc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6\\pm 0.5" :db/id 17592186554349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00f51304-e3b6-5932-ad51-bd19b9045d67" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6\\pm 0.1" :db/id 17592186553867} :answer-field/id #uuid "04602db9-708b-5fcf-bfdb-6de19a2849cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186554430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f28f1c0e-4ffd-5dee-98b7-1c475d5129ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-159398" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4-0.6x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{9},{y}_{9})$ of $n=9$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $1.16,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=240,$ find a $90%$  confidence interval for the slope of the regression line. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.895)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $7$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4-0.6x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=3.4-0.6x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-0.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.895)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{7,0.05}=1.895.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=1.895\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{1.16}{240}}≈0.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-0.6\\pm 0.1\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186555769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25d6bf9d-c36b-57f4-a4a3-d6d10d20a9fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id 17592186555078} {:answer/id #uuid "2d113435-7d4d-5e64-bf7a-6096265af4ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186555100} {:answer/id #uuid "97a99f95-7b5c-511f-b1dd-63628675fb7e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186555361} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5ab758-9dd9-5005-99ab-90aa7d7d9c95" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186555414} {:answer/id #uuid "b15e8623-fcd9-53a6-8695-28d9aee82949" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186555427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac5ab758-9dd9-5005-99ab-90aa7d7d9c95" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id 17592186555414} :answer-field/id #uuid "89806148-2b04-5dff-9c9d-9aecabad8125" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186555707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1cf3361e-986e-5750-bf9f-73762dff3b0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106771" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{1}{2},$ ${u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id 17592186555795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "726c8472-17c5-5d1f-9c91-e04c085b468c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-49" :db/id 17592186555258} {:answer/id #uuid "76a16c2d-6a05-575e-a83a-271e0b390c85" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-42" :db/id 17592186555272} {:answer/id #uuid "8bed455b-9692-567b-9f57-f93f82c4503d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id 17592186555328} {:answer/id #uuid "bc2c0f77-5481-5f8a-b556-ff5507543ca5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-35" :db/id 17592186555446} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb28c55-d1c7-5022-adb7-21280b2bfcb7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id 17592186555546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc2c0f77-5481-5f8a-b556-ff5507543ca5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-35" :db/id 17592186555446} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e192a93-84a5-59c0-884b-235a349cd678" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186555683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42d466fd-6c03-513d-b916-3193ae5d935b" :question/math-academy-id "q-106766" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=7,{u}_{22}=-5,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=-35.$"} {:db/id 17592186555807 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26a8adb1-6585-5d88-b2a9-a4c66146a7ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186555080} {:answer/id #uuid "ad69335a-fa27-5de3-98db-67ed27d046b7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186555417} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd4791a-30a0-5cef-b04a-83ca7b826af1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186555581} {:answer/id #uuid "f3907a7e-ba96-5506-8915-537e18e2116e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186555598} {:answer/id #uuid "f77569c8-f2a6-5605-a6af-f9b44459f38c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186555613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3907a7e-ba96-5506-8915-537e18e2116e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186555598} :answer-field/id #uuid "5605219e-b32b-50d6-ae8e-13893dac1527" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186555678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55b8ab85-3682-5003-bc82-d3e9f5248d03" :question/math-academy-id "q-106755" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} {:db/id 17592186555856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d7c47e5-bc18-5759-9dba-bef203ec6a76" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186555101} {:answer/id #uuid "382d6998-098c-5ac8-a4eb-2cf87144df8e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id 17592186555121} {:answer/id #uuid "49cdf79c-58f3-5591-94f0-0d48ea2d4f6c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id 17592186555158} {:answer/id #uuid "4da3e47c-22f5-5965-908e-6f3b266993d1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id 17592186555174} {:answer/id #uuid "c847da6e-0099-5446-9b32-bda4703a56ba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id 17592186555480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49cdf79c-58f3-5591-94f0-0d48ea2d4f6c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id 17592186555158} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bb19108-7f19-5591-ae3c-6544894088fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186555669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d20ab7a7-2299-5eca-8c7e-9709ddaf034c" :question/math-academy-id "q-32547" :question/problem "Given that\n $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-3,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-3.$"} {:db/id 17592186557211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124550 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5e23379-0f5d-5691-b10f-5125325ffa70" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Transformed Square Root Function Given Some Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "813db01e-cf21-5292-addd-723299c9c19b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2x-8}-1" :db/id 17592186556728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "813db01e-cf21-5292-addd-723299c9c19b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2x-8}-1" :db/id 17592186556728} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5ff4797-7c2a-514d-aa3d-bf7bbb62b553" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186557160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0bb9e173-3e0e-5cad-b727-d2df13c7ef82" :question/math-academy-id "q-277438" :question/problem "Let $f(x)=a\\sqrt{2x+b}+c.$ Now consider the following properties of$f(x):$\n\n- The domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in [4,\\infty ).$\n\n- The range is $f(x)\\in [-1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The point $(6,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x).$\n\nTherefore, our function is\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each property in turn.\n\n- The domain of $f(x)=a\\sqrt{2x+b}+c\\,$ is determined by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+b & \\ge 0 \\\\ 2x & \\ge -b \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{b}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the domain is $x\\in [4,\\infty ),$ which is equivalent to $x\\ge 4.$ Hence, we obtain\n\n$$\n-\\frac{b}{2}=4\\,⟹\\,b=-8.\n$$\n\n- Since the range is unbounded on the right-hand side, we know that $a>0.$ Furthermore, since we are given that the lowest point in the range is $-1,$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{a\\sqrt{2x-8}}_{⏟}}_{\\ge 0}+c\\ge -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $c=-1.$\n\n- Finally, since the point $(6,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =3 \\\\ a\\sqrt{2(6)-8}-1 & =3 \\\\ 2a-1 & =3 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our function is $f(x)=2\\sqrt{2x-8}-1.$"} {:db/id 17592186557216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21a0e737-5697-57a4-a73d-edbc478b30a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id 17592186556507} {:answer/id #uuid "45a303ab-287c-5d20-b61f-e565124eda57" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186556583} {:answer/id #uuid "65c8beba-7f48-5893-abed-5629555ee374" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id 17592186556668} {:answer/id #uuid "ca91840c-4fe1-5797-837e-cf0d04ba1b59" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186556917} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b5e34c-4678-5dca-b523-4a58e93795c8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id 17592186557007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65c8beba-7f48-5893-abed-5629555ee374" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id 17592186556668} :answer-field/id #uuid "640612ec-cca7-54d8-80ce-3588c7e329a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186557123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "220c02c6-9021-54d4-b05d-5f454f3a5b70" :question/math-academy-id "q-95938" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{2}-3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}-2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{2}-3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}-2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=18{q}_{1}+6{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=5{q}_{1}+10{q}_{2}+5{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=4+5+5=14.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186557222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172028 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26871e46-51ca-5680-a064-bcee4ac984fd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50ba80ef-f04e-50df-b6d3-a54349425c28" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $5$ and $4$" :db/id 17592186556614} {:answer/id #uuid "99191248-3fa5-597a-8b54-a603b1f3f7cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\frac{1}{5}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$" :db/id 17592186556797} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e84b15-e7c8-5fd4-aa6e-1f803de91be4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A single point" :db/id 17592186556826} {:answer/id #uuid "da3da08e-ab18-5b95-a7d3-b32ef06eaf72" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id 17592186556972} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4ed344-c362-572c-a34e-eb57da043aa3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id 17592186556977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1e84b15-e7c8-5fd4-aa6e-1f803de91be4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A single point" :db/id 17592186556826} :answer-field/id #uuid "2872dfa0-d71b-51c3-93f9-e6e87fc08e6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186557091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "27762923-ddbc-55ca-b261-ca5c61feb064" :question/math-academy-id "q-95396" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{16}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{16}\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{64}) & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{64})-\\frac{4}{64} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8})^{2}-\\frac{1}{16} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8})^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{z}_{1}^{2}+4{z}_{2}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{5}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{4}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}})^{2}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nA sum of squares is equal to zero if and only if each summand is zero, so the given quadratic equation represents a single point."} {:db/id 17592186557247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4d10f94-98d4-53d8-a35c-e65a06dd2c55" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using a Single Elementary Row Operation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a55d4c8-ba65-5de7-84a2-4ee94b361461" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id 17592186556487} {:answer/id #uuid "6294613e-6b1e-58a0-a797-a8bc314e0e5c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186556655} {:answer/id #uuid "82e69121-ee24-55ee-be9b-a4fee20e1b36" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186556734} {:answer/id #uuid "92f08831-eeff-53f3-8e72-199165e34fe2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id 17592186556784} {:answer/id #uuid "b6059bbc-7cd6-5c6e-a838-7b79406bffd2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id 17592186556879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6059bbc-7cd6-5c6e-a838-7b79406bffd2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id 17592186556879} :answer-field/id #uuid "44515a13-3042-5dad-a7e1-fc7dbbca7de6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186557102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c02183d-b4b3-51bb-97f0-443673f54a1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-32571" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=-\\frac{3}{2},{u}_{33}=-1,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=\\frac{3}{2}.$"} {:db/id 17592186557253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172028 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26871e46-51ca-5680-a064-bcee4ac984fd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03c543b2-c742-5216-a976-704265096bf3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of intersecting lines" :db/id 17592186556437} {:answer/id #uuid "4d58c25d-4003-58d6-ba24-34ce3e87a8a6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id 17592186556600} {:answer/id #uuid "e2716f9d-9b38-5af5-a1db-0a58828ce254" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id 17592186556989} {:answer/id #uuid "f08c1f85-9679-5c12-98ff-95ec5e74f12d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id 17592186557023} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b9bfec-c82b-508d-8c85-f0390ed2dd71" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $9$ and $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id 17592186557032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f08c1f85-9679-5c12-98ff-95ec5e74f12d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id 17592186557023} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0e2b3fe-1fd8-5dda-acb7-cbb560deb0ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186557174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "584e6ba7-6a66-5994-b1b0-75bfa6a71313" :question/math-academy-id "q-95392" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=\\frac{119}{8}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=\\frac{119}{8}\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{1} & =\\frac{119}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{1} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{1})-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{1}+\\frac{25}{16}-\\frac{25}{16})-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{({y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{8}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{({y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{z}_{1}^{2}-3{z}_{2}^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $\\sqrt{6}.$"} {:db/id 17592186557260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "950c1ed7-f804-54f5-bf97-16bf2d1a663b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19864df4-2d2b-59b7-8b29-57ccd76568d5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3.5-x" :db/id 17592186556484} {:answer/id #uuid "2ffa906f-87d9-5316-b4b0-5c81c75eda13" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3.5-x" :db/id 17592186556532} {:answer/id #uuid "c73853c3-d2b8-5386-8f71-11b0dfc356a4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1+3.5x" :db/id 17592186556909} {:answer/id #uuid "ca337b13-8de7-56cb-a6fd-dfe6db160da2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-3.5x" :db/id 17592186556914} {:answer/id #uuid "d6194152-573b-5c08-a9b7-e672113befaa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3.5+x" :db/id 17592186556951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6194152-573b-5c08-a9b7-e672113befaa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3.5+x" :db/id 17592186556951} :answer-field/id #uuid "32652aaf-8a9a-5672-9fc6-21a88c374b1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186557096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "685118c5-b7fa-5fb3-b5f9-06f8a56c7a87" :question/math-academy-id "q-139647" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $2$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{104}[\\begin{aligned}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{104}[\\begin{aligned}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=3.5+x.$"} {:db/id 17592186557275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133905 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6acfe386-f7e4-54b1-9701-6ff5905b4536" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Regression Line in Terms of Matrices and Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0878219e-c2fd-51d0-8c0f-8887e9b9bcc2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -3 & 0 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556448} {:answer/id #uuid "5fda055d-c731-555a-bf72-502387bb59a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556646} {:answer/id #uuid "74dde18c-c7c1-59de-833d-07f07f975058" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-15 & -6 \\\\ -10 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556695} {:answer/id #uuid "8987fbb9-4e36-5a31-a1b1-46618db9cc66" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -15 \\\\ 1 & -10 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556757} {:answer/id #uuid "f2120fda-c38d-5064-839f-093917dee005" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -15 & -10 & -5 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186557027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8987fbb9-4e36-5a31-a1b1-46618db9cc66" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -15 \\\\ 1 & -10 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556757} :answer-field/id #uuid "3086de9f-7992-5290-a78e-b92ab10911d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186557095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fc30a95-4823-5308-bc3d-c38eeb6c9b5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-139576" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-15$** | **$-10$** | **$-5$** | **$0$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$-15$** | **$-10$** | **$-5$** | **$0$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -15 \\\\ 1 & -10 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186557281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229131 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95223ee8-46d8-58c2-9e3b-017adb2789e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c917360-af2a-547f-83e0-3ae046b64006" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))" :db/id 17592186556681} {:answer/id #uuid "9a272e7c-5ccc-58a1-bc0e-68ec31a26ddd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))" :db/id 17592186556802} {:answer/id #uuid "d2839194-a132-5ab8-875a-6230951d4cfa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))" :db/id 17592186556938} {:answer/id #uuid "e345ffd9-c7a8-5122-b489-3449540bacce" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))" :db/id 17592186556992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2839194-a132-5ab8-875a-6230951d4cfa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))" :db/id 17592186556938} :answer-field/id #uuid "46896807-998c-5933-b1eb-99ca4e046406" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186557105} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b53c207-98c0-5e8d-8dea-4637e64bc338" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t+s))" :db/id 17592186556492} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff80451-645a-5833-911f-06d713dda45d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))" :db/id 17592186556609} {:answer/id #uuid "8bb6b832-e503-5169-a52a-89ac629263af" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t+s))" :db/id 17592186556765} {:answer/id #uuid "f6eb6cc2-da68-5a29-9a26-d0470fcf0791" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s))" :db/id 17592186557041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ff80451-645a-5833-911f-06d713dda45d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))" :db/id 17592186556609} :answer-field/id #uuid "9159810c-d2c6-59d8-84ab-dc3766a59aa8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186557147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e6e37b0-420c-5604-b7d4-9e71462bd5eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-334923" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}],\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"180px\">$2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td><td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"180px\">${e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td><td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186557283 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1274a3e1-55b9-552d-b003-12310c1f94da" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556469} {:answer/id #uuid "420e7c72-67c3-54bb-8ebc-2aa8890493a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556577} {:answer/id #uuid "65290f11-7840-570f-a534-0fa4fca0ae31" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556664} {:answer/id #uuid "7f46a36f-9fe5-5805-871e-6936924e326a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556722} {:answer/id #uuid "c548e49e-a7cd-5be9-b97c-4c57df80a6e1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65290f11-7840-570f-a534-0fa4fca0ae31" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556664} :answer-field/id #uuid "9220e030-d050-59e1-aeee-1e7f3bb39fe6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186557150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9735b81a-0c65-5842-9564-1b88b92db55e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32723" :question/problem "Given that\n$M=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $M=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $M$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $M$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{5}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot M \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot M \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}M=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}M & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}M & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ M & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ M & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ M & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ M & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186557289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229131 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95223ee8-46d8-58c2-9e3b-017adb2789e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "646e6165-7c3a-5090-b755-0054dbd03146" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "te^{4t}" :db/id 17592186556660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "646e6165-7c3a-5090-b755-0054dbd03146" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "te^{4t}" :db/id 17592186556660} :answer-field/id #uuid "174e2ccc-42c8-5609-b37a-59d23929885c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186557078} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aca58c0a-4a82-5bea-94ab-b3068980a554" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{4t}" :db/id 17592186556855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aca58c0a-4a82-5bea-94ab-b3068980a554" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{4t}" :db/id 17592186556855} :answer-field/id #uuid "5604af13-50f7-5867-8a20-353ebbce0164" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186557114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f8ace62-6215-5f44-93f8-e30dfc0372e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-334828" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$(t-s){e}^{4t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{4t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{4s}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186557294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133905 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6acfe386-f7e4-54b1-9701-6ff5905b4536" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Regression Line in Terms of Matrices and Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee8c8a2-5158-57a0-ad6b-eab622688ddb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 & 5 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556457} {:answer/id #uuid "1b31021f-f26a-5c88-9e24-49dab0b11f94" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 11 \\\\ 1 & 14 \\\\ 1 & 20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556490} {:answer/id #uuid "593ac918-30c0-55f9-9a45-8bca67e90e57" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556633} {:answer/id #uuid "76e17d5a-5046-5304-9556-d61074ae42ff" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 3 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 11 & 14 & 20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556700} {:answer/id #uuid "b96491c1-3d2f-5012-a011-55c9cccc0e67" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "593ac918-30c0-55f9-9a45-8bca67e90e57" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556633} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3f983e4-f068-54e8-9c9e-8a106c8c9d76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186557177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1f3a877-5233-5d11-bd7f-f8b8db99c5be" :question/math-academy-id "q-139575" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-1$** | **$1$** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $4$ | $8$ | $11$ | $14$ | $20$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$-1$** | **$1$** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $4$ | $8$ | $11$ | $14$ | $20$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186557314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4d10f94-98d4-53d8-a35c-e65a06dd2c55" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using a Single Elementary Row Operation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f45f559-94dd-563f-b6eb-86c40bcad18c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556606} {:answer/id #uuid "6135e766-b82e-5f99-857b-dc1d26563ce1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556651} {:answer/id #uuid "a75d4c60-2325-5df0-b74a-a5a6b37e1816" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556842} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc248cf-0e7d-5338-ad36-68399abd2b84" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186557011} {:answer/id #uuid "f53189c6-65a6-5c30-87b8-d21e34f090bf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186557034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a75d4c60-2325-5df0-b74a-a5a6b37e1816" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556842} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6d2c7d3-a8d8-5bda-af43-0a8e471ca20e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186557184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d742eff4-edcb-531d-a1ae-99b88035cdf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-32585" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186557317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c91fb2b-5607-555d-b897-f0e76765dfa6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lower Triangular Matrix Given the Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f0d8e4c-11e0-585b-98a8-e1e65e946a9f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556531} {:answer/id #uuid "360d0293-cec9-58b0-9fa0-f3f513479858" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556547} {:answer/id #uuid "840aef6c-7174-5636-bec4-0b4c79dba19b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556740} {:answer/id #uuid "c713eb9d-b36e-5847-8500-ce5007937f65" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556908} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c1dda6-8000-59cb-8a0d-65f0284741cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "360d0293-cec9-58b0-9fa0-f3f513479858" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186556547} :answer-field/id #uuid "76fb5c7d-850b-5730-b4f9-b378b453ae17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186557134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e07c46c7-d38b-5617-a5f3-42fc8a91e6b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-75098" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $H$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $H$ has the LU factorization $H=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ H & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186557325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229131 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95223ee8-46d8-58c2-9e3b-017adb2789e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5b356a7-3b0b-5f1f-bec8-e6b826f26b86" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos t" :db/id 17592186556876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5b356a7-3b0b-5f1f-bec8-e6b826f26b86" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos t" :db/id 17592186556876} :answer-field/id #uuid "17254fb8-737c-5411-ba14-63458b8c5c1e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186557077} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c887629a-5ab0-5f5a-85b8-798e29da3914" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(t-s)" :db/id 17592186556912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c887629a-5ab0-5f5a-85b8-798e29da3914" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(t-s)" :db/id 17592186556912} :answer-field/id #uuid "adcf2066-3be2-5f5c-966d-7fbfbab80820" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186557162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f491a1e5-e94d-5b1b-9331-94aa4176665c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334856" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186558485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-8acb-ea91958d8725" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56d62606-600b-56ec-a3fd-a47d37a5ff2b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id 17592186558030} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8b2bf8-b3b8-512a-a46d-88c0558b5fd8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186558086} {:answer/id #uuid "70c7791e-868a-52d8-ae41-1d3e92d6e599" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186558089} {:answer/id #uuid "ad64393f-6d2d-5220-9e5f-52726b3743a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186558210} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f6dbe2-f389-5865-a28c-083f626a394d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id 17592186558292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70c7791e-868a-52d8-ae41-1d3e92d6e599" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186558089} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e40607e-9b99-5e38-8f0d-dd955650b71c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186558437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ce9fe2d-58e8-5131-a802-985ae9f0bd60" :question/math-academy-id "q-163103" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y),$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=axy+b{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y) \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =0 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈0+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(0\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(1)\\cdot xy+2\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1xy+1{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1=2.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186558486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d216cc0e-58a7-5fab-b5fe-60c7656338cc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Numbers in the Matrix L of an LU Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c7cc88e-18df-5fe3-af5a-38a991c0dae8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186557895} {:answer/id #uuid "3d67b920-459f-5f08-84f8-3e459ffa4fc2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id 17592186557992} {:answer/id #uuid "6e44fa06-efa6-5be5-a23f-d0dbed3c820f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186558085} {:answer/id #uuid "9835d2c2-72ad-51c4-94ef-97435599e83c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id 17592186558162} {:answer/id #uuid "d8e91c9d-43f7-5e67-a37c-3beaf1413e49" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id 17592186558295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e44fa06-efa6-5be5-a23f-d0dbed3c820f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186558085} :answer-field/id #uuid "045659f1-2a9a-529d-b1c4-0756b52fca76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186558365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0dbf0280-1ed5-5f59-85a1-40459f1b28eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-105600" :question/problem "$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & \\boxed{} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of the entry ${l}_{32}$ in the matrix $L?$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make zeros below the main diagonal, we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+1{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+1{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the position ${a}_{32}$ is already $0.$ Therefore, the entry ${l}_{32}$ of the matrix $L$ is\n\n$$\n{l}_{32}=0.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186558492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa5bf185-de29-5185-ba57-c64250332dfa" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Elements of a Polynomial Regression Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7093db9c-29cd-58b2-bc04-0f2e75da08e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558088} {:answer/id #uuid "8b352bd0-126e-50a2-bef5-7ad0f9b65eb8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558141} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b70d6f-3a7e-54f3-b795-e1e558102083" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -20 & (-20)^{2} \\\\ 1 & -10 & (-10)^{2} \\\\ 1 & 40 & {40}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 20 & {20}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 10 & {10}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558240} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fd9ca4-29c6-5fdc-a096-cd7801d7ab8d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -20 & (-20)^{2} \\\\ 2 & -10 & (-10)^{2} \\\\ 3 & 40 & {40}^{2} \\\\ 4 & 20 & {20}^{2} \\\\ 5 & 10 & {10}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558293} {:answer/id #uuid "feed150c-2bb3-55f3-aad3-8f093d70fbc1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-20 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ -10 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 40 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 20 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 10 & 5 & {5}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7093db9c-29cd-58b2-bc04-0f2e75da08e3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558088} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4a46285-ecad-5138-9438-e49fce60ad95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186558444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17de842c-8376-5d01-8ddc-4fc760401c11" :question/math-academy-id "q-139654" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-20$ | $-10$ | $40$ | $20$ | $10$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-20$ | $-10$ | $40$ | $20$ | $10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186558497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d216cc0e-58a7-5fab-b5fe-60c7656338cc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Numbers in the Matrix L of an LU Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "033ae31a-ff94-5015-b529-58a259bb2d45" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186557876} {:answer/id #uuid "056f1d44-7ab1-528a-b2c5-7987a32eaa8a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id 17592186557880} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad04980-5d56-5284-8b53-de4d6eaf5c19" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186558073} {:answer/id #uuid "76c68f21-c07e-5ea8-822e-0b3aeab6d050" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186558098} {:answer/id #uuid "9e1f37c0-03ac-5f5c-90d5-c290d829c90d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id 17592186558180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76c68f21-c07e-5ea8-822e-0b3aeab6d050" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id 17592186558098} :answer-field/id #uuid "e16ab84f-f9e2-51b5-a8b8-31571c55bdac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186558467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1fff8cfc-a14b-5392-9c5a-4c6f022200cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-32810" :question/problem "$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 10 & -2 \\\\ 20 & 40 & -15\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\boxed{} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 20 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of the entry ${l}_{31}$ in the matrix $L?$" :question/worked-solution "We want to make $0$ at the position ${a}_{31}$ using Gaussian elimination. To do this, we take the $3$rd row of $A,$ and we add the $1$st row of $A$ multiplied by $-4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 10 & -2 \\\\ 20 & 40 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 10 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe entry ${l}_{31}$ of the matrix $L$ is the factor next to ${R}_{1},$ taken with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{l}_{31}=-(-4)=4.\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186558501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7634b3ac-f9e6-514e-87ba-e78590c9fa53" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 Linear System Using LU Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53492803-619c-5738-8780-eb85ef78082a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186558022} {:answer/id #uuid "5b75e368-dcfe-5035-a89a-0de98b0c81f4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id 17592186558040} {:answer/id #uuid "641e7890-d858-55b5-b90c-31018a57894b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id 17592186558059} {:answer/id #uuid "9acf41c5-2aee-5e2e-9787-52e0c514e5e2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id 17592186558168} {:answer/id #uuid "b4885fca-3afa-5082-a83c-7abaa4f102b9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id 17592186558224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53492803-619c-5738-8780-eb85ef78082a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186558022} :answer-field/id #uuid "791205e6-0e35-509a-8763-c44c2a7914e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186558418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b4d31f2-6228-5e16-8ece-d3de14298eac" :question/math-academy-id "q-32563" :question/problem "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=b=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-4 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-4$ and ${y}_{2}=-5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=y=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{1}=(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot (-4)=6.$"} {:db/id 17592186558502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133809 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eaee4e9-7be5-5c35-bc88-89697a15d75a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression That Passes Through the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "355030be-a7ab-5e66-af4a-2540e2e92aec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186557976} {:answer/id #uuid "4e101075-c3bc-5a40-a961-3e6a8881af79" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558015} {:answer/id #uuid "983b3d98-13ef-59b2-9f74-39c62ed79b05" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 14 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558163} {:answer/id #uuid "e4230e0d-665b-57f7-b630-19e515ba697b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558321} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb8eb4a-4fc6-503e-ad79-291909f464e2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "983b3d98-13ef-59b2-9f74-39c62ed79b05" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 14 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558163} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb4db6ef-abed-5126-b67a-ee73a47d1698" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186558448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ba6b722-d7db-5552-b536-7b5939d80058" :question/math-academy-id "q-139931" :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$8$** | **$5$** | **$4$** | **$2$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$2$** | **$5$** | **$9$** | **$14$** | **$20$** |\n| $y$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $-6$ | $-8$ |\n\n  \n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$8$** | **$5$** | **$4$** | **$2$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$2$** | **$5$** | **$9$** | **$14$** | **$20$** |\n| $y$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-4$ | $-6$ | $-8$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 9 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 14 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 20\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186558520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e33c97-0499-5829-9886-be30b00989ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fbd6c21-9bc8-5a63-b917-acba0f0377f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 15 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 19 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 25 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 17 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 32\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186557899} {:answer/id #uuid "11ac4880-ef29-5253-9d90-8376b6d5f7b8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186557905} {:answer/id #uuid "3b4d0f7e-c425-51ce-b954-b943e0a277aa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 15 \\\\ -1 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 25 \\\\ 1 & 17 \\\\ 2 & 32\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186557989} {:answer/id #uuid "7c67cb14-36da-537b-8226-70260c87485e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558106} {:answer/id #uuid "b615d1bf-c1a4-501f-9b7d-436d564b456a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 19 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 17 \\\\ 32\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c67cb14-36da-537b-8226-70260c87485e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558106} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c1ad98-6b3b-5b11-a8ca-da9be5dce3ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186558380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "637f51dc-9306-5135-a973-8a82796a3f19" :question/math-academy-id "q-139809" :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$15$** | **$19$** | **$25$** | **$17$** | **$32$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-8$ | $-3$ | $6$ | $13$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multi linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$15$** | **$19$** | **$25$** | **$17$** | **$32$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-8$ | $-3$ | $6$ | $13$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 15 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 19 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 25 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 17 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 32\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186558528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7634b3ac-f9e6-514e-87ba-e78590c9fa53" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 Linear System Using LU Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13c02160-8ba1-5939-bc73-8002657c6b03" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id 17592186557912} {:answer/id #uuid "23f077b2-98bc-5e17-b96a-87b4b7d0879e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id 17592186557942} {:answer/id #uuid "6de90c59-9c12-5214-92b7-f0abb8a7ca70" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id 17592186558082} {:answer/id #uuid "cadf1f12-7d08-5b35-add1-a8807ac05198" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id 17592186558272} {:answer/id #uuid "fade0afc-801b-5fae-b3a9-b016601c272f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186558358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fade0afc-801b-5fae-b3a9-b016601c272f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id 17592186558358} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4f155d4-3ea5-53d0-a6f6-8b69b1f92207" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186558468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "758b84b6-7328-584d-83ee-1df88702770d" :question/math-academy-id "q-107500" :question/problem "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{y}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=4 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=4$ and ${y}_{2}=5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=y=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=5$ and ${x}_{1}=3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{y}_{2}=3+5=8.$"} {:db/id 17592186558529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa9fbb8-b2bc-5c93-bb07-bc8c7ef7c115" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution of an Intermediate System Using the LU Factorization of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14beea86-f47c-5a8c-9bec-dff707ad6098" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186557916} {:answer/id #uuid "2e855c15-8cb8-5eb3-ad1a-06b9a164a41f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186557963} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb700b3-de35-5eef-a9b9-8f936285f7aa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558175} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd0b1f6-0564-5713-91c8-8b44037f4570" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558178} {:answer/id #uuid "a2fda1e6-ea2c-5685-8e6d-c69133ac2877" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dd0b1f6-0564-5713-91c8-8b44037f4570" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558178} :answer-field/id #uuid "72d3d84a-5d38-5708-8891-cf429ce55b84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186558415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "772e6d55-2cc0-5849-b50b-b1451b60fe6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-107185" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-1 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-3 \\\\ 8{y}_{1}-5{y}_{2}+{y}_{3}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-1, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-3+2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-3+2(-1) \\\\  & =-5, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =8-8{y}_{1}+5{y}_{2} \\\\  & =8-8(-1)+5(-5) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].$"} {:db/id 17592186558544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229131 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95223ee8-46d8-58c2-9e3b-017adb2789e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d59687fe-7fee-536b-ae53-62aa4e2062c6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3t}" :db/id 17592186558287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d59687fe-7fee-536b-ae53-62aa4e2062c6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3t}" :db/id 17592186558287} :answer-field/id #uuid "bda1e7e4-3c88-5a06-909e-308c1b22da65" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186558451} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81eb1113-b696-53f5-ab1f-005ae9690189" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3(t-1)}" :db/id 17592186558118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81eb1113-b696-53f5-ab1f-005ae9690189" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3(t-1)}" :db/id 17592186558118} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc834a44-d315-5323-8d37-5104d3268aa9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186558476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a3f4d85-0623-5fd9-9cc9-8c8eac44c1ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-334846" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$(t-2){e}^{3(t-1)}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-1)} & (t-1){e}^{3(t-1)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-1)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-2){e}^{3(t-1)} \\\\ {e}^{3(t-1)}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-2){e}^{3(t-1)} \\\\ {e}^{3(t-1)}\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186558545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa5bf185-de29-5185-ba57-c64250332dfa" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Elements of a Polynomial Regression Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088f31e3-f49b-508d-9b4f-3b50e9543366" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 8 & {8}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186557885} {:answer/id #uuid "0b3876e4-0be8-5285-af5a-7c1a626b5415" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 13 & {13}^{2} \\\\ 1 & -4 & (-4)^{2} \\\\ 1 & -18 & (-18)^{2} \\\\ 1 & -10 & (-10)^{2} \\\\ 1 & 11 & {11}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186557893} {:answer/id #uuid "6b09ea0f-5f4a-5921-ae4b-58da76e0f97b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 13 & {13}^{2} & {13}^{3} \\\\ 1 & -4 & (-4)^{2} & (-4)^{3} \\\\ 1 & -18 & (-18)^{2} & (-18)^{3} \\\\ 1 & -10 & (-10)^{2} & (-10)^{3} \\\\ 1 & 11 & {11}^{2} & {11}^{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558074} {:answer/id #uuid "9bda7b77-5680-5912-9bae-2e271d090576" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}13 & 0 & {0}^{2} \\\\ -4 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ -18 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ -10 & 6 & {6}^{2} \\\\ 11 & 8 & {8}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558172} {:answer/id #uuid "d015b672-4778-52e8-bb4e-468671c0d33d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2} & {6}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 8 & {8}^{2} & {8}^{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "088f31e3-f49b-508d-9b4f-3b50e9543366" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 8 & {8}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186557885} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cd99673-6502-5535-8b37-2664b2ceabf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186558391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b6fa50f-a6b0-54a4-b38d-0880cb666278" :question/math-academy-id "q-139656" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $13$ | $-4$ | $-18$ | $-10$ | $11$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $13$ | $-4$ | $-18$ | $-10$ | $11$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 8 & {8}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186558552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133809 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eaee4e9-7be5-5c35-bc88-89697a15d75a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression That Passes Through the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05bb58d1-77b6-50fd-ab99-d2d4d323a0a2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & 2 \\\\ 10 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 14 \\\\ 3 & 20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186557881} {:answer/id #uuid "284566a0-3da3-55ca-bb77-0ed809540044" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186557946} {:answer/id #uuid "90617472-e5bd-5a87-8f7d-f813914f93ec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 15 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 10 & 12 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 14 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 19 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 22\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558149} {:answer/id #uuid "d131d352-3af2-576b-b311-b8cb4da6aa76" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 15 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558281} {:answer/id #uuid "d4768944-f965-5824-9add-c65e25504f8a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 19 \\\\ 22\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90617472-e5bd-5a87-8f7d-f813914f93ec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 15 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 10 & 12 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 14 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 19 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 22\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186558149} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8486375-f7f7-5ba1-a597-785f266e09a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186558473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7822300-5e13-5e08-b60e-08535cff4002" :question/math-academy-id "q-159734" :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$15$** | **$10$** | **$8$** | **$7$** | **$3$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$8$** | **$12$** | **$14$** | **$19$** | **$22$** |\n| $y$ | $4$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ | $-10$ |\n\n  \n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$15$** | **$10$** | **$8$** | **$7$** | **$3$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$8$** | **$12$** | **$14$** | **$19$** | **$22$** |\n| $y$ | $4$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ | $-10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 15 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 10 & 12 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 14 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 19 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 22\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186558563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186229131 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95223ee8-46d8-58c2-9e3b-017adb2789e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "325e2bea-79c6-5d0e-86e5-ca3c08841f5a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t+s)" :db/id 17592186557971} {:answer/id #uuid "8e624ff8-2ee1-5b70-ba81-5895a41c4248" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s)" :db/id 17592186558145} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ecf254-834a-5dbe-8b2d-44e08553fb02" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s)" :db/id 17592186558228} {:answer/id #uuid "f6893209-e8c4-5a46-9f8a-822f69bcdaf7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t+s)" :db/id 17592186558350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4ecf254-834a-5dbe-8b2d-44e08553fb02" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s)" :db/id 17592186558228} :answer-field/id #uuid "dadfc882-5148-5c0c-af21-a9e418a22bd6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186558464} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69fe7ba5-1b53-56bf-9a9f-f5c568562239" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)" :db/id 17592186558069} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6829ae-e7c1-5884-afa0-116dfe94231c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)" :db/id 17592186558142} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7f1da6-be8e-55ae-86e2-b2f30e2b8500" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)" :db/id 17592186558212} {:answer/id #uuid "c87fcfa6-f6e3-5441-b2a1-174c935027eb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)" :db/id 17592186558267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69fe7ba5-1b53-56bf-9a9f-f5c568562239" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)" :db/id 17592186558069} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffb55f3c-2681-5734-9a74-f7bf06c53fcc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186558478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb2daa37-d9b1-570d-9a42-38ab64cc0e59" :question/math-academy-id "q-334859" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"120px\">$\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td><td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"120px\">{{field-1}} </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td><td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ {e}^{s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(t-s) \\\\ {e}^{s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186558570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119884 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c159d750-ab1f-538b-b8cc-ae66ef1ae414" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Numbers in the Matrix U of an LU Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a7fdb4-2b1b-532b-85f0-f8df64e8691a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id 17592186557917} {:answer/id #uuid "1b4dfd2e-6508-5fbd-9bc5-a8e2a4a7b39d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id 17592186557928} {:answer/id #uuid "1bfb90a2-2e70-5671-9927-bd4cc934f12d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186557929} {:answer/id #uuid "4e4b5b3e-ad7c-5cf1-918a-e39ed5f2a6b5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186558016} {:answer/id #uuid "c77be089-5efb-5143-95d8-be5cfb388426" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id 17592186558264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c77be089-5efb-5143-95d8-be5cfb388426" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id 17592186558264} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c7a758e-cf09-5976-8b76-1ff485bab836" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186558382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "daed3fa2-ebc7-5729-abaf-5914fd69e7b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-106962" :question/problem "$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 28 & -8 & -3 \\\\ -49 & 37 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of ${u}_{33}$ in the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make zeros below the main diagonal, we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 28 & -8 & -3 \\\\ -49 & 37 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -49 & 37 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 16 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${u}_{33}=-4.$"} {:db/id 17592186558571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7634b3ac-f9e6-514e-87ba-e78590c9fa53" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 Linear System Using LU Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ea7e0be-9a1f-5523-b787-ed5f453051d8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id 17592186557964} {:answer/id #uuid "30c57b85-780d-5e2f-88d6-2b0bf8507810" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id 17592186557967} {:answer/id #uuid "6b2c83f9-30fa-58a9-ab6d-b815a4faa3cc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id 17592186558075} {:answer/id #uuid "978ea838-5cf8-51dc-9cc3-827714cd57ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id 17592186558160} {:answer/id #uuid "fad0f84d-6ada-5fb9-b0e6-bd2a4abb6d9b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id 17592186558357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ea7e0be-9a1f-5523-b787-ed5f453051d8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id 17592186557964} :answer-field/id #uuid "97da889e-de7b-5fe7-9f12-0a62150c1338" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186558433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e26325a0-a17c-5d3b-96e5-ffcb13cdecc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-107491" :question/problem "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-3 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-3$ and ${y}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=y=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=1$ and ${x}_{1}=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{2}=(-1)\\cdot 2=-2.$"} {:db/id 17592186558577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e33c97-0499-5829-9886-be30b00989ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eea679e-1d00-5848-aa6b-11d4e2a5e9c7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :db/id 17592186558017} {:answer/id #uuid "86e0a1d5-1b21-502e-9724-c39fe8bedec2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 12 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 15 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 20 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 17\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :db/id 17592186558131} {:answer/id #uuid "8f98a11a-a9f4-5967-826b-9561bcb1c4a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 50 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 58 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 62 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 65 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 70\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :db/id 17592186558147} {:answer/id #uuid "a34dfa2c-d0f7-5cbc-becb-9f788df746d8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}10 & 1 \\\\ 12 & 2 \\\\ 15 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 4 \\\\ 17 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :db/id 17592186558191} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ffd8b7-b5cd-5adc-9457-bfa1bef7fd01" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :db/id 17592186558203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f98a11a-a9f4-5967-826b-9561bcb1c4a3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 50 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 58 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 62 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 65 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 70\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :db/id 17592186558147} :answer-field/id #uuid "d12fb1e1-1778-564e-b514-5a394648ba52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186558459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe25ae24-c60e-57ec-8c52-078b3a7606de" :question/math-academy-id "q-139864" :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$50$** | **$58$** | **$62$** | **$65$** | **$70$** |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $12$ | $15$ | $20$ | $17$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$50$** | **$58$** | **$62$** | **$65$** | **$70$** |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $12$ | $15$ | $20$ | $17$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 50 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 58 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 62 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 65 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 70\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."} {:db/id 17592186561038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179730 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a5c75b-e93d-5f09-a126-3d0cb5ede2d5" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Factorial in an Equivalent Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e8a9640-d5a2-5af9-85c3-9df541069e3f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot (k-3)!" :db/id 17592186560634} {:answer/id #uuid "8408d7f7-986c-53a7-8060-d0e712a043fb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot (k-3)" :db/id 17592186560641} {:answer/id #uuid "ac845e78-dfdf-51a2-9552-e2548a59f300" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(k-1)\\cdot (k-3)!" :db/id 17592186560710} {:answer/id #uuid "ea8b46c1-8a21-5eb2-9692-195052a1df74" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 4" :db/id 17592186560820} {:answer/id #uuid "f0444fa0-516b-5c9b-82d3-af49b3feedc9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)" :db/id 17592186560834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e8a9640-d5a2-5af9-85c3-9df541069e3f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot (k-3)!" :db/id 17592186560634} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc7e4408-fbc9-5f18-bf91-1f672892b184" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186560969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8478761-1f4e-5c9d-a634-3425df7889e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-124490" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(k-1)!$ for any $k\\ge 4?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of factorial, we have \n\n$$\n(k-1)!=(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot (k-3)\\cdot (k-4)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first two factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k-1)! & =(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot \\overset{(k-3)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(k-3)\\cdot (k-4)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(k-1)\\cdot (k-2)\\cdot (k-3)!\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186562518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12fab1be-92c3-5c73-9057-2ce01cfa87cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186561641} {:answer/id #uuid "1b236a4d-fc2f-53b6-af86-4c4f9793792b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186561656} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca133f2-63fd-5e30-bf07-bb0ae8d8988a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186561777} {:answer/id #uuid "66b397cb-7b81-5983-a6b0-49e027e16f2b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186561836} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5d726b-4c91-5de1-b99c-cbf38e533e45" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186562066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12fab1be-92c3-5c73-9057-2ce01cfa87cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186561641} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fea878e-a991-5b88-82fa-0dff5d838738" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186562229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fed17ade-b98f-5b7e-aa56-3747d2eae8ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-99343" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-4+3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3-2\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}-4+3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186563647 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c68664c1-bc59-51c1-932c-d6f7c8476470" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Vector Function Using Properties of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04142752-c01a-5e11-89a6-6b5adc4ed5ae" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,-2,4⟩" :db/id 17592186562945} {:answer/id #uuid "21d9e57e-bfa3-586e-9b91-d4dc21a2545d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,-1,3⟩" :db/id 17592186563020} {:answer/id #uuid "24d7bfc2-51e1-5033-adbd-48343a267821" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,0,7⟩" :db/id 17592186563028} {:answer/id #uuid "2918dce8-4f46-5431-aa05-6401af531bcf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,3⟩" :db/id 17592186563037} {:answer/id #uuid "caef0b1a-9245-5da8-b22a-8dda3c23c0a7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,1,6⟩" :db/id 17592186563394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24d7bfc2-51e1-5033-adbd-48343a267821" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨4,0,7⟩" :db/id 17592186563028} :answer-field/id #uuid "88941887-29dc-54a4-8201-4203e16bb84e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186563588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05950ac2-f758-5682-b193-f12241b0a085" :question/math-academy-id "q-127820" :question/problem "Find $f(5)$ given that  $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which   $g(5)=⟨-1,1,-2⟩$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)]=⟨1,3,1⟩\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 5}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 5}[3g(t)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 5}f(t)+3{lim}_{t\\to 5}g(t) \\\\  & =f(5)+3g(5) \\\\  & =f(5)+3{{⟨-1,1,-2⟩}_{⏟}}_{g(5)} \\\\  & =f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)]=⟨1,3,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(5):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩ & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨1,3,1⟩-⟨-3,3,-6⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨4,0,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186563690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c68664c1-bc59-51c1-932c-d6f7c8476470" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Vector Function Using Properties of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44b00ccb-8b01-596c-b4dc-2a3f3aa5a049" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,-3,-1⟩" :db/id 17592186563099} {:answer/id #uuid "45c07133-7c78-580f-9ccc-4af3dc31a4d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The value of $f(0)$ cannot be determined." :db/id 17592186563102} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea82692-100a-5038-b8d4-644562b66a89" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-3,1⟩" :db/id 17592186563267} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1f037b-158b-5c79-8e25-3276bfa998c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,3,1⟩" :db/id 17592186563399} {:answer/id #uuid "f107cc53-ee96-570c-9ee3-a5f4ee8def54" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,-3,1⟩" :db/id 17592186563483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f107cc53-ee96-570c-9ee3-a5f4ee8def54" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,-3,1⟩" :db/id 17592186563483} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fe35960-523d-5648-9c8e-2980e2ae45c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186563604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56eb1f68-e0b0-5d8c-82fe-65522b38bb34" :question/math-academy-id "q-35115" :question/problem "Find $f(0)$ given that  $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which   $g(0)=⟨-1,0,1⟩$ and \n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)]=⟨-2,-3,0⟩\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 0}[-g(t)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)-{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =f(0)-g(0) \\\\  & =f(0)-{{⟨-1,0,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{g(0)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)]=⟨-2,-3,0⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)-g(t)] & =⟨-2,-3,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩ & =f(0)-⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,-3,0⟩+⟨-1,0,1⟩ & =f(0) \\\\ f(0) & =⟨-3,-3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186564473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186073714 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58e2e40e-209b-5bc7-98df-11841c2bbcc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midpoint of a Segment With Variable Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13df7405-37a9-5376-a794-b50ade14b257" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2p,-5q)" :db/id 17592186563997} {:answer/id #uuid "2248598d-8d45-5b33-a1bb-161e7e022f16" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2p,-4q)" :db/id 17592186564017} {:answer/id #uuid "41743567-93b3-52b5-956f-5e3cf584ae66" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6p,-5q)" :db/id 17592186564079} {:answer/id #uuid "b2dfc1f4-7244-51d1-b08e-beaf085cffe1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3p,-5q)" :db/id 17592186564249} {:answer/id #uuid "cd30a56d-8702-5fad-b81c-b55db3829574" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4p,-2q)" :db/id 17592186564290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2dfc1f4-7244-51d1-b08e-beaf085cffe1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3p,-5q)" :db/id 17592186564249} :answer-field/id #uuid "47c69590-b35f-5b4e-b1cf-478ea7e99763" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186564392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a0af22d-5fcd-5a62-b138-4a5969bb9fc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73730" :question/problem "Find the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(8p,-q)$ and $(-2p,-9q).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(8p,-q)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(-2p,-9q)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{8p+(-2p)}{2},\\frac{-q+(-9q)}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{8p-2p}{2},\\frac{-q-9q}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{6p}{2},\\frac{-10q}{2}) \\\\  & =(3p,-5q).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186564531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d177b305-cdd1-5f91-b9d5-90623e52ad10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e9e21c3-28d7-5744-b751-8735eef80c3e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186564048} {:answer/id #uuid "45ccbd17-a611-5e51-8f96-c134314902f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186564085} {:answer/id #uuid "6c912a39-aa9c-5584-bd27-ebb5f9b76c23" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186564139} {:answer/id #uuid "8c06bd3e-d522-582e-8e6e-842930f9ae84" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186564186} {:answer/id #uuid "ae08b2e2-ed79-5464-95a9-3d40e4f6d24d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186564235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c06bd3e-d522-582e-8e6e-842930f9ae84" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id 17592186564186} :answer-field/id #uuid "99733e29-1d7e-5770-81f4-dc925a89eb32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186564425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0061631-6269-58a0-a054-4e33a330ba6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-139798" :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,R=\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ {x}_{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=9.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8(9) & =-9 \\\\ 9{x}_{1} & =-81 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].$"} {:db/id 17592186564537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186073714 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58e2e40e-209b-5bc7-98df-11841c2bbcc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midpoint of a Segment With Variable Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00b86450-d396-50c2-b792-96eb2e069dee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-w,-z)" :db/id 17592186563969} {:answer/id #uuid "1707b2e5-7f13-581b-928d-cb954858885d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2w,2z)" :db/id 17592186563999} {:answer/id #uuid "2295ae60-1e26-5128-8f27-466b06ffd6c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2w,3z)" :db/id 17592186564018} {:answer/id #uuid "2c117928-80a8-595c-9b86-d8c72d7f0afb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2w,-2z)" :db/id 17592186564039} {:answer/id #uuid "c0661565-ff3d-5e5e-b035-22e247858f0b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-w,2z)" :db/id 17592186564264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1707b2e5-7f13-581b-928d-cb954858885d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2w,2z)" :db/id 17592186563999} :answer-field/id #uuid "87c637ca-477b-51e8-ad73-42e7989eff3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186564417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f548e29c-4fb0-5de0-8aa0-2659faa5a2eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-73720" :question/problem "Find the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(-w,7z)$ and $(5w,-3z).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between ${{(-w,7z)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(5w,-3z)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-w+5w}{2},\\frac{7z+(-3z)}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-w+5w}{2},\\frac{7z-3z}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4w}{2},\\frac{4z}{2}) \\\\  & =(2w,2z).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186565446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e2ea816-3a62-5167-9f08-17ebcf97c22e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Nested Compound Statements in Symbolic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12e69b24-bdab-519d-9db4-fa9f763bec4a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∧(Q∨R)" :db/id 17592186564969} {:answer/id #uuid "40a41c4a-74f2-5a0d-80b4-6255b626fcc4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(P∧Q)∨R" :db/id 17592186565033} {:answer/id #uuid "577b7d65-cc3e-5730-82a1-86c384479356" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∨(Q∧R)" :db/id 17592186565072} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ad3c78-1256-5dff-9d91-4d2324618c7d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(P∨Q)∧R" :db/id 17592186565203} {:answer/id #uuid "f27b8716-005f-5b70-a4d1-fcf5671b7cd9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∧(Q∧R)" :db/id 17592186565305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "577b7d65-cc3e-5730-82a1-86c384479356" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∨(Q∧R)" :db/id 17592186565072} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2accb16-6316-5d25-b406-7af92337b373" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186565387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9036632e-a4e5-591e-b6d4-bd5830e984df" :question/math-academy-id "q-177452" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{5}$ is rational, **or** $3|12$ **and** $3|18.$\n\nExpress the compound statement above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $P:\\sqrt{5}\\text{ is rational}$\n\n- $Q:3|12$\n\n- $R:3|18\\text{ }$" :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧$\", while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨$\".\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $\\sqrt{5}$ is rational. This corresponds to $P.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $3|12$ and $3|18.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{3|12}_{⏟}}_{Q}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{3|18}_{⏟}}_{R},\n$$\n\nthat is, $Q∧R.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{\\sqrt{5}\\text{ is rational}}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{3|12\\text{ and }3|18}_{⏟}}_{Q\\,∧\\,R},\n$$\n\nthat is, $P∨(Q∧R).$"} {:db/id 17592186565456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186054644 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c15e521-f848-5d9c-ba06-00550277b8cb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Truth Values of Complex Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4290994a-4111-5f59-8d28-488485afe845" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186565035} {:answer/id #uuid "56b8008e-3e0e-50d5-b98d-6f1ae9548a59" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186565069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4290994a-4111-5f59-8d28-488485afe845" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186565035} :answer-field/id #uuid "1de9cdf1-94b6-56c2-bf7d-3647210da7b1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186565335} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c62bc40-0349-5024-a000-9e42299ef3ba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186564958} {:answer/id #uuid "addf42f9-b92a-50aa-840e-9cebd676a066" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186565197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c62bc40-0349-5024-a000-9e42299ef3ba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186564958} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d6f16fd-3b2c-5aaa-8d4b-bff5822eb1f4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186565366} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cde20091-5ea0-5e89-8e56-3c6da49f96e9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 17592186565246} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d52984-4570-5e61-afbd-0073a1b7199b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186565311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6d52984-4570-5e61-afbd-0073a1b7199b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 17592186565311} :answer-field/id #uuid "c48b5f22-688c-554b-b977-496452316b1e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id 17592186565382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b08d6228-4d71-56ec-903b-067eaa383489" :question/math-academy-id "q-178163" :question/problem "$$\n{{0\\text{ is even}}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{\\{1,2\\}\\cap \\{1\\}=\\{1,2\\}}}\\,\\overset{∨}{\\overset{⏞}{\\text{or}}}\\,\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{2}{3}\\text{ is irrational}}}}_{⏟}}_{Q\\,∨\\,R}\n$$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$\\,$ $P$ is {{field-1}},\n$\\,$ $Q∨R$ is {{field-2}},\n$\\,$ $P∨(Q∨R)$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is true, $Q$ is false, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $Q∨R$ is false, but the result is true:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∨(Q∨R) & ≡\\text{true}∨\\overset{\\text{false}}{\\overset{⏞}{(\\text{false}∨\\text{false})}} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}∨\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186569965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3286434a-593f-5cc4-acfc-6bb0f2812c00" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186569516} {:answer/id #uuid "4c6682e2-223f-5551-9555-606e0dc281da" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186569547} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb0dc58-f354-538c-8aef-2413ca8d7f9c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186569648} {:answer/id #uuid "97984476-2d2e-53cd-84f2-a709077ec7d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186569683} {:answer/id #uuid "dd2156fb-e1ff-51c3-b81e-f5cafa615c40" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186569795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97984476-2d2e-53cd-84f2-a709077ec7d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186569683} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9382a07-3a03-5be2-b528-235df29742b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186569925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14def4d3-3d45-5610-862f-8d4c651f0955" :question/math-academy-id "q-106760" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -16 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -16 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & \\,{R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-8){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186569998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60d81581-c221-5494-a432-08d979f7a43d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186569593} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca3072c-d6ac-5918-a623-20411f8b7700" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{3} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186569647} {:answer/id #uuid "acfff4be-1f46-5b1f-a60f-b1bb756a3b25" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186569714} {:answer/id #uuid "fb15c2de-88fd-5d61-a019-1eb95f578d9b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186569838} {:answer/id #uuid "fba3dcfb-a5b2-547c-8691-6e722e502046" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186569841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb15c2de-88fd-5d61-a019-1eb95f578d9b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186569838} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e1d1148-bcd2-52a8-8f81-b71b67b2ffca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186569897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66c0358e-dcf2-5965-acb4-eb43f1da9799" :question/math-academy-id "q-74951" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186570009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17429e2c-6a7d-5799-9f99-11411731d372" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186569478} {:answer/id #uuid "3cbd73d2-cd1b-5b1f-b775-9c124c82a91b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186569526} {:answer/id #uuid "4d58d8e8-ebbf-5227-901f-b377591464ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186569550} {:answer/id #uuid "9a8638fd-45d4-525e-afd5-446d9bab6953" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186569689} {:answer/id #uuid "dcdfcc6c-043a-5b9e-8a4c-0bfab8faa665" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186569793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d58d8e8-ebbf-5227-901f-b377591464ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186569550} :answer-field/id #uuid "808d2fc9-2e7f-5ef1-86ff-cfcf9e8e2abc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186569892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "903883c2-c07e-50f2-a202-1c38e08cadfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-32550" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186572113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186068721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdded031-fd95-5a7f-92d4-c0d6713ba64d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfccf1a2-81a4-586f-b6af-2f70838ec300" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186571939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfccf1a2-81a4-586f-b6af-2f70838ec300" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186571939} :answer-field/id #uuid "13d56c94-2d25-54b7-b65c-c2ab11270b92" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186571990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18f21b66-7036-5909-982a-f6e8c6a583c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-216300" :question/problem "The distance between the points $(5,1)$ and $(1,-2)$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have ${{(5,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(1,-2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$ \nSo, the distance $d$ can be calculated using the distance formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-5)^{2}+(-2-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186572125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186068720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5df52cb5-75b1-58d9-8535-07c1bb87be34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Length of a Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "379919fe-14c5-5033-9b3a-c01fc06e063d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id 17592186571636} {:answer/id #uuid "52c6b9cc-ce8b-5b07-bd83-778a557af4fe" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id 17592186571689} {:answer/id #uuid "8c3f86e3-aa37-529d-833c-43163e45c374" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{15}" :db/id 17592186571779} {:answer/id #uuid "92676a16-1339-55d9-9df9-b454ed1dd9f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186571797} {:answer/id #uuid "dcfb51fc-582b-51c5-801a-8eebb92fb524" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id 17592186571935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92676a16-1339-55d9-9df9-b454ed1dd9f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id 17592186571797} :answer-field/id #uuid "99558049-622d-5685-b782-575ef24f9379" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186572050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "410f5e90-695f-5bc0-8245-a684010c1ba9" :question/math-academy-id "q-119112" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d939db899518bec1c91fd70e4d16726ec7a12d32f2d7b41575759843e5837d6c.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $\\overset{―}{AB}?$" :question/worked-solution "From the figure, we note that the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates ${{(-1,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(3,-2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})},$ respectively.\n\nTo find the length of $\\overset{―}{AB},$ we use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-(-1))^{2}+(-2-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186572130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186068720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5df52cb5-75b1-58d9-8535-07c1bb87be34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Length of a Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78183e78-e617-5692-8a7d-a04e6565cbe6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id 17592186571744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78183e78-e617-5692-8a7d-a04e6565cbe6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id 17592186571744} :answer-field/id #uuid "b282633f-0ee1-506a-a430-4ffd87e35c4d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186572066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c32c57d-e2a6-5951-aed3-798fe23141b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-216289" :question/problem "![](images/92/923997b18a24320bab5eb7ca39dc04d7da52e10ebf18ff4cb7f8ec40bf00b491.png)\n\nThe length of $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "From the figure, we note that the points $C$ and $D$ have coordinates ${{(7,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(1,9)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})},$ respectively. \n\nTo find the distance $CD,$ we can use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-7)^{2}+(9-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186572149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186129214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27fcac0c-32b4-50b7-a320-2ac89d925ebb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186571606} {:answer/id #uuid "39c17d8b-7383-565d-82ff-9034e842313a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186571643} {:answer/id #uuid "894dd0ee-996e-5946-926e-1667d9661d2f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186571774} {:answer/id #uuid "a5514041-c574-508e-9c48-296d90937fe8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186571825} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a5c562-7c09-52ed-9124-62137601a6cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186571941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27fcac0c-32b4-50b7-a320-2ac89d925ebb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id 17592186571606} :answer-field/id #uuid "621d2560-2a88-546c-aa01-9273b93c0401" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186572029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80ffb1ef-2b1d-5387-91a1-65dcd637622b" :question/math-academy-id "q-99410" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1+4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2-5\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=2+5\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-4\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186572671 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172028 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26871e46-51ca-5680-a064-bcee4ac984fd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52c08f07-4189-509a-b220-5216f042453d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id 17592186572415} {:answer/id #uuid "8635fe6e-fac6-55c1-996c-c85b1599c832" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An circle of radius $1$" :db/id 17592186572461} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d80198-d0f9-5a4a-bda4-ee01da631e00" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $3$" :db/id 17592186572491} {:answer/id #uuid "a5629f9b-9362-5ae4-8c5b-612ef5f66982" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of intersecting lines" :db/id 17592186572493} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3f94a7-6795-5611-8617-8bdcc199a8ca" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id 17592186572546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5629f9b-9362-5ae4-8c5b-612ef5f66982" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of intersecting lines" :db/id 17592186572493} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1a95532-8eb9-58d2-950f-597816548f39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186572644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "13426e81-09a1-5f21-879b-4654e3684393" :question/math-academy-id "q-95398" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=2.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=2\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2})-2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2}) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}-(\\sqrt{2})^{2}) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+2-2) & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}+\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+2 & =2 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}+\\sqrt{2})}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}+\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}_{1}^{2}-{z}_{2}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the pair of intersecting lines ${z}_{2}=\\sqrt{3}{z}_{1}$ and ${z}_{2}=-\\sqrt{3}{z}_{1}.$"} {:db/id 17592186575529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2993b4ab-e45b-579a-8a11-b5e9ee107d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Compound Predicate in Symbolic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11730b49-5c1b-553f-a0cd-5065a0dc5199" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A∨A" :db/id 17592186574977} {:answer/id #uuid "3f3c59c9-503f-5ae0-b3a5-872155cbacb4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A∧(B∨A)" :db/id 17592186575056} {:answer/id #uuid "3fc95fc3-0584-51d0-a312-1cdf402e6d98" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A∧B" :db/id 17592186575059} {:answer/id #uuid "63d24958-d529-55a1-bb97-dbc8287d4ab4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A∨B" :db/id 17592186575130} {:answer/id #uuid "a1142e71-f01d-53db-85c7-f72b0e85cdc6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(A∧B)∨B" :db/id 17592186575231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63d24958-d529-55a1-bb97-dbc8287d4ab4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A∨B" :db/id 17592186575130} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a0545d6-6269-5947-88bb-012e08cb4777" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186575412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c5b86f8-d52f-5a20-8d0d-0aaa46ba2066" :question/math-academy-id "q-170732" :question/problem "$x$ is a multiple of $3$ **or** negative.\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $A(x):$ $x$ is a multiple of $3$\n\n- $B(x):$ $x$ is negative" :question/worked-solution "The word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nSo, the sentence reads\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is a multiple of 3}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{x\\text{ is negative}}_{⏟}}_{B},\n$$\n\nthat is, $A∨B.$"} {:db/id 17592186575540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce659c55-ca26-52c6-b15e-ec317013d856" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Nested Compound Predicate in Symbolic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23c5f076-2dad-57e5-86da-dcf767728159" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(P∧N)∨(U∧W)" :db/id 17592186575007} {:answer/id #uuid "2aa24a3b-0cf6-564d-bbd2-5ed0ad7d9580" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(U∨W)∧(N∨P)" :db/id 17592186575017} {:answer/id #uuid "544b3410-55a9-57b5-ab4a-a712e386d5b7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(N∧W)∨(P∧U)" :db/id 17592186575096} {:answer/id #uuid "83370d52-ce6f-51ff-b890-36f3abdaa7d8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(N∨W)∨(P∨U)" :db/id 17592186575184} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d89619-99fb-5b26-aa6a-eddccb7eb088" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(N∨W)∧(P∨U)" :db/id 17592186575279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "544b3410-55a9-57b5-ab4a-a712e386d5b7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(N∧W)∨(P∧U)" :db/id 17592186575096} :answer-field/id #uuid "2649b6c2-492e-5356-a831-8263ce9a3a65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186575430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b324032-316f-5d2c-a64e-c71b992cc557" :question/math-academy-id "q-170744" :question/problem "$x$ is negative **and** irrational, **or** $x$ is positive **and** less than $5.$\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $N(x):$ $x$ is negative\n\n- $W(x):$ $x$ is irrational\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is positive\n\n- $U(x):$ $x$ is less than $5$" :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧,$\" while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts: \n\n- **Part 1:** $x$ is negative and irrational. This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{x\\,\\text{is negative}}_{⏟}}_{N}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{x\\,\\text{is irrational}}_{⏟}}_{W},\n$$\n\nthat is, $N∧W.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $x$ is positive and less than $5.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is positive }}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{x\\text{ is less than }5}_{⏟}}_{U},\n$$\n\nthat is, $P∧U.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{x\\,\\text{is negative and irrational}}_{⏟}}_{N∧W}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{x\\text{ is positive and less than }5}_{⏟}}_{P∧U},\n$$\n\nthat is, $(N∧W)∨(P∧U).$"} {:db/id 17592186575602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce659c55-ca26-52c6-b15e-ec317013d856" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Nested Compound Predicate in Symbolic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fc6846f-da86-5edc-8197-5dd6ab0d515b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N∨I∨G" :db/id 17592186575031} {:answer/id #uuid "3a990b3b-e50c-55f4-841c-48540a89bea3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(N∨I)∧(N∨G)" :db/id 17592186575048} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4cdf67-55da-5845-afc2-158e6ab7917e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(N∨I)∧G" :db/id 17592186575111} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ae40aa-e0c5-5ecd-993e-b28f6b3e0dac" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N∧(I∨G)" :db/id 17592186575360} {:answer/id #uuid "fd90d54e-01f7-575a-a1ce-e4adcea956f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N∧I∧G" :db/id 17592186575404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fc6846f-da86-5edc-8197-5dd6ab0d515b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N∨I∨G" :db/id 17592186575031} :answer-field/id #uuid "86923f36-2336-5931-98c5-4bc40ad3f1a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186575468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf7bd3ae-1af0-5d1c-98ba-75865a7d1ce0" :question/math-academy-id "q-175379" :question/problem "$x$ is negative **or** an integer, **or** $x$ is greater than $4.$\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $N(x):$ $x$ is negative\n\n- $I(x):$ $x$ is an integer\n\n- $G(x):$ $x$ is greater than $4$" :question/worked-solution "The word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts: \n\n- **Part 1:** $x$ is negative or an integer. This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{x\\,\\text{is negative}}_{⏟}}_{N}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{x\\,\\text{is an integer }}_{⏟}}_{I},\n$$\n\nthat is, $N∨I.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $x$ is greater than $4.$ This corresponds to $G.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{x\\,\\text{is negative or an integer }}_{⏟}}_{N∨I}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{x\\text{ is greater than }4}_{⏟}}_{G},\n$$\n\nthat is, $N∨I∨G.$"} {:db/id 17592186575608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce659c55-ca26-52c6-b15e-ec317013d856" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Nested Compound Predicate in Symbolic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b2831f9-56d4-5d26-b2d1-8c3127c9f5fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∧(L∨O)" :db/id 17592186575018} {:answer/id #uuid "452d777d-7cc1-590f-b684-a0da89c9b7d2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(P∧L)∨O" :db/id 17592186575071} {:answer/id #uuid "7f61874f-0b6a-5d57-af59-98cf5c43faea" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∨L∨O" :db/id 17592186575179} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ee7079-fd3c-5328-92cb-17dcbcb9c236" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∧L∧O" :db/id 17592186575238} {:answer/id #uuid "d5975a4d-0efb-50bd-ae9f-843c27623fba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∨(L∧O)" :db/id 17592186575328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3ee7079-fd3c-5328-92cb-17dcbcb9c236" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∧L∧O" :db/id 17592186575238} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6828370-b586-53a4-8e88-116bcad7ec85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186575486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da39f028-b35d-56a8-9983-4818944cc5ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-175382" :question/problem "$x$ is a prime **and** less than $15$, **and** $x$ is odd.\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is prime\n\n- $L(x):$ $x$ is less than $15$\n\n- $O(x):$ $x$ is odd" :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts: \n\n- **Part 1:**  $x$ is a prime and less than $15.$ This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{x\\,\\text{is prime}}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{x\\,\\text{is less than }15}_{⏟}}_{L},\n$$\n\nthat is, $P∧L.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $x$ is odd. This corresponds to $O.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is a prime and less than }15}_{⏟}}_{P∧L}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{x\\,\\text{is odd }}_{⏟}}_{O},\n$$\n\nthat is, $P∧L∧O.$"} {:db/id 17592186575614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2993b4ab-e45b-579a-8a11-b5e9ee107d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Compound Predicate in Symbolic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c6b864-ec7f-507b-a1d6-37aaf7109761" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q∨(P∧Q)" :db/id 17592186574989} {:answer/id #uuid "3f48ff9c-4f28-58c3-8057-031c97ce17cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∨(P∧Q)" :db/id 17592186575057} {:answer/id #uuid "70090cfa-39e0-535c-97ba-4e7cc9a1fe00" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∧Q" :db/id 17592186575153} {:answer/id #uuid "a66a4923-5edc-5e09-90ef-eec31f5cf445" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∨Q" :db/id 17592186575240} {:answer/id #uuid "ae05cfac-91fd-526f-a243-a17648420ae6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∧(P∨Q)" :db/id 17592186575258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70090cfa-39e0-535c-97ba-4e7cc9a1fe00" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P∧Q" :db/id 17592186575153} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3382ac5-0ab3-59c9-ab02-9b04f3ae190e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186575514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e879e661-bcef-55f1-b1d6-53b6386bc86f" :question/math-academy-id "q-175347" :question/problem "$x$ is odd **and** a multiple of $3.$\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is odd\n\n- $Q(x):$ $x$ is a multiple of $3$" :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧.$\"\n\nSo, the sentence reads\n\n$$\n{{x\\text{ is odd}}_{⏟}}_{P}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{x\\text{ is a multiple of }3}_{⏟}}_{Q},\n$$\n\nthat is, $P∧Q.$"} {:db/id 17592186575626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce659c55-ca26-52c6-b15e-ec317013d856" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Nested Compound Predicate in Symbolic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c69ab7e-fdc4-577a-afc4-5efc1c8da77a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F∨(G∧H)" :db/id 17592186575113} {:answer/id #uuid "76dd5f9c-6749-54d9-8fc6-d3fec5efe88a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F∧(G∨H)" :db/id 17592186575168} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ea5ef2-3ada-54f7-969b-3198aafa6288" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(F∧G)∧H" :db/id 17592186575245} {:answer/id #uuid "addc3908-57ac-54ed-883f-425294653323" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(F∨G)∧H" :db/id 17592186575257} {:answer/id #uuid "f75427e7-171a-5d90-be71-724fefa93612" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(F∧G)∨H" :db/id 17592186575388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f75427e7-171a-5d90-be71-724fefa93612" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(F∧G)∨H" :db/id 17592186575388} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3de54ca-72e7-5a7f-8abc-16424ae427df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186575481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff367501-0855-59a2-85d9-edfbeeaeb047" :question/math-academy-id "q-170746" :question/problem "$x$ is greater than $10$ **and** prime, **or** $x$ is negative.\n\nExpress the compound predicate above in symbolic form given the following notations:\n\n- $F(x):$ $x$ is greater than $10$\n\n- $G(x):$ $x$ is prime\n\n- $H(x):$ $x$ is negative" :question/worked-solution "The word \"and\" is represented by the symbol \"$∧,$\" while the word \"or\" is represented by the symbol \"$∨.$\"\n\nNote that this sentence consists of two parts:\n\n- **Part 1:** $x$ is greater than $10$ and prime. This corresponds to\n\n$$\n{{x\\,\\text{is greater than}\\,10}_{⏟}}_{F}\\,{{\\text{and}}_{⏟}}_{∧}\\,{{x\\text{ is prime}}_{⏟}}_{G},\n$$\n\nthat is, $F∧G.$\n\n- **Part 2:** $x$ is negative. This corresponds to $H.$\n\nSo, we can express the sentence as\n\n$$\n{{x\\,\\text{ is greater than}\\,10\\,\\text{and prime}}_{⏟}}_{F∧G}\\,{{\\text{or}}_{⏟}}_{∨}\\,{{x\\text{ is negative}}_{⏟}}_{H},\n$$\n\nthat is, $(F∧G)∨H.$"}]
